","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 1**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n````\nIn `@dylint_lints/README.md`:\n- Around line 258-274: Update the fenced code block in README.md (the block\nshowing make/cargo commands) to include a language hint by changing the opening\nfence from ``` to ```bash so markdownlint MD040 is satisfied; locate the block\nthat currently starts with ``` and replace it with ```bash while keeping the\nblock contents unchanged.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/README.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`335-335`: **Remove hardcoded line number reference.**\n\nHardcoded line numbers are fragile and require manual updates when the referenced file changes. Consider removing the line number or replacing it with a section reference.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-- [Makefile](../Makefile) - Tool comparison table (line 60)\n+- [Makefile](../Makefile) - Tool comparison table\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>apps/gts-docs-validator/src/scanner.rs (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`30-32`: **Consider caching the compiled regex.**\n\n`gts_pattern()` compiles a new `Regex` on every call. For a CLI tool this is acceptable, but if scan performance becomes a concern, consider using `std::sync::LazyLock` (stable in Rust 1.80+) or the `once_cell` crate to cache the compiled pattern.\n\n---\n\n`148-158`: **Inefficient regex compilation in loop.**\n\nEach call to `is_false_positive` compiles the `FALSE_POSITIVE_PATTERNS` regexes inside the loop. Consider pre-compiling these patterns at module load time using `LazyLock` for better performance.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested improvement</summary>\n\n```rust\nuse std::sync::LazyLock;\n\nstatic FALSE_POSITIVE_REGEXES: LazyLock<Vec<Regex>> = LazyLock::new(|| {\n    FALSE_POSITIVE_PATTERNS\n        .iter()\n        .filter_map(|p| Regex::new(p).ok())\n        .collect()\n});\n\nfn is_false_positive(gts_id: &str) -> bool {\n    FALSE_POSITIVE_REGEXES.iter().any(|re| re.is_match(gts_id))\n}\n```\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`197-201`: **Minor inefficiency: allocating `prev_lines` on each iteration.**\n\nLine 198 creates a new `Vec<&str>` on every iteration by cloning all previous line references. Since `is_bad_example_context` only examines the last 3 lines, consider passing a slice directly.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested improvement</summary>\n\n```diff\n-            let prev_lines: Vec<&str> = lines[..line_idx].to_vec();\n-            if is_bad_example_context(line, &prev_lines) {\n+            if is_bad_example_context(line, &lines[..line_idx]) {\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>apps/gts-docs-validator/src/main.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`143-163`: **Consider including context in JSON output.**\n\nThe JSON output manually constructs error objects without the `context` field. Since `GtsError` derives `Serialize` with conditional context inclusion (`#[serde(skip_serializing_if = \"String::is_empty\")]`), you could simplify by serializing the errors directly to include context when available.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested simplification</summary>\n\n```diff\n     let output = serde_json::json!({\n         \"files_scanned\": result.files_scanned,\n         \"errors_count\": result.errors.len(),\n         \"ok\": result.is_ok(),\n-        \"errors\": result.errors.iter().map(|e| {\n-            serde_json::json!({\n-                \"file\": e.file.display().to_string(),\n-                \"line\": e.line,\n-                \"column\": e.column,\n-                \"gts_id\": e.gts_id,\n-                \"error\": e.error,\n-            })\n-        }).collect::<Vec<_>>(),\n+        \"errors\": &result.errors,\n     });\n```\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`181-182`: **Consider importing `HashMap` at the top of the file.**\n\nUsing `std::collections::HashMap` inline works, but importing it with other standard library types would be more consistent with Rust conventions.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/266#pullrequestreview-3681757190","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/266","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/266#pullrequestreview-3681757190"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/266"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-20T11:39:01Z","commit_id":"d6d49bde781c4df3ae6a5121760a80a143b7b09e"}]},{"source_pr_number":265,"source_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/265","source_state":"closed","source_author":"ffedoroff","target_issue_number":null,"title":"[PR #265] Add Resource Group specs draft","status":"exported","mirrored_at":null,"comments_status":"none","comments_mirrored":0,"pr_head_ref":"feature/resource-group-spec","pr_head_sha":"44ee240b5140f4d19e4d60d9af9adefcfd284404","pr_base_ref":"main","pr_head_owner":"ffedoroff","pr_from_fork":1,"source_data":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/265","id":3181939946,"node_id":"PR_kwDOPaLyBs69qIzq","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/265","diff_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/265.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/265.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/issues/265","number":265,"state":"closed","locked":false,"title":"Add Resource Group specs draft","user":{"login":"ffedoroff","id":1179771,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjExNzk3NzE=","a