or\nproper highlighting; update the block delimiter from ``` to a tagged fence like\n```text (or ```http) so the template `/ {module}/v{version}/{resource}` is\nfenced as ```text /{module}/v{version}/{resource} ```—modify the code block\naround the Endpoint Pattern heading accordingly.\n\nIn `@FDD-Adapter/specs/architectural-lints.md`:\n- Around line 187-196: The \"Don't use #[allow(...)] unless absolutely necessary\"\nguideline is ambiguous—add a concise \"When #[allow(...)] is Acceptable\"\nsubsection that enumerates approved scenarios (generated code, external trait\nimplementations, documented false positives, temporary workarounds with tracked\nissues), require an approval/process (a brief in-source justification comment\nand a link to an issue/approval ticket), and describe false-positive handling\n(file issue in dylint_lints/ with repro and link it in the comment); also\ninclude the exact comment format to require (e.g., a LINT_ALLOW tag, brief\njustification, and Issue: `#nnnn`) so reviewers can validate suppressions\nconsistently.\n- Around line 197-207: Update the \"CI Integration\" section to specify the CI\nsystem and pipeline placement: state that the dylint step is configured for the\nproject's GitHub Actions workflow (or alternative CI you use), reference the\nexisting command \"python scripts/ci.py dylint\" (and \"make dylint\") as the step\nto execute, and indicate it should run in the lint/static-analysis job before\nbuild/test stages (e.g., on push and PRs). Also describe the failure mechanism\nand reporting: explain that the script exits with status code 1 on any dylint\nviolations, causing the CI job to fail, and that violations are printed to\nstdout/stderr by the script (or written to a log artifact) so reviewers can\ninspect the reported issues.\n\nIn `@FDD-Adapter/specs/domain-model.md`:\n- Line 70: The markdown currently shows a bare URL after the Tool description\n(\"gts CLI\") which should be formatted as a proper markdown link; update the line\ncontaining \"**Tool**: `gts` CLI from\nhttps://github.com/GlobalTypeSystem/gts-rust\" to use markdown link syntax (e.g.,\nlink the repository URL with descriptive text such as the project name or \"gts\nCLI\") so the `gts` CLI reference and the URL are combined into a single markdown\nlink.\n- Around line 70-140: The doc references a non-existent \"gts\" CLI (commands like\n`gts generate-from-rust`, `gts validate-id`, and the macro\n`#[struct_to_gts_schema]` with example `ModuleInfo`) which must be clarified or\nremoved: either replace the bogus `gts` references with the actual tool/crate\nname and repo URL and update example commands/annotation to match that project's\nCLI and macro, or mark this GTS section as TODO/removed and change the CI\nrequirement (\"GTS validation MUST pass in CI pipeline\") to a conditional (e.g.,\n\"If using GTS...\") or to a real validation step (e.g., link to the actual schema\ngenerator/validator used), and ensure the example annotation\n(`#[struct_to_gts_schema]`, `ModuleInfo`) is aligned with the real codegen\nattribute if one exists.\n\nIn `@FDD-Adapter/specs/security.md`:\n- Around line 265-270: Update the \"Rate Limiting\" section to use\nproduction-appropriate thresholds (recommend standard: 1,000–10,000\nrequests/hour for authenticated users instead of 100/hour), explicitly define\nwhether limits are per-user or per-IP and how to treat unauthenticated requests,\nspecify concrete lower thresholds for \"sensitive ops\" (e.g., 100–1,000/hour) and\nwhether those apply per-endpoint or globally, and add guidance on burst handling\n(e.g., token-bucket/leaky-bucket with short-term burst allowance and smoothing)\nwhile retaining the requirement to return 429 with a Retry-After header; ensure\nyou update the bullets under \"Rate Limiting\" (Standard, Sensitive ops, Return\n429 with `Retry-After`) to reflect these values and strategies.\n- Around line 272-290: The audit logging example using tracing::warn! must\ninclude the trace_id field to match the security checklist; update the\ntracing::warn! call that currently sets user_id, tenant_id, action, and\ntarget_id (and the message \"User deleted another user\") to also include trace_id\n= %trace_id so security events can be correlated across services; ensure the\nvariable name trace_id matches the surrounding context where traces are\ncreated/passed.\n- Around line 100-124: The example in list_users_handler creates a SecurityCtx\nfrom auth.tenant_id without checking that the authenticated user is authorized\nfor that tenant; before calling SecurityCtx::for_tenants, validate that\nAuthContext (e.g. an authorized_tenants list on auth) contains auth.tenant_id\nand return an appropriate error (Problem/403) if not authorized, or document\nthat token validation guarantees tenant scoping if you intend to skip the\nruntime check; update the handler to perform this explicit membership check\nusing AuthContext before creating the SecurityCtx.\n\nIn `@FDD-Adapter/specs/testing.md`:\n- Around line 114-118: Update the docs section that lists test data to reference\nthe actual directory name: replace the incorrect path \"testing/e2e/fixtures/\"\nwith \"testing/e2e/testdata/\" in FDD-Adapter/specs/testing.md (the Test\nConfiguration block that references the pytest config file\ntesting/e2e/conftest.py).\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (13)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/conventions.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`44-53`: **Add language specifier to fenced code block.**\n\nThe module structure diagram should specify a language (e.g., `text` or `plaintext`) to satisfy linting rules.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n modules/{module}/\n ├── src/\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/testing.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`81-85`: **Add language specifier to dependencies code block.**\n\nThe dependencies list should specify a language (e.g., `text` or `requirements`) to satisfy linting rules.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n pytest\n pytest-asyncio\n httpx\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n---\n\n`161-169`: **Add language specifier to test organization code block.**\n\nThe test organization structure should specify a language (e.g., `text` or `plaintext`) to satisfy linting rules.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n {crate}/\n ├── src/\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/domain-model.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`102-112`: **Clarify GTS annotation integration with existing derives.**\n\nThe example shows `use gts::struct_to_gts_schema;` and `#[struct_to_gts_schema]` annotation applied to a struct that already has multiple derives. It's unclear how this proc macro integrates with the existing derive chain and whether it needs to be positioned before or after other derives. Consider adding a note about annotation ordering or potential conflicts with the other macros (Serialize, Deserialize, ToSchema).\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested clarification</summary>\n\nAdd a note after line 112:\n\n```markdown\n**Note**: The `#[struct_to_gts_schema]` annotation should be placed before derive macros. Ensure the `gts` crate is added to your `Cargo.toml` dependencies.\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/module-creation.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`32-68`: **Consider adding language identifier to directory structure code block.**\n\nThe fenced code block showing the canonical directory structure could benefit from a language identifier for better syntax highlighting and tooling support.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Optional improvement</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n modules/<your-module>/\n ├─ <your-module>-sdk/           # SDK crate (public API)\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/patterns.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`16-27`: **Consider adding language identifiers to structure code blocks.**\n\nTwo fenced code blocks showing structural layouts could benefit from language identifiers for better tooling support and syntax highlighting.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Optional improvements</summary>\n\nFor the module structure block around line 16:\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n modules/{module}/\n```\n\nFor the layered architecture block around line 56:\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n api/rest/           → REST API endpoints (DTOs)\n```\n\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 56-60\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/security.md (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`63-72`: **Add language identifier to fenced code block.**\n\nThe architecture diagram should specify a language identifier for proper rendering. Use `text` for ASCII diagrams or consider `mermaid` for more sophisticated flow diagrams.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n API Handler (per-request)\n     ↓ Creates SecurityCtx from auth/session\n SecureConn (stateless wrapper)\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n---\n\n`292-298`: **Consider stronger password requirements aligned with current best practices.**\n\nWhile the 8-character minimum meets baseline NIST SP 800-63B requirements, modern security practices recommend:\n- Minimum length of 12-15 characters for stronger protection\n- Maximum length of at least 64 characters (per NIST)\n- Clarification on whether complexity requirements are enforced (the example code at lines 23-28 only checks length)\n\nAdditionally, consider documenting policies for:\n- Password reuse prevention (e.g., last N passwords)\n- Account lockout thresholds for failed attempts\n- MFA/2FA requirements for privileged accounts\n\n\n\nNote: NIST no longer recommends forced password rotation, so the absence of password age requirements is appropriate.\n\n---\n\n`235-247`: **Enhance CORS configuration guidance with additional security considerations.**\n\nThe CORS example correctly prohibits wildcards in production, but could be more comprehensive. Consider documenting:\n\n- **Credentials handling**: When `cors_allow_credentials: true` is needed and its security implications\n- **Allowed methods**: Explicitly list allowed HTTP methods (GET, POST, etc.)\n- **Allowed headers**: Specify which custom headers are permitted\n- **Preflight caching**: Configure `Access-Control-Max-Age` to reduce OPTIONS requests\n- **Error responses**: How CORS violations should be logged for security monitoring\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📋 Enhanced CORS configuration example</summary>\n\n```yaml\n# config/server.yaml\nmodules:\n  api_gateway:\n    cors_enabled: true\n    cors_allowed_origins:\n      - \"https://app.example.com\"\n      - \"https://admin.example.com\"\n    cors_allowed_methods:\n      - GET\n      - POST\n      - PUT\n      - DELETE\n    cors_allowed_headers:\n      - Authorization\n      - Content-Type\n      - X-Request-ID\n    cors_allow_credentials: false  # Only enable if necessary\n    cors_max_age: 3600  # Cache preflight for 1 hour\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/architectural-lints.md (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`101-109`: **Clarify the scope and numbering for DE05xx category.**\n\nThe DE05xx category is titled \"Client/Gateway Layer\" but only includes DE0503 (Plugin Client Suffix). This suggests either:\n- Missing DE0501 and DE0502 lints\n- Or this category is incomplete\n\nConsider documenting what DE0501/DE0502 cover, or explain why numbering starts at DE0503.\n\n---\n\n`161-163`: **Cross-reference security lints with security specification.**\n\nThe planned DE07xx Security category mentions \"Enforce Secure ORM usage\" but should explicitly reference the detailed requirements in `FDD-Adapter/specs/security.md`. This ensures consistency between architectural lints and security policies.\n\n\n\nConsider adding specific lint ideas based on security.md requirements:\n- **DE0701**: Detect database operations without SecurityCtx parameter\n- **DE0702**: Enforce #[secure(...)] attribute on entity models\n- **DE0703**: Prevent raw SQL usage without `insecure-escape` feature flag\n- **DE0704**: Validate parameterized queries in raw SQL blocks\n- **DE0705**: Detect hardcoded secrets in source code\n\nThis would provide compile-time enforcement of the security patterns documented in security.md.\n\n---\n\n`177-186`: **Add decision criteria for when to create new lints vs. documentation.**\n\nThe section describes *how* to add new lints but not *when* a rule should become a lint rather than a guideline. This could lead to over-linting or under-linting.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📋 Suggested decision criteria</summary>\n\nAdd before step 1:\n\n```markdown\n### When to Create a Lint vs. Guideline\n\n**Create a lint when**:\n- ✅ Rule is objective and mechanically verifiable\n- ✅ Violation causes architectural breakage or security risk\n- ✅ Rule is agreed upon by architecture team\n- ✅ Violation is a common code review comment\n- ✅ Can be checked without deep semantic analysis\n\n**Keep as guideline when**:\n- ❌ Rule requires human judgment or context\n- ❌ Too complex or slow (>100ms per file)\n- ❌ High false positive rate (>5%)\n- ❌ Still experimental or under discussion\n- ❌ Applies only to specific modules/contexts\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on learnings about lint development practices.\n\n---\n\n`209-226`: **Consider expanding troubleshooting section with debugging guidance.**\n\nThe current troubleshooting covers common setup issues effectively. For a more complete developer experience, consider adding:\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📋 Additional troubleshooting scenarios</summary>\n\n```markdown\n**Testing individual lint**:\n```bash\ncd dylint_lints/de01xx_lint_name\ncargo test\n```\n\n**Viewing detailed lint output**:\n```bash\nDYLINT_RUSTFLAGS=\"--verbose\" make dylint\n```\n\n**Debugging lint logic**:\n```bash\n# Add println! debugging in lint implementation\ncd dylint_lints/de01xx_lint_name\ncargo build --release\n# Run on specific file\ndylint list  # Find lint name\ndylint run --lib de01xx_lint_name -- path/to/test/file.rs\n```\n\n**Handling false positives**:\n1. Verify lint logic in `dylint_lints/de01xx_name/src/lib.rs`\n2. Add test case in `dylint_lints/de01xx_name/ui/`\n3. If legitimate false positive, file issue with reproduction\n4. Temporarily use `#[allow(...)]` with comment (see best practices)\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📜 Review details</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commits</summary>\n\nReviewing files that changed from the base of the PR and between a7f2527cb9910751b6f5a6b4664039ffc8e37513 and f68547173d29283c630d98c8de1999e7221d4728.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📒 Files selected for processing (20)</summary>\n\n* `.gitmodules`\n* `AGENTS.md`\n* `FDD`\n* `FDD-Adapter/AGENTS.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/api-contracts.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/architectural-lints.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/build-deploy.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/conventions.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/domain-model.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/linting.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/module-creation.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/patterns.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/project-structure.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/rest-api-guidelines.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/rust-guidelines.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/security.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/tech-stack.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/testing.md`\n* `guidelines/DNA`\n* `guidelines/GTS`\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Additional context used</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings (29)</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📓 Common 