cement consistency.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/project-structure.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-192`: **LGTM - Clear and comprehensive project structure specification.**\n\nThe document provides well-organized guidance on repository structure with canonical patterns for modules (contract/, api/, domain/, infra/), clear separation of concerns, and systematic organization principles. The validation checklists (lines 103-164) ensure structural compliance, and agent instructions enforce proper layering and organization.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/api-contracts.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`43-66`: **LGTM - Well-structured API route example.**\n\nThe example demonstrates proper utoipa::path annotation with response documentation, error handling using Problem Details (RFC 7807), and clean Axum router setup. The pattern clearly shows how to integrate OpenAPI generation with REST endpoints.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/AGENTS.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`13-39`: **All referenced spec files are present.** Verification confirms that all 14 spec files referenced in AGENTS.md (tech-stack.md, domain-model.md, api-contracts.md, patterns.md, conventions.md, linting.md, build-deploy.md, testing.md, project-structure.md, rest-api-guidelines.md, rust-guidelines.md, module-creation.md, security.md, architectural-lints.md) exist in FDD-Adapter/specs/.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/module-creation.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-375`: **Excellent comprehensive module creation guide!**\n\nThis document provides clear, actionable guidance for creating ModKit modules. The structure is logical, progressing from concepts through implementation details to validation checklists. Key strengths:\n\n- SDK pattern is clearly explained with concrete examples\n- Security context requirements are consistently emphasized\n- Layer separation (API/Domain/Infra) is well-documented\n- Validation checklists provide measurable compliance criteria\n- Agent instructions offer clear ALWAYS/NEVER rules\n\nThe guide effectively bridges architectural principles with practical implementation.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/rust-guidelines.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-373`: **Comprehensive and well-structured Rust development guidelines!**\n\nThis document provides excellent guidance for Rust development in the HyperSpot ecosystem. Notable strengths:\n\n- Clear preference for modern libraries (`time` over `chrono`, RFC 9457 for errors)\n- Practical code examples for all major patterns\n- Consistent emphasis on type safety and structured error handling\n- OpenAPI integration with utoipa is well-documented\n- Import organization follows community best practices\n- Validation checklists provide clear compliance criteria\n\nThe guidelines effectively balance prescriptive standards with practical implementation guidance.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/rest-api-guidelines.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-343`: **Excellent REST API guidelines with industry best practices!**\n\nThis document provides comprehensive, well-structured REST API guidance that aligns with modern API design principles. Key strengths:\n\n- Core principles emphasize consistency, explicitness, and evolvability\n- RFC 9457 Problem Details for standardized error responses\n- Cursor-based pagination for scalability\n- OData subset provides powerful filtering without complexity\n- Clear idempotency guidance with retention tiers\n- Path-based versioning with explicit breaking change criteria\n- Strong security and observability requirements\n- Client compatibility guidelines prevent brittle integrations\n\nThe document effectively balances prescriptive standards (MUST requirements) with flexibility (SHOULD/MAY), providing clear compliance criteria while allowing for context-specific decisions.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/tech-stack.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-105`: **Clear and concise tech stack specification!**\n\nThis document effectively documents the technology choices and tooling for the HyperSpot project. Strengths:\n\n- Clear language and edition specification (Rust 2021)\n- ModKit custom framework clearly identified\n- Multi-database support strategy documented (SQLite dev, PostgreSQL/MariaDB prod)\n- Standard Rust tooling comprehensively listed\n- Testing tools span unit (cargo test) through E2E (pytest)\n- Agent instructions prevent framework fragmentation\n\nThe specification provides clear guidance on the approved technology stack while maintaining flexibility for appropriate variations (e.g., database choice based on environment).\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/patterns.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-168`: **Excellent comprehensive architecture patterns specification!**\n\nThis document provides clear, actionable architectural guidance that complements the other FDD-Adapter specifications. Key strengths:\n\n- Modular architecture as the foundational pattern is clearly articulated\n- Gateway pattern provides flexibility for pluggable implementations\n- Layered architecture (API/Domain/Infra) enforces separation of concerns\n- Configuration pattern balances hierarchy with environment overrides\n- Custom lints (dylint) provide compile-time architectural compliance\n- Validation checklists with clear pass/fail criteria\n- Agent instructions offer concrete ALWAYS/NEVER rules\n\nThe specification effectively establishes architectural constraints while providing the flexibility needed for diverse module implementations.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/security.md (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`376-461`: **Excellent validation framework and agent instructions.**\n\nThe validation checklists and agent instructions provide clear, actionable guidance with appropriate categorization (MUST/SHOULD/MAY). The 14 mandatory requirements cover critical security controls, and the 20-item implementation checklist provides concrete verification steps before deployment.\n\nThe structured approach with compliance criteria and explicit ALWAYS/NEVER rules will help ensure consistent security practices across the codebase.\n\n---\n\n`48-214`: **Comprehensive Secure ORM specification with strong security guarantees.**\n\nThis section provides excellent coverage of the secure-by-default ORM pattern:\n- ✅ Clear architectural principles (request-scoped context, stateless services)\n- ✅ Compile-time enforcement using typestate pattern\n- ✅ Deny-by-default policy with explicit `WHERE 1=0` for empty scopes\n- ✅ Well-documented implicit security policy table\n- ✅ Comprehensive examples for all CRUD operations\n- ✅ Handling of both scoped and unrestricted entities\n\nThe design prevents common multi-tenant security vulnerabilities at compile time rather than runtime, which is a significant security improvement.\n\n\n\nNote: Ensure this aligns with the referenced `docs/SECURE-ORM.md` documentation (see separate verification comment).\n\n---\n\n`3-3`: All referenced documentation files exist and are properly maintained. No action required.\n\n- `guidelines/SECURITY.md` exists (95 lines, updated 2026-01-16)\n- `docs/SECURE-ORM.md` exists (610 lines, updated 2026-01-16)\n- `modkit-auth` is properly configured as a workspace path dependency\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/architectural-lints.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`253-330`: **Well-structured validation framework with clear compliance criteria.**\n\nThe validation checklists effectively organize requirements by priority (MUST/SHOULD/MAY) and provide actionable agent instructions. The dual checklists for lint compliance (pre-commit) and adding new lints (development) ensure both usage and maintenance perspectives are covered.\n\nKey strengths:\n- ✅ 10 mandatory requirements cover essential lint infrastructure\n- ✅ Clear pass/fail criteria (10/10 MUST + lint pass)\n- ✅ Practical agent instructions (8 ALWAYS, 4 NEVER rules)\n- ✅ Comprehensive pre-commit checklist\n- ✅ Development checklist for creating new lints\n\n---\n\n`3-3`: All referenced documentation files and example lint implementations exist and are correctly referenced. No action needed.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<sub>✏️ Tip: You can disable this entire section by setting `review_details` to `false` in your review settings.</sub>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/258#pullrequestreview-3669526673","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/258","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/258#pullrequestreview-3669526673"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/258"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-16T08:27:18Z","commit_id":"f68547173d29283c630d98c8de1999e7221d4728"},{"id":3669709233,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7au1Gx","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations