\n<summary>🧠 Learnings (3)</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📓 Common learnings</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z\nLearning: Use `@/openspec/AGENTS.md` to learn how to create and apply change proposals, spec format and conventions, and project structure and guidelines\n```\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z\nLearning: When a request mentions planning or proposals (words like proposal, spec, change, plan), or introduces new capabilities, breaking changes, architecture shifts, or big performance/security work, open `@/openspec/AGENTS.md`\n```\n\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>📚 Learning: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z\nLearning: Use `@/openspec/AGENTS.md` to learn how to create and apply change proposals, spec format and conventions, and project structure and guidelines\n```\n\n**Applied to files:**\n- `FDD-Adapter/AGENTS.md`\n\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>📚 Learning: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z\nLearning: When a request mentions planning or proposals (words like proposal, spec, change, plan), or introduces new capabilities, breaking changes, architecture shifts, or big performance/security work, open `@/openspec/AGENTS.md`\n```\n\n**Applied to files:**\n- `FDD-Adapter/AGENTS.md`\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔇 Additional comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>FDD (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **Submodule commit is valid and reachable.** The gitlink points to commit 281ddc9739d87b763b786b7de97c1890269b6cb7, which exists at HEAD on the main branch of https://github.com/ainetx/FDD and is reachable by CI/dev flows.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/AGENTS.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-39`: **Note: AI summary mentions missing Commit Rule section.**\n\nThe AI summary for this file mentions \"Introduces a new Commit Rule section, enforcing pre-commit conventions\" with DCO requirements and commit message format rules. However, no such section appears in the provided code (lines 1-39).\n\nThis may be a confusion with the root `AGENTS.md` file (mentioned in PR objectives as receiving a commit rule on the main branch). If the Commit Rule section should be in this file but is missing, please add it. Otherwise, the AI summary may need correction.\n\n---\n\n`13-39`: All 14 specification files referenced in the workflow rules are present under FDD-Adapter/specs/.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<sub>✏️ Tip: You can disable this entire section by setting `review_details` to `false` in your review settings.</sub>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/258#pullrequestreview-3669709233","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/258","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/258#pullrequestreview-3669709233"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/258"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-16T09:17:42Z","commit_id":"ec698ae0ff131e5e07b39275f2750ee0fa5cb3a7"},{"id":3669784953,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7avHl5","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 1**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@FDD-Adapter/specs/performance.md`:\n- Around line 238-244: Update the outdated dependency declaration for\nconsole-subscriber in the example Cargo.toml snippet: replace the line\n`console-subscriber = \"0.1\"` with `console-subscriber = \"0.5.0\"` (keeping the\nsurrounding tokio dependency line and the `tokio-console` run instruction\nunchanged) so the example uses the current console-subscriber version.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/performance.md (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`23-28`: **Clarify LTO benefits beyond binary size.**\n\nThe comment on Line 26 states LTO is for \"smaller binaries\" but LTO (Link Time Optimization) primarily enables cross-crate optimizations that improve runtime performance through better inlining and dead code elimination. Binary size reduction is a secondary benefit.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n [profile.release]\n codegen-units = 1        # Maximum optimization, slower builds\n-lto = false              # Can enable for smaller binaries (much slower builds)\n+lto = false              # Can enable for cross-crate optimization & smaller binaries (much slower builds)\n opt-level = 3            # Maximum optimization (default)\n```\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`189-195`: **Add blank lines around the decision matrix table.**\n\nMarkdown linting rules require blank lines before and after tables for proper rendering and consistency.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Formatting fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n **Example Decision Matrix**:\n+\n | Module Type | Deployment | Rationale |\n |-------------|-----------|-----------|\n | Core business logic | In-process | Maximum performance |\n | Third-party integrations | OoP | Fault isolation |\n | Compute-intensive tasks | OoP | Resource isolation |\n | High-frequency APIs | In-process | Low latency |\n+\n```\n</details>\n\nAs per static analysis hints (markdownlint MD058).\n\n---\n\n`62-67`: **Clarify which lint groups are included beyond `perf`.**\n\nThe configuration enables the `perf` group with `clone_on_copy` and `cast_precision_loss` listed separately, but `clone_on_copy` belongs to the `complexity` group and `cast_precision_loss` to the `pedantic` group. While the configuration is valid and functional, consider adding a comment explaining why lints from other groups are explicitly included alongside the `perf` group, such as: `# Additional performance-related checks from complexity and pedantic groups` or restructuring the layout to make the group separation clearer.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📜 Review details</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commits</summary>\n\nReviewing files that changed from the base of the PR and between ec698ae0ff131e5e07b39275f2750ee0fa5cb3a7 and bdfbdecebd2c45fdc63dd67b47c556bee123669d.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📒 Files selected for processing (2)</summary>\n\n* `FDD-Adapter/AGENTS.md`\n* `FDD-Adapter/specs/performance.md`\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🚧 Files skipped from review as they are similar to previous changes (1)</summary>\n\n* FDD-Adapter/AGENTS.md\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧰 Additional context used</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧠 Learnings (3)</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📓 Common learnings</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z\nLearning: Use `@/openspec/AGENTS.md` to learn how to create and apply change proposals, spec format and conventions, and project structure and guidelines\n```\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z\nLearning: When a request mentions planning or proposals (words like proposal, spec, change, plan), or introduces new capabilities, breaking changes, architecture shifts, or big performance/security work, open `@/openspec/AGENTS.md`\n```\n\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>📚 Learning: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:36.009Z\nLearning: When a request mentions planning or proposals (words like proposal, spec, change, plan), or introduces new capabilities, breaking changes, architecture shifts, or big performance/security work, open `@/openspec/AGENTS.md`\n```\n\n**Applied to files:**\n- `FDD-Adapter/specs/performance.md`\n\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>📚 Learning: 2026-01-14T17:18:55.263Z</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:55.263Z\nLearning: Create proposal.md file for all changes that add features, make breaking changes, change architecture/patterns, optimize performance, or update security patterns\n```\n\n**Applied to files:**\n- `FDD-Adapter/specs/performance.md`\n\n</details>\n\n</details><details>\n<summary>🪛 markdownlint-cli2 (0.18.1)</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/performance.md</summary>\n\n189-189: Tables should be surrounded by blank lines\n\n(MD058, blanks-around-tables)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔇 Additional comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>FDD-Adapter/specs/performance.md (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-16`: **LGTM! Clear performance philosophy.**\n\nThe overview effectively establishes performance as a core design principle with well-articulated goals: compile-time optimization, async-first architecture, resource efficiency, measured optimization, and quality-driven performance.\n\n---\n\n`299-351`: **LGTM! Comprehensive performance testing strategy.**\n\nThe section clearly defines test categories (latency, throughput, resource, scalability) and provides practical CI integration examples including baseline comparison workflows. The 90%+ coverage target including performance tests sets a strong quality bar.\n\n---\n\n`354-416`: **LGTM! Practical optimization guidelines with clear examples.**\n\nThe section effectively demonstrates optimization priorities and provides concrete good/bad pattern comparisons for strings, collections, async operations, and database queries. The \"profile first, measure impact, document rationale\" approach is sound.\n\n---\n\n`419-481`: **LGTM! Well-structured validation framework.**\n\nThe checklist provides clear MUST/SHOULD/MAY requirements with measurable compliance criteria (10/10 mandatory items). The 12 agent instructions (8 ALWAYS, 4 NEVER rules) and 8-item pre-commit checklist offer practical guidance for maintaining performance standards throughout development.\n\n---\n\n`484-510`: **LGTM! Comprehensive reference and actionable summary.**\n\nThe reference section consolidates all tools and resources mentioned throughout the document. The summary effectively distills the specification into memorable golden rules: Profile → Optimize → Benchmark → Document.\n\n---\n\n`154-159`: **Verify LazyLock MSRV compatibility for documentation examples.**\n\nThis is a documentation example using `std::sync::LazyLock`, which was stabilized in Rust 1.80.0 (July 2024). The project's Cargo.toml specifies edition 2021 but does not explicitly set a rust-version field. If the project maintains an MSRV lower than 1.80.0, this example should be updated to use `once_cell::sync::Lazy` or `lazy_static!` macro instead, or the documentation should note the minimum Rust version requirement.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<sub>✏️ Tip: You can disable this entire section by setting `review_details` to `false` in your review settings.</sub>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/258#pullrequestreview-3669784953","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/258","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/258#pullrequestreview-3669784953"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/258"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-16T09:29:12Z","commit_id":"bdfbdecebd2c45fdc63dd67b47c556bee123669d"},{"id":3671777346,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7a2uBC","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 4**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn @.fdd-adapter/specs/domain-model.md:\n- Around line 68-140: The spec mandates a non-existent external tool \"gts-rust\"\nand lacks schemas/CI integration; either provide the actual repository (or\ninternal location) and add generated schemas and CI hooks, or update the spec to\nreference an existing tool and remove the unreachable MUSTs: locate occurrences\nin domain-model.md (GTS Validation section), any gts_schemas/ references, and\nthe custom linters in dylint_lints/ (e.g., de09_gts_layer) to: 1) if gts-rust is\ninternal, add its repo URL and installation instructions and commit generated\nschemas under gts_schemas/, plus add CI steps to run the CLI commands shown; or\n2) if a different tool should be used, change the spec to name that tool, update\nthe example commands and CI scripts accordingly, and remove/soften MUST\nrequirements until tooling and schemas exist.\n\nIn @.fdd-adapter/specs/patterns.md:\n- Line 83: Replace the outdated RFC reference string \"RFC 7807 Problem Details\"\nin .fdd-adapter/specs/patterns.md with \"RFC 9457 Problem Details\" so it matches\nthe rest of the repository (as referenced in\n.fdd-adapter/specs/rust-guidelines.md); search for the exact header/text \"RFC\n7807 Problem Details\" and update it to \"RFC 9457\" (ensure surrounding wording\nand capitalization remain consistent).\n\nIn @.fdd-adapter/specs/project-structure.md:\n- Line 122: Replace the outdated path string \"FDD-Adapter/\" with the migrated\ndirectory name \".fdd-adapter/\" in the referenced documentation line so the\nchecklist entry reads \"- [ ] .fdd-adapter/ at project root\"; update any other\noccurrences of \"FDD-Adapter/\" in this document (project-structure.md) to\n\".fdd-adapter/\" to keep references consistent.\n- Around line 9-10: Update the directory names in the documentation to reflect\nthe migrated folder names: replace instances of \"FDD/\" with \".fdd/\" and\n\"FDD-Adapter/\" with \".fdd-adapter/\" in the project-structure.md content (the\nfile .fdd-adapter/specs/project-structure.md) so the doc matches the current\nrepository layout.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (7)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.fdd-adapter/specs/security.md (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`100-124`: **Add tenant authorization validation before SecurityCtx creation.**\n\nThe example creates `SecurityCtx` directly from `auth.tenant_id` without validating that the authenticated user is actually authorized for that tenant. In a multi-tenant system, this could allow privilege escalation if token validation doesn't enforce tenant membership.\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔒 Recommended pattern with validation</summary>\n\n```diff\n pub async fn list_users_handler(\n     Extension(auth): Extension<AuthContext>,\n     Extension(db): Extension<DbHandle>,\n ) -> Result<Json<Vec<User>>, Problem> {\n+    // Validate user has access to tenant\n+    if !auth.authorized_tenants.contains(&auth.tenant_id) {\n+        return Err(Problem::forbidden(\"Not authorized for tenant\"));\n+    }\n+    \n     // Create security context from request\n     let ctx = SecurityCtx::for_tenants(\n         vec![auth.tenant_id], \n         auth.user_id\n     );\n```\n</details>\n\nThis assumes `AuthContext` includes authorized tenant list. If the OAuth2/OIDC token validation already enforces tenant scoping, document that assumption explicitly in the example.\n\n---\n\n`265-270`: **Review rate limiting thresholds for production viability.**\n\nThe standard rate limit of 100 requests/hour (~1.67 req/min) is extremely restrictive and may break legitimate use cases such as paginated list operations, dashboard loading, or batch operations. Most production APIs set standard limits at 1,000-10,000 requests/hour for authenticated users.\n\nAdditionally, the specification should clarify:\n- Per-user vs. per-IP rate limiting strategy\n- Specific thresholds for \"sensitive ops\"\n- Whether limits apply per-endpoint or globally\n- Burst allowances for short-term spikes\n\n\n\n\nConsider revising to production-appropriate values and adding more specific guidance on rate limiting strategies.\n\n---\n\n`272-290`: **Include trace_id in audit logging example for consistency.**\n\nThe security implementation checklist (line 452) requires logging security events with `trace_id`, but the example code doesn't demonstrate this. Including trace_id is critical for correlating security events across distributed systems.\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Enhanced example with trace_id</summary>\n\n```diff\n tracing::warn!(\n+    trace_id = %trace_id,\n     user_id = %user_id,\n     tenant_id = %tenant_id,\n     action = \"delete_user\",\n     target_id = %target_user_id,\n     \"User deleted another user\"\n );\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.fdd-adapter/specs/domain-model.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`70-70`: **Fix markdown formatting for the URL.**\n\nThe bare URL should use proper markdown link syntax for consistency with documentation standards.\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Tool**: `gts` CLI from https://github.com/GlobalTypeSystem/gts-rust\n+**Tool**: `gts` CLI from [gts-rust](https://github.com/GlobalTypeSystem/gts-rust)\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.fdd-adapter/specs/performance.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`238-244`: **Update `console-subscriber` version.**\n\nThe example shows `console-subscriber = \"0.1\"`, which is significantly outdated. The latest version is 0.5.0, which includes recent features and bug fixes.\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested update</summary>\n\n```diff\n # Enable console in Cargo.toml\n tokio = { version = \"1\", features = [\"full\", \"tracing\"] }\n-console-subscriber = \"0.1\"\n+console-subscriber = \"0.5\"\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.fdd-adapter/specs/api-contracts.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`18-22`: **Specify language for the fenced code block.**\n\nThe endpoint pattern template should specify a language identifier for proper syntax highlighting and tooling support. Consider using `text` as the language identifier.\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n ## Endpoint Pattern\n \n-```\n+```text\n /{module}/v{version}/{resource}\n ```\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.fdd-adapter/specs/testing.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`114-118`: **Update documentation to reference correct test data directory.**\n\nThe documentation references `testing/e2e/fixtures/`, but the actual directory is `testing/e2e/testdata/`.\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n ## Test Configuration\n \n **Config File**: `testing/e2e/conftest.py` (pytest fixtures)\n \n-**Test Data**: `testing/e2e/fixtures/` (if needed)\n+**Test Data**: `testing/e2e/testdata/` (if needed)\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>.fdd-adapter/specs/rest-api-guidelines.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`16-16`: **RFC version consistency with related specs.**\n\nThis file correctly references RFC 9457 (the latest Problem Details standard), while `.fdd-adapter/specs/api-contracts.md` line 35 references RFC 7807 (the obsoleted version). Both refer to the same standard, but for consistency, consider updating the older reference.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.fdd-adapter/specs/testing.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`81-85`: **Add language identifier to fenced code block.**\n\nThe dependencies list should specify a language identifier for proper rendering. Consider using `text` or `requirements` as the language identifier.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n **Dependencies**:\n-```\n+```text\n pytest\n pytest-asyncio\n httpx\n ```\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n---\n\n`161-169`: **Add language identifier to fenced code block.**\n\nThe directory structure should specify a language identifier. Consider using `text` for better tooling support and consistent rendering.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n ## Test Organization\n \n-```\n+```text\n {crate}/\n ├── src/\n │   └── lib.rs              # Unit tests here\n ├── tests/\n │   ├── integration.rs      # Integration tests\n │   └── helpers/            # Test utilities\n └── Cargo.toml\n ```\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.fdd-adapter/specs/patterns.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`16-27`: **Add language identifier to fenced code block.**\n\nThe code block displaying the module structure should have a language identifier for proper syntax highlighting and markdown compliance.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n modules/{module}/\n ├── src/\n │   ├── lib.rs              # Module declaration\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n---\n\n`56-60`: **Add language identifier to fenced code block.**\n\nThe code block displaying the layered architecture should have a language identifier for proper syntax highlighting and markdown compliance.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n api/rest/           → REST API endpoints (DTOs)\n domain/             → Business logic\n infra/              → Infrastructure (DB, external services)\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on static analysis hints.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>.fdd-adapter/specs/rust-guidelines.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`119-174`: **Complete the imports in the code example.**\n\nThe code example is missing imports that would be required for compilation:\n- Line 128 uses `HashMap<String, String>` but `std::collections::HashMap` is not imported\n- Line 162 uses `impl IntoResponse` but `axum::response::IntoResponse` is not imported\n\nWhile this may be intentional for brevity, including complete imports helps developers who copy examples directly.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📦 Complete imports</summary>\n\n```diff\n ```rust\n+use std::collections::HashMap;\n+\n use axum::{extract::Query, response::Json};\n+use axum::response::IntoResponse;\n use utoipa::{IntoParams, ToSchema};\n+use serde::{Deserialize, Serialize};\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📜 Review details</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commits</summary>\n\nReviewing files that changed from the base of the PR and between bdfbdecebd2c45fdc63dd67b47c556bee123669d and 82c11e73c235c1946abd87da4ab283cc8fc926e2.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📒 Files selected for processing (19)</summary>\n\n* `.fdd`\n* `.fdd-adapter/AGENTS.md`\n* `.fdd-adapter/specs/api-contracts.md`\n* `.fdd-adapter/specs/architectural-lints.md`\n* `.fdd-adapter/specs/build-deploy.md`\n* `.fdd-adapter/specs/conventions.md`\n* `.fdd-adapter/specs/domain-model.md`\n* `.fdd-adapter/spe