curity/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/README.md</summary>\n\n3-3: Heading levels should only increment by one level at a time\nExpected: h2; Actual: h3\n\n(MD001, heading-increment)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>⏰ Context from checks skipped due to timeout of 90000ms. You can increase the timeout in your CodeRabbit configuration to a maximum of 15 minutes (900000ms). (8)</summary>\n\n* GitHub Check: Test Suite (macos-latest, rust=stable)\n* GitHub Check: Run Dylint Lints\n* GitHub Check: Code Coverage (cargo-llvm-cov)\n* GitHub Check: Dylint Lints Tests\n* GitHub Check: Test Suite (ubuntu-latest, rust=stable)\n* GitHub Check: Test Suite (windows-latest, rust=stable)\n* GitHub Check: UI macro tests (trybuild)\n* GitHub Check: users_info integration (Postgres)\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔇 Additional comments (19)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/.cargo/config.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-6`: **LGTM!**\n\nStandard dylint linker configuration that follows the established pattern used in other lint crates (de0301, de0308). The commented alternative for Rust 1.74.0+ is a helpful note for future maintainability.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/rust-toolchain (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-3`: **Verify the pinned nightly and components are still available.**  \nNightly snapshots and components (especially `rustc-dev`) can be pruned; please confirm `nightly-2025-09-18` and required components still install cleanly and align with any workspace-level toolchain pin to avoid drift.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0301_no_infra_in_domain/.cargo/config.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-6`: **LGTM!**\n\nStandard dylint linker configuration. This boilerplate is required for all dylint lint crates and matches the pattern used in other crates in this workspace.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/Cargo.toml (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`11-14`: **New lint crates correctly registered as workspace members.**\n\nThe three new lints (DE0301, DE0308, DE0706) are properly registered. As per coding guidelines, new lint crates must be added to the `members` list in the workspace root `Cargo.toml`.\n\nNote: The AI summary indicates `de0503_plugin_client_suffix` was \"Removed\", but it remains present at line 13. This may be a summary discrepancy rather than a code issue.\n\n---\n\n`47-54`: **Dev dependencies appropriately added for lint UI tests.**\n\nThese workspace-level dev dependencies enable lint test crates to compile code samples that use external libraries (e.g., testing detection of `sqlx` imports, `http` types, `serde` derives, etc.). The `thiserror = \"2.0\"` constraint is appropriate and will resolve to the latest available version.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/ui/trait_bounds_http.stderr (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-18`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe stderr output correctly captures the expected DE0308 lint diagnostics. The lint properly detects HTTP traits (`IntoHeaderName`) in return type bounds within domain modules, with clear guidance directing developers to handle HTTP in the `api/` layer.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/README.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`77-80`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe \"See Also\" section now correctly references DE0301 and DE0308, which are both part of this PR. The relative paths are accurate.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/ui/nested_generic_http_type.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-15`: **Well-structured UI test for nested generic HTTP types.**\n\nThe test correctly exercises DE0308 detection of HTTP types wrapped in generic containers (`Option`, `Vec`, `&'static`) within domain structs. Comment annotations are properly placed on the lines immediately before the expected error locations, the `.stderr` file is correctly formatted with matching diagnostics and line numbers, and the lint crate includes the required test validation.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/README.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`36-82`: **Well-documented examples showing the correct domain pattern.**\n\nThe examples clearly demonstrate the transformation from HTTP-coupled domain code to transport-agnostic domain errors with API-layer mapping. This provides excellent guidance for developers.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/lint_utils/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`33-55`: **Path exclusion helpers are well-documented.**\n\nThe doc comments and inline comments clearly explain the purpose of the multiple OR conditions, addressing the different path contexts (absolute, relative, simulated test paths). This provides good maintainability for future developers.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/src/lib.rs (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-49`: **Excellent doc comments following coding guidelines.**\n\nThe lint declaration includes comprehensive documentation with:\n- Clear explanation of what the lint checks\n- Rationale for why HTTP types in domain are problematic\n- Concrete code examples showing bad and good patterns\n\nThis follows the coding guidelines requirement to document lint behavior in doc comments.\n\n---\n\n`66-80`: **Clean use of shared `use_tree_to_strings` utility.**\n\nThe check_use_item function correctly uses the shared utility from lint_utils to get all import paths and checks each against HTTP_PATTERNS. This addresses the past review about handling all nested use items.\n\n---\n\n`239-251`: **Tests follow required conventions.**\n\nThe test module includes:\n1. `ui_examples()` using `dylint_testing::ui_test_examples` for external dependencies (http, axum)\n2. `test_comment_annotations_match_stderr()` as required by coding guidelines\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/ui/good_domain_0308.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-19`: **Good negative test case following UI test conventions.**\n\nThis file correctly:\n- Includes a no-violations test case as required by coding guidelines\n- Uses `simulated_dir` to simulate domain path\n- Places \"Should not trigger\" comments before non-HTTP imports\n- Demonstrates the recommended pattern with a transport-agnostic `DomainResult` enum\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/ui/trait_bounds_http.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-17`: **Test file structure and annotation placement are correct.**\n\nThe test properly:\n- Places \"Should trigger\" comments on the line before each function declaration\n- Uses `simulated_dir` to simulate a domain path\n- Tests HTTP types appearing in `impl Trait` return type bounds\n\nThe stderr file correctly reports two DE0308 errors at lines 9 and 14, matching the annotation comments. The lint implementation successfully detects `axum::http::header::IntoHeaderName` as a direct HTTP trait in the bounds.\n\nNote: The current implementation checks trait paths but doesn't recurse into generic arguments within bounds (e.g., `Iterator<Item = http::StatusCode>`). This test covers only direct HTTP traits, which are caught correctly. Associated type constraints within generics remain outside the current scope.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0301_no_infra_in_domain/src/lib.rs (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-51`: **LGTM - Lint declaration follows guidelines.**\n\nThe lint is correctly declared using `declare_early_lint!` with comprehensive doc comments explaining what it does, why violations are bad, and providing good/bad examples. This follows the coding guidelines for documenting lint behavior in doc comments.\n\n---\n\n`205-250`: **LGTM - EarlyLintPass implementation is comprehensive.**\n\nThe lint correctly scopes to domain paths using `is_in_domain_path` and handles the key item kinds: `Use`, `Struct`, `Enum`, `Fn`, and `TyAlias`. This covers the main vectors for infrastructure leakage.\n\n---\n\n`252-264`: **LGTM - Required tests are present.**\n\nThe implementation includes both the UI test via `ui_test_examples` and the required `test_comment_annotations_match_stderr` test using the `lint_utils` helper. Based on learnings, this is mandatory for lint crates.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0301_no_infra_in_domain/ui/bad_infra_import.stderr (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-27`: **LGTM - Test artifact correctly captures expected diagnostics.**\n\nThe stderr file properly documents the expected lint output for infrastructure imports (sea_orm, sqlx, axum) in domain code. Error messages are clear, include the lint code (DE0301), and provide actionable help text pointing to the correct resolution.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<sub>✏️ Tip: You can disable this entire section by setting `review_details` to `false` in your review settings.</sub>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3672231300","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3672231300"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-16T18:41:12Z","commit_id":"571ad8ed83ab8ade58a0dbb97563b1f215280109"},{"id":3677172948,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7bLTTU","user":{"login":"nanoandrew4","id":14365320,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzY1MzIw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/14365320?u=4d0fde58d4a710f70ed62e0ec0b5071b0f67f2a7&v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4","html_url":"https://github.com/nanoandrew4","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"I'm just now realizing after Mikes comment on #247 how brittle a lot of our lints are, since we may ban sqlx and sea-orm but there are other alternatives that the LLM could use to skirt around these restrictions. I'm not sure there is anything that can be done about it, besides accepting that some of these lints should give LLMs a limited level of guidance, but won't stop them from going against the guidelines.","state":"CHANGES_REQUESTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3677172948","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"MEMBER","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3677172948"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-19T09:57:05Z","commit_id":"b57ecb4995c724e8dba342666c911a0bb4914448"},{"id":3678164876,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7bPFeM","user":{"login":"nanoandrew4","id":14365320,"node_id":"MDQ6VXNlcjE0MzY1MzIw","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/u/14365320?u=4d0fde58d4a710f70ed62e0ec0b5071b0f67f2a7&v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4","html_url":"https://github.com/nanoandrew4","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/nanoandrew4/received_events","type":"User","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"After further review I've found that all three lints miss some scenarios and can be circumvented. Here are some examples that should fail but pass:\r\n\r\n```rust\r\n// Test file for DE0706: No Direct sqlx Usage via Qualified Paths\r\n// This file demonstrates BAD patterns using fully qualified paths without imports\r\n#![allow(unused_imports)]\r\n#![allow(dead_code)]\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0706 - sqlx\r\nstruct Database {\r\n    pool: sqlx::pool::Pool<sqlx::Any>,\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0706 - sqlx\r\nasync fn query_users(pool: &sqlx::pool::Pool<sqlx::Any>) -> Result<(), sqlx::Error> {\r\n    Ok(())\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0706 - sqlx\r\ntype DbPool = sqlx::pool::Pool<sqlx::Any>;\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0706 - sqlx\r\nenum DatabaseConfig {\r\n    Any(sqlx::pool::PoolOptions<sqlx::Any>),\r\n}\r\n\r\nfn main() {}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```rust\r\n// simulated_dir=/hyperspot/modules/some_module/domain/service.rs\r\n// Test file for DE0301: Circumvention via expressions in function bodies\r\n// This file demonstrates potential circumvention using qualified paths in expressions\r\n#![allow(unused_imports)]\r\n#![allow(dead_code)]\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0301 - infra in domain (via local variable type annotation)\r\nfn use_sqlx_in_local() {\r\n    let pool: sqlx::pool::Pool<sqlx::Any> = todo!();\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0301 - infra in domain (via turbofish)\r\nfn use_sqlx_turbofish() {\r\n    let _result = some_generic_fn::<sqlx::pool::Pool<sqlx::Any>>();\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0301 - infra in domain (via as cast)\r\nfn use_sqlx_cast() {\r\n    let _val = some_value() as sqlx::Error;\r\n}\r\n\r\nfn some_generic_fn<T>() -> T {\r\n    todo!()\r\n}\r\n\r\nfn some_value() -> i32 {\r\n    42\r\n}\r\n\r\nfn main() {}\r\n```\r\n\r\n```rust\r\n// simulated_dir=/hyperspot/modules/some_module/domain/service.rs\r\n// Test file for DE0308: Circumvention via expressions in function bodies\r\n// This file demonstrates potential circumvention using qualified paths in expressions\r\n#![allow(unused_imports)]\r\n#![allow(dead_code)]\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0308 - HTTP in domain (via local variable type annotation)\r\nfn use_http_in_local() {\r\n    let status: http::StatusCode = todo!();\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0308 - HTTP in domain (via turbofish)\r\nfn use_http_turbofish() {\r\n    let _result = some_generic_fn::<http::StatusCode>();\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0308 - HTTP in domain (via as cast)\r\nfn use_http_cast() {\r\n    let _val = some_value() as http::Method;\r\n}\r\n\r\n// Should trigger DE0308 - HTTP in domain (via nested generic in local)\r\nfn use_http_nested() {\r\n    let _opt: Option<http::HeaderMap> = None;\r\n}\r\n\r\nfn some_generic_fn<T>() -> T {\r\n    todo!()\r\n}\r\n\r\nfn some_value() -> i32 {\r\n    42\r\n}\r\n\r\nfn main() {}\r\n```\r\n\r\nPlease review these examples to tweak the lints to be more robust. AI can help you identify possible holes in the implementation that should be caught by the lint but aren't.","state":"CHANGES_REQUESTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3678164876","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"MEMBER","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3678164876"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-19T13:43:46Z","commit_id":"b57ecb4995c724e8dba342666c911a0bb4914448"},{"id":3721386173,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7dz9i9","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 4**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/src/lib.rs`:\n- Around line 163-187: The ImplTrait handler (TyKind::ImplTrait) currently only\nchecks the trait path string against HTTP_PATTERNS and must be extended to\nrecursively inspect its generic arguments and any associated-type constraints\n(AngleBracketedArg::Constraint) just like the TyKind::Path handler does; update\nthe ImplTrait branch to walk trait_ref.trait_ref.path segments' generic args and\nfor each AngleBracketedArg handle both Arg and Constraint variants, recursively\ninvoking the same type-check routine that triggers DE0308_NO_HTTP_IN_DOMAIN when\na path starts with an HTTP_PATTERNS entry, and add unit tests covering cases\nlike impl Iterator<Item = http::StatusCode> and impl Service<Response =\nhttp::Response>.\n\nIn `@dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/src/lib.rs`:\n- Around line 96-108: The lint misses plain `extern crate sqlx;` because current\nlogic only inspects `name` in ItemKind::ExternCrate; update the check in the\nExternCrate branch to also inspect `_ident` when `name` is None: if `name` is\nSome use `sym.as_str()` as before, otherwise get the identifier string from\n`_ident` (e.g., `_ident.as_str()` or `_ident.to_string()` equivalent) and\ncompare it against SQLX_PATTERN; when it matches, emit the same\nDE0706_NO_DIRECT_SQLX diagnostic using item.span and the existing\ndiag.primary_message/diag.help/diag.note flow.\n\nIn `@libs/modkit-macros/src/domain_model.rs`:\n- Around line 46-63: The generated field assertions currently reference type\nparameters out of scope; update the field_assertions generation so the helper\nvalidation function includes the original item's generic parameters and\nwhere-clauses (i.e., propagate generics and where predicates) so generic types\ncompile; modify the quoted block that defines __validate_domain_model_fields\n(and keeps __assert_field_is_domain_safe and uses #(`#field_types`)*) to declare\nthe same generics and where-clause as the input (so T and bounds are in scope),\nand add the suggested unit test for a generic struct to verify DomainSafe bounds\nare preserved.\n\nIn `@libs/modkit/src/domain/mod.rs`:\n- Around line 54-59: The docblock claims blanket implementations for\nchrono::DateTime and rust_decimal::Decimal but those impls are missing; either\nadd the missing impls or remove them from the documentation. Fix by locating the\nblanket-impl module in mod.rs (the \"Blanket Implementations\" section) and either\n(A) add the corresponding impl blocks for chrono::DateTime<Tz> and\nrust_decimal::Decimal matching the pattern used for uuid::Uuid (implement the\nsame trait(s)/From/Into/Serialize/Deserialize or the project's custom trait\nnames used elsewhere), or (B) if you don’t intend to support them, remove the\nchrono::DateTime and rust_decimal::Decimal lines from the doc comment so the\nlist only contains actually implemented types like uuid::Uuid; ensure the\nidentifiers chrono::DateTime and rust_decimal::Decimal are referenced exactly\nwhen adding impls so reviewers can find them.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit/tests/domain_model_tests.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`8-21`: **Test is ignored pending `.stderr` file generation.**\n\nThe compile-fail test is properly structured but currently disabled. The `#[ignore]` attribute with the TODO comment is appropriate for incremental development. The windows-gnu skip logic is a good defensive measure for CI stability.\n\n\n\nWould you like me to help generate the expected `.stderr` file for `domain_model_with_infra_type.rs`? The error should indicate that `http::StatusCode` does not implement `DomainSafe`.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple_user_settings/simple_user_settings/src/domain/repo.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`7-11`: **Verify: Unconventional where clause on concrete types.**\n\nThe `where` clause constrains concrete types (`SimpleUserSettings`, `SimpleUserSettingsPatch`) rather than generic parameters. This is a valid Rust pattern that acts as a compile-time assertion—if these types don't implement `DomainModel`, the trait definition itself fails to compile.\n\nWhile this achieves the enforcement goal, it's an unusual pattern. More commonly, domain model constraints are enforced either:\n1. At the impl site via generic bounds\n2. Via module-level const assertions\n3. Through the `#[domain_model]` macro on the types themselves\n\nIf this is the intended compile-time guard pattern for repositories, consider documenting it as a project convention.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Alternative: Rely solely on `#[domain_model]` on the types</summary>\n\nIf `SimpleUserSettings` and `SimpleUserSettingsPatch` are already annotated with `#[domain_model]`, the where clause here is redundant—the macro already enforces `DomainModel` implementation. The where clause adds an extra layer of enforcement at the repository trait level.\n\nIf redundant enforcement is not desired:\n```diff\n #[async_trait]\n-pub trait SettingsRepository: Send + Sync\n-where\n-    SimpleUserSettings: DomainModel,\n-    SimpleUserSettingsPatch: DomainModel,\n-{\n+pub trait SettingsRepository: Send + Sync {\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit-macros/src/domain_model.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`142-155`: **Consider adding a test that verifies generic struct expansion compiles correctly.**\n\nThe current test only checks that the output contains \"DomainSafe\" and \"DomainModel\" strings. It would be more robust to have an integration test that actually compiles a generic struct with a bound like `Container<T> where T: DomainSafe` to ensure the generated code is valid.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit/src/domain/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`119-123`: **Consider adding `DomainSafe` implementation for references.**\n\nReferences (`&T`, `&mut T`) are commonly used and would benefit from blanket implementations. Without this, domain models cannot easily reference other domain-safe data.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested addition</summary>\n\n```rust\nimpl<T: DomainSafe + ?Sized> DomainSafe for &T {}\nimpl<T: DomainSafe + ?Sized> DomainSafe for &mut T {}\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3721386173","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3721386173"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-29T08:42:54Z","commit_id":"a634384a3b43f6964ec935caf04f7444fac03412"},{"id":3721452163,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7d0NqD","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 3**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@libs/modkit/src/domain/mod.rs`:\n- Around line 135-138: The impls for HashMap and HashSet currently allow any\nBuildHasher S, letting non-domain-safe hashers slip through; update the impl\nheaders for HashMap<K, V, S> and HashSet<T, S> so that S is constrained to be\nDomainSafe as well (e.g. S: DomainSafe + std::hash::BuildHasher) while keeping\nK, V, T bounds unchanged; modify the impl lines for DomainSafe for HashMap and\nDomainSafe for HashSet accordingly.\n- Around line 11-19: Update the docs example for the User struct to use\nfully-qualified chrono types: replace DateTime<Utc> with\nchrono::DateTime<chrono::Utc> in the rust,ignore block so the User struct\nexample (pub struct User { pub id: Uuid, pub email: String, pub created_at: ...\n}) explicitly references chrono and matches the project's documentation style.\n- Around line 1-37: Document or justify the Domain marker traits (DomainSafe,\nDomainModel, DomainErrorMarker) in the ModKit docs by adding a short section to\nthe modkit_unified_system docs (e.g., 01_overview.md or 05_errors_rfc9457.md)\nexplaining their intended architectural independence or how they integrate with\nSDK/ClientHub/lifecycle patterns; and tighten the DomainSafe impls for\ncollection types by requiring the hasher type parameter to also be DomainSafe so\ncustom hashers cannot introduce infra deps—update the HashMap and HashSet impl\nblocks (the impl for HashMap<K, V, S> and the impl for HashSet<T, S>) to add S:\nDomainSafe alongside std::hash::BuildHasher.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3721452163","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3721452163"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-29T08:58:10Z","commit_id":"d2ca80a6fd91a4672c0be9ba5ac53622dcfc1806"},{"id":3721528131,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7d0gND","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 2**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/src/lib.rs`:\n- Line 8: Add a guard to skip contract modules alongside the existing\nis_in_modkit_db_path and is_in_hyperspot_server_path checks so contract/\ndefinitions are not linted, and update the helper call that currently passes\ncx.sess().source_map() to instead pass the EarlyContext directly (the helper\nexpects EarlyContext). Locate the code around use_tree_to_strings and the\nexisting is_in_modkit_db_path / is_in_hyperspot_server_path checks, add the\ncontract-module guard there, and change the helper invocation to accept the\nEarlyContext (cx) rather than cx.sess().source_map().\n- Around line 49-76: The current check_use_for_sqlx relies on starts_with_sqlx\nwhich inspects tree.prefix.segments and misses grouped imports; instead, call\nuse_tree_to_strings(use_tree) inside check_use_for_sqlx and iterate all returned\npath strings, splitting or parsing each path to check whether its first segment\nequals SQLX_PATTERN, and if any match emit DE0706_NO_DIRECT_SQLX with the same\ndiagnostic text; alternatively refactor starts_with_sqlx to accept the vector of\npaths from use_tree_to_strings and return true if any path's first segment ==\nSQLX_PATTERN, then use that helper in check_use_for_sqlx.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3721528131","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3721528131"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-29T09:16:52Z","commit_id":"0ad50568fe86ac69d56f4eeee63b3429f7ea33fd"},{"id":3739639827,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7e5mAT","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 3**\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> Some comments are outside the diff and can’t be posted inline due to platform limitations.\n> \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>⚠️ Outside diff range comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>.github/workflows/pr-dashboard.yml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `572-590`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n> \n> **Avoid clobbering existing labels on sync.**  \n> The `labels` parameter in `issues.update` uses replace semantics—it overwrites the entire label set on the issue. Passing `labels: [\"pr-issue\"]` will erase any existing labels (including manual triage labels) on every sync run. Merge existing labels with `\"pr-issue\"` before updating, or use the \"Add labels\" endpoint (`POST /repos/{owner}/{repo}/issues/{issue_number}/labels`) to add without removing existing labels.\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🔧 Proposed fix (preserve existing labels)</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n> -                await github.rest.issues.update({\n> -                  owner,\n> -                  repo,\n> -                  issue_number: issue.number,\n> -                  title: issueTitle,\n> -                  body,\n> -                  labels: [\"pr-issue\"]\n> -                });\n> +                const existingLabels = (issue.labels || [])\n> +                  .map(l => typeof l === \"string\" ? l : l.name)\n> +                  .filter(Boolean);\n> +                const labels = Array.from(new Set([...existingLabels, \"pr-issue\"]));\n> +\n> +                await github.rest.issues.update({\n> +                  owner,\n> +                  repo,\n> +                  issue_number: issue.number,\n> +                  title: issueTitle,\n> +                  body,\n> +                  labels\n> +                });\n> ```\n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@docs/spec-templates/README.md`:\n- Line 152: The example ID shown as `fdd-api-nfr-response-time` uses the `nfr`\nkind but the Kind Reference requires NFRs to use the `req` kind; update the\nexample to follow the convention (rename or reformat the ID and any associated\nkind marker to use `req` instead of `nfr`, e.g., `fdd-api-req-response-time`) so\nthe example matches the \"NFR (still uses `req`)\" guidance and any surrounding\ntext that references the kind.\n- Line 144: The example ID uses the `fr` kind (`p1` - `fdd-auth-fr-user-roles`)\nbut the Kind Reference mandates using `req` for requirements; update the example\nto use the `req` kind (e.g., rename `fdd-auth-fr-user-roles` to\n`fdd-auth-req-user-roles` or otherwise change the kind field to `req`) and\nadjust any local references in that example to match the new ID/kind so the\nexample aligns with the Kind Reference.\n\nIn `@libs/modkit-macros/src/domain_model.rs`:\n- Around line 17-35: The current FORBIDDEN_PATTERNS only covers root-prefixed\nforms like \"::infra::\" and \"::api::\" but type_path_to_string strips a leading\n\"::\", so \"infra::Foo\" / \"api::Bar\" bypass the check; update the check by adding\nnon-root-prefixed variants (e.g., \"infra::\", \"infrastructure::\", \"api::\") to\nFORBIDDEN_PATTERNS or alternatively normalize the string returned by\ntype_path_to_string (prefix with \"::\" before matching) so that symbols\nreferenced here (FORBIDDEN_PATTERNS and type_path_to_string) will correctly\ndetect both \"::infra::\"/\"::api::\" and \"infra::\"/\"api::\" usages.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (16)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>docs/spec-templates/FEATURE.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`34-34`: **Clarify or make consistent the phase placeholder usage across sections.**\n\nThe Flow section now includes a `ph-X` placeholder (line 34), but the Algorithm (line 66), State (line 92), and Requirement (line 112) sections do not have similar phase placeholders at the section level. This inconsistency may confuse users of the template.\n\nConsider one of the following approaches:\n1. Add `ph-X` placeholders to all section-level IDs (Algorithms, States, Requirements) for consistency.\n2. Document in the template why only Flows receive phase indicators at the section level.\n3. Remove the `ph-X` from the Flow section if phasing should only apply at the step/sub-item level (where `ph-1` already appears).\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>examples/plugin-modules/tenant_resolver/tenant_resolver-gw/src/module.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`35-39`: **Update log message to match new module name.**\n\nThe rename from `tenant_resolver_gateway` to `tenant_resolver_gw` aligns with the crate naming convention (`tenant_resolver-gw`). The new `\"types\"` dependency is valid and exists in the workspace at `modules/system/types/`.\n\nHowever, the log message at line 59 still references the old name `\"tenant_resolver_gateway\"`:\n\n```rust\ninfo!(vendor = %cfg.vendor, \"Initializing tenant_resolver_gateway\");\n```\n\nUpdate it to match the new module name:\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        info!(vendor = %cfg.vendor, \"Initializing tenant_resolver_gateway\");\n+        info!(vendor = %cfg.vendor, \"Initializing tenant_resolver_gw\");\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>Makefile (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`79-82`: **Consider adding lychee configuration for CI reliability.**\n\nExternal link checking can cause intermittent CI failures due to rate limiting, network issues, or temporarily unavailable sites. Consider:\n\n1. Adding a `.lychee.toml` configuration file with timeouts, retries, and excludes for known flaky URLs\n2. Using `--no-progress` flag for cleaner CI output\n3. Using `--exclude-mail` if email validation isn't needed\n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested improvement</summary>\n\n```diff\n # Run markdown checks with 'lychee'\n lychee:\n \t$(call ensure_tool,lychee)\n-\tlychee docs examples dylint_lints guidelines\n+\tlychee --no-progress docs examples dylint_lints guidelines\n```\n\nOr create a `.lychee.toml` at project root:\n```toml\nmax_retries = 3\ntimeout = 20\nexclude_mail = true\n# exclude = [\"example\\\\.com\"]\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/types-sdk/Cargo.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`24-25`: **Clarify: Is `modkit-odata` truly optional?**\n\nThe comment states \"Optional: OData filtering/pagination support\" but the dependency is declared without `optional = true`. If this dependency is genuinely optional for consumers who don't need OData support, it should be marked as a Cargo feature:\n\n```toml\n[features]\ndefault = []\nodata = [\"modkit-odata\"]\n\n[dependencies]\n# ...\nmodkit-odata = { workspace = true, optional = true }\n```\n\nOtherwise, if it's a required dependency, consider updating the comment to avoid confusion.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit-macros/src/domain_model.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`274-455`: **Add a regression test for root-relative infra paths.**\n\nThis locks in the `infra::` / `api::` matching behavior and avoids regressions.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✅ Suggested test</summary>\n\n```diff\n@@\n     fn test_forbidden_type_by_name() {\n         let input: DeriveInput = parse_quote! {\n             pub struct BadModel {\n                 pub pool: PgPool,\n             }\n         };\n@@\n         assert!(output_str.contains(\"compile_error\"));\n         assert!(output_str.contains(\"PgPool\"));\n     }\n+\n+    #[test]\n+    fn test_forbidden_infra_module_path() {\n+        let input: DeriveInput = parse_quote! {\n+            pub struct BadModel {\n+                pub repo: infra::Repo,\n+            }\n+        };\n+\n+        let output = expand_domain_model(&input);\n+        let output_str = output.to_string();\n+\n+        assert!(output_str.contains(\"compile_error\"));\n+        assert!(output_str.contains(\"infra::\"));\n+    }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit/Cargo.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`84-117`: **Consider moving `zip` to workspace dependencies to reduce duplication.**\n\nThe `zip` dependency is defined identically in both runtime and build-dependencies (version \"2.3\", features [\"deflate\"]). Adding it to `[workspace.dependencies]` in the root `Cargo.toml` and referencing it with `{ workspace = true }` aligns with the pattern used by other dependencies in this crate and helps prevent version drift between sections.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit/build.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`35-58`: **Redundant existence check for `schemas_dir`.**\n\nThe directory existence was already validated at Line 13. This check is always true here.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed simplification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-    // Add all .schema.json files\n-    if Path::new(schemas_dir).exists() {\n-        let mut entries: Vec<_> = std::fs::read_dir(schemas_dir)?.collect::<Result<_, _>>()?;\n-        entries.sort_by_key(std::fs::DirEntry::path);\n-        for entry in entries {\n+    // Add all .schema.json files\n+    let mut entries: Vec<_> = std::fs::read_dir(schemas_dir)?.collect::<Result<_, _>>()?;\n+    entries.sort_by_key(std::fs::DirEntry::path);\n+    for entry in entries {\n```\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`28-30`: **Consider using `0o644` for data file permissions.**\n\nJSON schema files are data, not executables. Using `0o755` (executable) is unconventional for non-executable content. While this doesn't affect functionality within the ZIP, `0o644` is more appropriate.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n     let options = zip::write::FileOptions::<()>::default()\n         .compression_method(zip::CompressionMethod::Deflated)\n-        .unix_permissions(0o755);\n+        .unix_permissions(0o644);\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 51-53\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/types-sdk/src/error.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`42-52`: **Consider adding `is_internal()` predicate for API completeness.**\n\nThe other two variants have predicate methods. For API consistency, `Internal` could have one too.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Optional addition</summary>\n\n```diff\n     /// Returns `true` if this is a registration failed error.\n     #[must_use]\n     pub const fn is_registration_failed(&self) -> bool {\n         matches!(self, Self::RegistrationFailed(_))\n     }\n+\n+    /// Returns `true` if this is an internal error.\n+    #[must_use]\n+    pub const fn is_internal(&self) -> bool {\n+        matches!(self, Self::Internal(_))\n+    }\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit/src/gts/schemas.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-3`: **Consider more specific import.**\n\nThe `use zip;` import is unconventional. Prefer importing specific items or using fully qualified paths at usage sites.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested import style</summary>\n\n```diff\n use std::io::Read;\n use tracing::info;\n-use zip;\n+use zip::ZipArchive;\n```\n\nThen update Line 23:\n```diff\n-    let mut archive = zip::ZipArchive::new(cursor)?;\n+    let mut archive = ZipArchive::new(cursor)?;\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/types/src/domain/local_client.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`24-32`: **Consider relaxed memory ordering for the readiness flag.**\n\n`SeqCst` is the strongest ordering and works correctly, but for a simple flag that's set once during initialization and read later, `Release`/`Acquire` semantics are sufficient and express intent more precisely.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Optional refinement</summary>\n\n```diff\n     pub fn set_ready(&self) {\n-        self.ready.store(true, Ordering::SeqCst);\n+        self.ready.store(true, Ordering::Release);\n     }\n }\n\n #[async_trait]\n impl TypesClient for TypesLocalClient {\n     async fn is_ready(&self) -> Result<bool, TypesError> {\n-        Ok(self.ready.load(Ordering::SeqCst))\n+        Ok(self.ready.load(Ordering::Acquire))\n     }\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file_parser/tests/xlsx_parser_tests.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`38-41`: **Silent test skipping may mask missing test data in CI.**\n\nTests return early with `eprintln` when files are missing, causing them to pass silently. If test data provisioning fails in CI, these tests won't catch regressions.\n\nConsider using `#[ignore]` for tests requiring external data, or failing explicitly when running in CI:\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Alternative: fail in CI, skip locally</summary>\n\n```rust\nfn get_test_file_or_skip(filename: &str) -> Option<PathBuf> {\n    let path = get_test_file_path(filename);\n    if !path.exists() {\n        if std::env::var(\"CI\").is_ok() {\n            panic!(\"Required test file not found in CI: {path:?}\");\n        }\n        eprintln!(\"Skipping test: test file not found at {path:?}\");\n        return None;\n    }\n    Some(path)\n}\n```\n\nThen in tests:\n```rust\nlet Some(test_file) = get_test_file_or_skip(\"simple_data.xlsx\") else {\n    return;\n};\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 71-74, 127-130\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file_parser/tests/pptx_parser_tests.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`7-16`: **Duplicate helper function across test files.**\n\nThe `get_test_file_path` helper is identical to the one in `xlsx_parser_tests.rs`. Consider extracting to a shared test utilities module to reduce duplication.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Example shared helper location</summary>\n\nCreate `modules/file_parser/tests/common/mod.rs`:\n```rust\nuse std::path::PathBuf;\n\npub fn get_test_file_path(subdir: &str, filename: &str) -> PathBuf {\n    PathBuf::from(env!(\"CARGO_MANIFEST_DIR\"))\n        .parent()\n        .unwrap()\n        .parent()\n        .unwrap()\n        .join(\"testing/e2e/testdata\")\n        .join(subdir)\n        .join(filename)\n}\n```\n\nThen in test files:\n```rust\nmod common;\n// ...\nlet test_file = common::get_test_file_path(\"pptx\", \"simple_presentation.pptx\");\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/types/src/module.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`77-82`: **Review `set_ready()` timing relative to client registration.**\n\nCurrently `set_ready()` is called before the client is registered in the hub. This means if another component checks `is_ready()` between these lines (unlikely but possible in concurrent scenarios), the client reports ready but isn't yet accessible via the hub.\n\nIf \"ready\" semantically means \"the TypesClient is available\", consider:\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Move set_ready after registration</summary>\n\n```diff\n         // Create and register the local client\n         let client = TypesLocalClient::new(Arc::clone(&self.ready));\n-        client.set_ready();\n\n         let api: Arc<dyn TypesClient> = Arc::new(client);\n         ctx.client_hub().register::<dyn TypesClient>(api);\n\n+        // Mark ready after registration so is_ready() implies the client is accessible\n+        self.ready.store(true, std::sync::atomic::Ordering::Release);\n+\n         info!(\"Types module initialized\");\n```\n\nNote: This requires calling `store` directly on `self.ready` since `client` is moved into `Arc`.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`49-55`: **Clone impl could be derived.**\n\nSince the struct only contains `Arc<AtomicBool>`, you could `#[derive(Clone)]` instead of the manual impl. The generated code would be identical.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Simplification</summary>\n\n```diff\n+#[derive(Clone)]\n pub struct Types {\n     ready: Arc<AtomicBool>,\n }\n\n impl Default for Types {\n     fn default() -> Self {\n         Self {\n             ready: Arc::new(AtomicBool::new(false)),\n         }\n     }\n }\n\n-impl Clone for Types {\n-    fn clone(&self) -> Self {\n-        Self {\n-            ready: Arc::clone(&self.ready),\n-        }\n-    }\n-}\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/testdata/generate_xlsx_pptx_testdata.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`12-24`: **Record generator dependencies in dev requirements/docs.**  \nThis script relies on `openpyxl` and `python-pptx`; capturing these in a dev/test requirements file or contributor docs will make setup reproducible.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3739639827","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3739639827"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-02T14:08:13Z","commit_id":"1dc3fe15841b7421eb05a482e8d7d3604828b160"},{"id":3740766395,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7e95C7","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github