8_no_http_in_domain needs to be\nexpanded to accurately describe the lint's full detection scope: update the\ndescription to state that de0308_no_http_in_domain inspects domain files\n(*/domain/*.rs) for HTTP types not only in imports but anywhere they appear\n(function signatures, struct/enum fields, type aliases, generic arguments), and\nexplicitly list framework re-exports and extra crates it recognizes (e.g.,\naxum::http re-exports, hyper::StatusCode, hyper::Method, reqwest::StatusCode);\nreference the implementation in lib.rs (where axum::http is handled) and any\nhelper identifiers that perform path/type checks so readers know the README\nmatches the code behavior.\n\nIn `@libs/modkit-macros/src/domain_model.rs`:\n- Around line 175-259: The macro currently misses validation for bare struct\ngeneric parameters, allowing patterns like struct Wrapper<T>{inner:T} and then\nimpl DomainModel for Wrapper<T> to accept infra types; update the macro\nexpansion to collect generic type parameters from the struct (e.g., from the\nTypeGenerics / Generics on the input) and treat unbounded TypeParam idents as\nforbidden when they appear as field types unless the param has a bound like\nDomainModel or DomainSafe; modify or extend\nvalidate_field_type/validate_type_path to detect syn::Type::Path pointing to a\nsingle-segment Ident that matches one of the struct's generic params and then\nemit a syn::Error requiring an explicit DomainModel/DomainSafe bound, and add a\ncompile-fail UI test that defines struct Wrapper<T>{ inner: T } and attempts\nWrapper<sqlx::PgPool> to ensure the macro rejects it at compile time.\n\nIn `@libs/modkit/src/domain/mod.rs`:\n- Around line 50-52: The current import \"use uuid::Uuid\" is private but the\ncomment says it should be re-exported; change it to a public re-export by\nreplacing the private import with \"pub use uuid::Uuid\" so other modules can\nrefer to domain::Uuid (locate this in mod.rs where Uuid is imported).\n\nIn `@libs/modkit/tests/domain_enforcement_demo.rs`:\n- Line 12: The documentation comment referencing \"cargo check -p cf-modkit\"\nlikely uses the wrong package name compared to the crate imports (modkit and\nmodkit_macros) in domain_enforcement_demo.rs; update the comment so the package\nname matches the actual crate published in Cargo.toml (either change \"cf-modkit\"\nto \"modkit\" or adjust the imports to the cf-modkit crate name), and ensure the\ntest file symbols (modkit, modkit_macros) compile with that package name so\n`cargo check -p <correct-package>` runs successfully.\n\nIn `@libs/modkit/tests/domain_model_tests.rs`:\n- Around line 8-21: The test domain_model_compile_fail_tests is ignored because\nthe compile-fail expected output for tests/ui/domain_model_with_infra_type.rs is\nmissing; run the test locally (or cargo test --test ...) to produce the trybuild\n.stderr artifact for domain_model_with_infra_type.rs, add that generated .stderr\nfile into tests/ui alongside the .rs file, and then remove the #[ignore = \"TODO:\nEnable after generating .stderr files\"] attribute from the\ndomain_model_compile_fail_tests test so\ntrybuild::TestCases::compile_fail(\"tests/ui/domain_model_with_infra_type.rs\")\ncan validate the expected compiler output.\n\nIn `@modules/file_parser/src/infra/parsers/xlsx_parser.rs`:\n- Around line 180-194: Replace the debug-format default filename and incorporate\nthe content_type hint into format detection: instead of building\ndefault_filename with format!(\"unknown.{:?}\", ExcelFormat::default()), generate\na proper lowercase extension using the ExcelFormat API (e.g.,\nExcelFormat::default().extension() or to_string().to_lowercase()) so the default\nbecomes \"unknown.xlsx\" not \"unknown.Xlsx\"; then include the provided\ncontent_type (the _content_type parameter) as an intermediate hint in the\ndetection chain so the format resolution becomes\nExcelFormat::from_magic_bytes(bytes.as_ref()).or_else(||\ncontent_type.and_then(|ct| ExcelFormat::from_mime_type(ct).ok())).or_else(||\nExcelFormat::from_filename(&filename).ok())..., and remove the unused underscore\nprefix if you use the parameter.\n- Around line 157-164: The default filename uses the debug formatter (\"{:?}\")\nproducing \"unknown.Xlsx\" which doesn't match lowercase checks in\nExcelFormat::from_filename; change the construction of default_filename to yield\na lowercase extension that matches from_filename (e.g., use the\nDisplay/to_string() value or call to_lowercase() on it, or better, use an\nexplicit extension helper/extension() method if ExcelFormat provides one) so\nthat default_filename, filename, and the subsequent\nExcelFormat::from_filename(filename) behave correctly.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file_parser/src/infra/parsers/pptx_parser.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`50-54`: **Consider centralizing the PPTX MIME literal.**\n\nThe same MIME string is now duplicated in two call sites; a `const PPTX_MIME: &str = \"...\";` would prevent drift if it changes later.  \n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed refactor</summary>\n\n```diff\n+const PPTX_MIME: &str =\n+    \"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation\";\n...\n-        let mut builder = DocumentBuilder::new(ParsedSource::LocalPath(path.display().to_string()))\n-            .content_type(\n-                \"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation\",\n-            )\n+        let mut builder = DocumentBuilder::new(ParsedSource::LocalPath(path.display().to_string()))\n+            .content_type(PPTX_MIME)\n             .blocks(blocks);\n...\n-        let mut builder = DocumentBuilder::new(source)\n-            .content_type(\n-                \"application/vnd.openxmlformats-officedocument.presentationml.presentation\",\n-            )\n+        let mut builder = DocumentBuilder::new(source)\n+            .content_type(PPTX_MIME)\n             .blocks(blocks);\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 80-83\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file_parser/src/infra/parsers/xlsx_parser.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`29-39`: **Silent fallback to default for unknown extensions.**\n\nThe `From<&str>` impl silently defaults to `Xlsx` for unrecognized extensions. This is a reasonable choice since `From` cannot fail, and the fallible `from_filename` method exists for cases where error handling is needed. Consider adding a brief doc comment noting this fallback behavior for future maintainers.\n\n---\n\n`224-254`: **Duplicate format detection.**\n\nFormat is detected in `parse_bytes` (lines 187-193) and again here (line 226). Consider passing the already-detected format as a parameter to avoid redundant work.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested refactor</summary>\n\n```diff\n-fn parse_spreadsheet_from_bytes(bytes: &[u8]) -> Result<Vec<ParsedBlock>, DomainError> {\n-    // Detect format from magic bytes\n-    let format = ExcelFormat::from_magic_bytes(bytes)\n-        .ok_or_else(|| DomainError::parse_error(\"Unrecognized Excel file format\"))?;\n+fn parse_spreadsheet_from_bytes(bytes: &[u8], format: ExcelFormat) -> Result<Vec<ParsedBlock>, DomainError> {\n```\n\nThen update the call site to pass the format.\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/Cargo.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`32-41`: **Consider inheriting `http` from workspace to maintain consistency.**\n\nThe workspace root defines `http` under `[workspace.dependencies]` at version `1.3`. If this version is acceptable for your dev-dependencies, use `http.workspace = true` to stay in sync with the workspace definition. Currently, the lint crate specifies `http = \"1.0\"` inline.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✅ Optional change</summary>\n\n```diff\n[dev-dependencies]\ndylint_testing.workspace = true\n-http = \"1.0\"\n+http.workspace = true\naxum.workspace = true\nanyhow.workspace = true\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`89-110`: **Consider adding expression and type path checks for defense-in-depth.**\n\nThe current implementation correctly prevents direct sqlx usage by catching imports via `use` statements and `extern crate` declarations. However, for defense-in-depth and clearer error reporting at usage sites, consider adding `check_expr()` and `check_ty()` methods to lint fully-qualified paths like `sqlx::query(...)` or `sqlx::Error` in expressions and type annotations.\n\nThis is an optional enhancement. The lint already prevents such code from compiling (Rust requires imports before using qualified paths), but catching these at lint-time would provide more direct feedback.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Implementation sketch</summary>\n\n```diff\n use rustc_ast::{Item, ItemKind};\n+use rustc_ast::{Expr, ExprKind, Ty, TyKind};\n use rustc_lint::{EarlyLintPass, LintContext};\n \n+fn path_starts_with_sqlx(path: &rustc_ast::Path) -> bool {\n+    path.segments\n+        .first()\n+        .map(|seg| seg.ident.name.as_str() == SQLX_PATTERN)\n+        .unwrap_or(false)\n+}\n+\n impl EarlyLintPass for De0706NoDirectSqlx {\n+    fn check_expr(&mut self, cx: &rustc_lint::EarlyContext<'_>, expr: &Expr) {\n+        if let ExprKind::Path(None, path) = &expr.kind {\n+            if path_starts_with_sqlx(path) {\n+                cx.span_lint(DE0706_NO_DIRECT_SQLX, expr.span, |diag| {\n+                    diag.primary_message(\"direct sqlx usage detected (DE0706)\");\n+                    diag.help(\"use Sea-ORM EntityTrait or SecORM abstractions instead\");\n+                    diag.note(\"sqlx bypasses security enforcement and architectural patterns\");\n+                });\n+            }\n+        }\n+    }\n+\n+    fn check_ty(&mut self, cx: &rustc_lint::EarlyContext<'_>, ty: &Ty) {\n+        if let TyKind::Path(None, path) = &ty.kind {\n+            if path_starts_with_sqlx(path) {\n+                cx.span_lint(DE0706_NO_DIRECT_SQLX, ty.span, |diag| {\n+                    diag.primary_message(\"direct sqlx usage detected (DE0706)\");\n+                    diag.help(\"use Sea-ORM EntityTrait or SecORM abstractions instead\");\n+                    diag.note(\"sqlx bypasses security enforcement and architectural patterns\");\n+                });\n+            }\n+        }\n+    }\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit/tests/domain_enforcement_demo.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`101-106`: **Consider a more idiomatic generic constraint.**\n\nThe `where ValidUser: DomainModel` bound on the trait is valid for demonstration but ties the trait to a concrete type. A more flexible pattern would use generics:\n\n```rust\npub trait Repository<T: DomainModel> {\n    fn find_by_id(&self, id: Uuid) -> Option<T>;\n}\n```\n\nThis is fine for demonstration purposes, but the current design won't generalize to other domain models without duplicating traits.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3740766395","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3740766395"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-02T17:01:03Z","commit_id":"dbce7ec4cd3d467293062543a2330fcdb0613496"},{"id":3741160808,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7e_ZVo","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 1**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/src/lib.rs`:\n- Around line 66-78: The lint misses bare or glob imports like `use http` or\n`use http::*` because use_tree_to_strings yields \"http\" but HTTP_PATTERNS only\nmatches \"http::\"; update check_use_item to detect either exact \"http\" or strings\nstarting with \"http::\" (or equivalently add \"http\" to HTTP_PATTERNS) when\niterating use_tree_to_strings, and still emit DE0308_NO_HTTP_IN_DOMAIN via the\nsame diag call; reference check_use_item, HTTP_PATTERNS, use_tree_to_strings,\nand DE0308_NO_HTTP_IN_DOMAIN when making the change.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de01_contract_layer/de0103_no_http_types_in_contract/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`9-9`: **Use the contract AST helper for path checks.**  \nGuidelines require `is_in_contract_module_ast()` for AST items; `is_in_contract_path` is the older span-only helper and can miss module-context nuances.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested change</summary>\n\n```diff\n-use lint_utils::is_in_contract_path;\n+use lint_utils::is_in_contract_module_ast;\n\n@@\n-        if matches!(item.kind, ItemKind::Use(_))\n-            && is_in_contract_path(cx.sess().source_map(), item.span)\n+        if matches!(item.kind, ItemKind::Use(_))\n+            && is_in_contract_module_ast(cx, item)\n         {\n             check_use_in_contract(cx, item);\n         }\n```\n</details>\n\nBased on learnings: “Use `is_in_contract_module_ast()` helper from `lint_utils` crate to check if an AST item is in a contract/ directory”.\n\n\nAlso applies to: 126-128\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3741160808","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3741160808"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-02T18:20:04Z","commit_id":"9512644e47c3939f82d399e90ab90103b94ddb18"},{"id":3741395321,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7fASl5","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 1**\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> Some comments are outside the diff and can’t be posted inline due to platform limitations.\n> \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>⚠️ Outside diff range comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>dylint_lints/de01_contract_layer/de0103_no_http_types_in_contract/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `123-128`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟡 Minor_\n> \n> **Use the contract-module AST helper instead of raw path checks.**  \n> The lint should rely on `is_in_contract_module_ast()` to align with the shared helper behavior for contract-module detection.  \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🔧 Proposed adjustment</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n> -use lint_utils::is_in_contract_path;\n> +use lint_utils::is_in_contract_module_ast;\n> ...\n> -        if matches!(item.kind, ItemKind::Use(_))\n> -            && is_in_contract_path(cx.sess().source_map(), item.span)\n> +        if matches!(item.kind, ItemKind::Use(_))\n> +            && is_in_contract_module_ast(cx, item)\n>          {\n>              check_use_in_contract(cx, item);\n>          }\n> ```\n> </details>\n> Based on learnings: Use `is_in_contract_module_ast()` helper from `lint_utils` crate to check if an AST item is in a contract/ directory.\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/src/lib.rs`:\n- Around line 200-205: The lint currently only checks is_in_domain_path in\nDe0308NoHttpInDomain::check_item and thus flags contract modules; update\ncheck_item to early-return when the AST item is in a contract module by calling\nlint_utils::is_in_contract_module_ast (e.g., if\nis_in_contract_module_ast(cx.sess().source_map(), item.span) { return; }) before\nperforming the domain-path check so contract/ items are skipped.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file_parser/src/infra/parsers/pptx_parser.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`66-66`: **Consider consistency with `XlsxParser` for filename fallback.**\n\nIn `parse_bytes`, when `filename_hint` is `None`, the `original_name` defaults to `\"unknown.pptx\"` (Line 77), but `title` and `original_filename` are not set (Lines 86-88). This differs from `XlsxParser` which always sets these fields.\n\nThis is minor since the fallback exists in `original_name`, but consider whether the builder should also receive the fallback filename for consistency:\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Optional: Consistent filename handling</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        if let Some(filename) = filename_hint {\n-            builder = builder.title(filename).original_filename(filename);\n-        }\n+        let filename = filename_hint.unwrap_or(\"unknown.pptx\");\n+        builder = builder.title(filename).original_filename(filename);\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 77-77, 86-88\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file_parser/src/infra/parsers/xlsx_parser.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`29-39`: **Infallible conversion silently defaults on unknown extensions.**\n\nThe `From<&str>` implementation silently returns `Xlsx` for unrecognized extensions. This differs from `from_filename()` which returns an error. Consider whether this silent fallback is intentional or if `TryFrom` would be more appropriate.\n\n\n\nIf infallible conversion is intentional, the current implementation is fine. Otherwise, consider using `TryFrom<&str>` to align with `from_filename()` behavior and make failures explicit.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`76-87`: **Consider adding contract module exclusion.**\n\nThe past review suggested adding `is_in_contract_module_ast` guard to skip contract/ definitions, similar to the modkit-db and hyperspot-server exclusions. If contract modules legitimately need sqlx for some reason (e.g., test fixtures), this guard would prevent false positives.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n+use lint_utils::{\n+    is_in_contract_module_ast,\n+    is_in_hyperspot_server_path,\n+    is_in_modkit_db_path,\n+    use_tree_to_strings,\n+};\n-use lint_utils::{is_in_hyperspot_server_path, is_in_modkit_db_path, use_tree_to_strings};\n\n impl EarlyLintPass for De0706NoDirectSqlx {\n     fn check_item(&mut self, cx: &rustc_lint::EarlyContext<'_>, item: &Item) {\n+        // Skip contract/ modules\n+        if is_in_contract_module_ast(cx, item) {\n+            return;\n+        }\n+\n         // Skip libs/modkit-db/ - this is the internal wrapper library\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3741395321","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3741395321"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-02T19:22:22Z","commit_id":"2f442908e8a9b3568c9c5e33f4a87e810dac9a07"},{"id":3743738397,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7fJOod","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 2**\n\n> [!CAUTION]\n> Some comments are outside the diff and can’t be posted inline due to platform limitations.\n> \n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>⚠️ Outside diff range comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>dylint_lints/de01_contract_layer/de0103_no_http_types_in_contract/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n> \n> `84-102`: _⚠️ Potential issue_ | _🟠 Major_\n> \n> **Nested use statements only check the first item, potentially missing violations.**\n> \n> The early `return` on line 96 exits after processing only the first nested item. For grouped imports like `use http::{Uri, StatusCode};`, the function returns `\"http::Uri\"` and never checks `StatusCode`, causing a false negative.\n> \n> \n> <details>\n> <summary>🐛 Proposed fix to check all nested items</summary>\n> \n> ```diff\n>  UseTreeKind::Nested { items, .. } => {\n>      let prefix = tree\n>          .prefix\n>          .segments\n>          .iter()\n>          .map(|seg| seg.ident.name.as_str())\n>          .collect::<Vec<_>>()\n>          .join(\"::\");\n> \n> +    let mut paths = Vec::new();\n>      for (nested_tree, _) in items {\n>          let nested_str = use_tree_to_string(nested_tree);\n>          if !nested_str.is_empty() {\n> -            return format!(\"{}::{}\", prefix, nested_str);\n> +            paths.push(format!(\"{}::{}\", prefix, nested_str));\n>          }\n>      }\n> -    prefix\n> +    if paths.is_empty() {\n> +        prefix\n> +    } else {\n> +        paths.join(\",\")  // Return all paths for pattern matching\n> +    }\n>  }\n> ```\n> \n> Alternatively, refactor to return `Vec<String>` and check each path against patterns in `check_use_in_contract`.\n> </details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n> \n> </blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/src/lib.rs`:\n- Around line 53-57: The current matching using HTTP_PATTERNS with starts_with\ncauses false positives (e.g., \"http_client\"); implement a helper fn\nmatches_http_pattern(path: &str) -> bool that iterates HTTP_PATTERNS and returns\ntrue only if path == pattern or path.starts_with(&format!(\"{}::\", pattern));\nthen replace the existing starts_with checks (where code examines path_str\nagainst HTTP_PATTERNS) with calls to matches_http_pattern(&path_str) (also\nupdate the other occurrences noted around lines 84-85) so that only exact\ncrate/module names or those followed by \"::\" are matched.\n\nIn `@modules/file_parser/src/module.rs`:\n- Around line 10-12: The XLSX and PPTX parser implementations were removed\ncausing e2e failures; restore XlsxParser and PptxParser implementations (create\nxlsx_parser.rs and pptx_parser.rs matching other parsers) and re-register them\nin the parsers vector inside module.rs where other parsers (DocxParser,\nPdfParser, etc.) are instantiated, and ensure the module's supported_extensions\nlist includes \"xlsx\" and \"pptx\"; alternatively, if the PR intent is to drop\nXLSX/PPTX support, delete the corresponding tests and any references to\nXlsxParser/PptxParser from module.rs and supported_extensions so the code and\ntests remain consistent.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>examples/modkit/users_info/users_info/src/domain/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-4`: **Consider linking the TODO to a tracking issue.**\n\nThe lint suppression with an explanatory TODO is a reasonable approach for rolling out DE0301 on existing code. However, TODOs without issue references tend to get lost. Consider adding a reference to a tracking issue (e.g., `// TODO(`#123`): DE0301 - ...`) to ensure this technical debt is properly tracked and prioritized.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple_user_settings/simple_user_settings/src/domain/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-5`: **Scope lint suppression to the smallest module(s).**\n\nThe crate-level `#![allow(de0301_no_infra_in_domain)]` disables the new lint for the entire domain module, which weakens enforcement and may hide future infra imports. Prefer scoping the allow to only the module(s) that still depend on `modkit_db`, and consider linking the TODO to a tracking issue.\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Example refactor (scope to a specific module)</summary>\n\n```diff\n-#![allow(de0301_no_infra_in_domain)]\n-\n pub mod error;\n pub mod fields;\n pub mod local_client;\n-pub mod repo;\n+#[allow(de0301_no_infra_in_domain)]\n+pub mod repo;\n pub mod service;\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit-macros/src/domain_model.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`173-212`: **Consider unwrapping parenthesized and grouped types.**\n\nThe catch-all `_ => Ok(())` allows `Type::Paren` (parenthesized types like `(http::StatusCode)`) and `Type::Group` (used by some macros) to bypass validation. While rare in practice, handling these would make the validation more robust.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested addition before the catch-all</summary>\n\n```diff\n         Type::TraitObject(trait_obj) => {\n             // ... existing code ...\n         }\n\n+        // Unwrap parenthesized types: (T) -> T\n+        Type::Paren(type_paren) => validate_field_type(&type_paren.elem, context),\n+\n+        // Unwrap grouped types (used by some macros)\n+        Type::Group(type_group) => validate_field_type(&type_group.elem, context),\n+\n         // Other type kinds are typically safe or will be caught by other means\n         _ => Ok(()),\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3743738397","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/253#pullrequestreview-3743738397"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/253"}},"submitted_at":"2026-02-03T08:17:02Z","commit_id":"e2e7dd7f22be7f7f45fe8a0450085ffd2f497a7c"},{"id":3781821951,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7hagX_","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com