(per-item status, error codes, and overall HTTP\nstatus), and include mapping rows for the existing categories\n(model_not_found/model_not_approved, validation_error, capability_not_supported,\nbudget_exceeded, rate_limited, hook_blocked, provider_error,\njob_not_found/expired) so each has code, status, response example and retry\nguidance.\n- Around line 220-241: Update S1.7 Text-to-Speech to explicitly define streaming\ntransport and message format: state whether streaming uses SSE (as in S1.2) or\nWebSocket (as in S1.15), specify chunk payload format (base64-encoded audio\nchunks vs binary frames) and encoding (e.g., PCM16/opus), and define the\nfinal/terminal message schema including metadata fields (duration, sample_rate,\nsample_format, codec, content_type) and whether stored_url is provided or null\nin streaming mode; add a short streaming sequence diagram or protocol block\nunder S1.7 and reference the exact section title \"S1.7 Text-to-Speech\" so\nreaders can find the new specs.\n- Around line 659-662: The \"S3.2 Embeddings Batching\" section must explicitly\ndefine the partial-failure response: state the chosen HTTP status (recommend 207\nMulti-Status or note if using 200 with mixed results), define a top-level\nresponse object with a \"results\" array where each element preserves request\nordering and contains an \"index\" (original request position) and either an\n\"embedding\" (on success) or an \"error\" object (on failure), reference RFC 9457\nfor the error object shape and required fields, and include a \"summary\" object\nwith succeeded/failed counts; update the prose in the S3.2 section to list these\nfield names (\"results\", \"index\", \"embedding\", \"error\", \"summary\") and explicitly\nstate ordering preservation and partial-success semantics.\n- Around line 10-20: Add a formal \"Normalized API Contract\" section to the PRD\nunder the LLM Gateway overview that explicitly defines the request and response\nschemas consumers must rely on (referencing the Gateway's promise to \"normalize\neverything to our contract\" and the \"Core capabilities\" list); include: a\nRequest format spec (message array structure, content types like\ntext/image/audio/document, parameters such as model, temperature, max_tokens,\nstreaming flag), a Response format spec (completion object structure, choices,\nfinal vs partial, provider metadata), a Streaming event format (SSE event types,\ndata payload for partial/completion/error), a Tool call/result schema\n(normalized tool invocation, arguments, status, result), a Usage metadata\nstructure (token counts, cost fields, latency/timestamps), and an Error response\nformat (structured error object following RFC-like fields: code, type, message,\ndetails); ensure all names match PRD terminology (\"LLM Gateway\", \"Core\ncapabilities\") so implementers can map provider-specific fields to these\ncanonical names.\n- Around line 148-168: Update the S1.4, S1.6, S1.8 and S1.13 scenarios in PRD.md\nto include FileStorage fetch error cases (503 Service Unavailable, 404 Not\nFound/invalid URL, 413 Payload Too Large, and generic network fetch failures)\nfor the GW->FS steps shown in the sequence diagrams; for each error list the\nconsumer-facing error code (map to existing provider_error where appropriate or\nintroduce a new media_fetch_failed code), the exact error payload shape returned\nby the Gateway, and explicit retry behavior including max retries and a backoff\npolicy (e.g., exponential backoff with jitter and specific retryable codes like\n503 and network failures but not 404/413). Reference the diagram call sites (the\nGW->FS fetch images/fetch media steps in S1.4/S1.6/S1.8/S1.13) so the reader can\nsee which interaction the errors apply to.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/llm_gateway/docs/PRD.md (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`323-342`: **Clarify schema source for structured output.**\n\nS1.11 shows `response_schema` in the request but doesn't indicate whether the schema can be a Type Registry reference (like tools in S1.10, line 40). \n\nFor consistency with the tool calling pattern, consider documenting whether:\n- Structured output schemas can reference Type Registry\n- Only inline schemas are supported\n- Both are supported\n\nThis would align with the Tools section pattern (lines 38-44).\n\n---\n\n`323-342`: **Specify validation retry policy for structured output.**\n\nLine 325 states \"On validation failure, Gateway retries with error context\" but doesn't specify:\n- Maximum number of retry attempts\n- What \"error context\" is included in the retry request\n- Final error handling when retries are exhausted\n- Whether retry behavior is configurable\n\nConsider documenting:\n- Default retry attempts (e.g., 1-2 retries)\n- Error context format sent to provider (validation errors as additional message?)\n- Final error response to consumer when validation fails after retries\n- Configuration options for retry behavior\n\n---\n\n`421-447`: **Consider documenting WebSocket message format.**\n\nS1.15 specifies WebSocket transport but doesn't document:\n- Message format: binary frames, JSON-wrapped, or other?\n- Audio encoding format: PCM, Opus, or provider-specific?\n- Session management: authentication method, keepalive/ping-pong?\n- Error handling: how are errors communicated during the session?\n\nWhile the high-level flow is clear, adding these details would help implementers understand the complete protocol.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/245#pullrequestreview-3682031449","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/245","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/245#pul