itter for transient 502/503) and a concrete\nmaximum retry attempts value (e.g., 3); reference the Gateway/FileStorage\nintegration and scenarios S1.4, S1.6, S1.8, S1.13 so readers can map each error\ncase to existing requirements.\n- Around line 389-418: Extend the \"S1.14 Async Jobs\" section to specify a\nbounded internal polling policy when \"Gateway polls internally\" (i.e., when a\nconsumer calls chat_completion(sync) against an async provider): define a\nMaximum polling duration (suggest 30s), a Polling interval (e.g., 500–1000ms),\nthe Timeout error code and payload (suggest HTTP 504 and an error id like\n\"provider_timeout\" with a clear message), and explicit Connection timeout\nenforcement to prevent resource exhaustion; update returned error messages to\nrecommend retrying with async=true and add a simple behavior matrix mapping\nConsumer mode (sync/async) × Provider mode (sync/async) → Gateway action\n(simulate job / poll internally / return job_id) including the timeout values\nand retry suggestion for each path, and reference the API actions\nchat_completion and get_job in the docs so implementers know where to enforce\nthese limits.\n- Around line 703-713: Update the \"Errors\" section in\nmodules/llm_gateway/docs/PRD.md by replacing the high-level bullet list with a\ncomprehensive error reference table (or appendix) that enumerates Error Code,\nHTTP Status, Response Structure, Triggered When, and Retry Guidance; include the\nexample entries (model_not_found 404, filestorage_unavailable 503,\nfile_not_found 404 with url, file_too_large 413 with maxBytes, provider_timeout\n504 with async retry guidance) plus FileStorage errors (503, 404, 413, 502),\nasync polling timeout errors, partial batch failure response shape, and all\ncategories currently listed in the Errors section (model not found/approved,\nvalidation, capability, budget, rate limit, hooks, provider errors/timeouts, job\nnot found/expired); standardize the response shapes (suggest adopting RFC 9457\nProblem Details) and ensure each table row includes retry guidance and example\nJSON response structures.\n- Around line 196-216: Update the S1.6 Speech-to-Text scenario to include\nFileStorage error handling: enumerate specific error cases (503 Service\nUnavailable, 404 Not Found, 413 Payload Too Large, and generic network\nfailures), state the Gateway's consumer-facing response for each case (HTTP\nstatus/code and user-friendly message), describe retry behavior (which errors\nare retriable and backoff policy) and reference the FileStorage error handling\nspecification from the Content Model; also add audio-specific limits (max file\nsize and supported formats) so the GW->FS: Fetch audio step and the overall S1.6\nflow clearly show how the gateway handles failures and limits.\n- Around line 220-241: Update S1.7 Text-to-Speech to explicitly specify the\nstreaming protocol and message format: state whether streaming uses SSE (like\nS1.2) or WebSocket (like S1.15), define chunk encoding (e.g., base64-encoded\naudio chunks or binary frames), and enumerate required audio parameters\n(sample_rate, channels, format e.g., PCM16/opus, codec). Add the final\nmessage/schema fields (duration, sample_rate, format, codec, stored_url which\nmust be a URL or null in pure streaming mode) and clarify storage behavior for\nstreaming vs non-streaming. Also include a short streaming sequence diagram\nshowing chunk flow (Consumer -> GW -> OB -> P -> GW chunked stream -> FS store\nfinal file) and a small example message/payload spec for both chunk and final\nmetadata in the S1.7 section.\n- Around line 659-661: Update the S3.2 Embeddings Batching section to explicitly\ndefine the partial-failure response: return HTTP 200 with a top-level \"results\"\narray preserving request order and an optional \"summary\" object; each array item\nmust include \"index\" (original request position) and either \"embedding\" and\n\"usage\" for successes or an \"error\" object for failures (error object fields\nshould follow RFC 9457: \"type\"/\"title\"/\"status\"/\"detail\"/\"instance\" or at\nminimum \"code\" and \"message\"); include an example JSON schema showing items like\n{ \"index\": 0, \"embedding\": [...], \"usage\": {...} } and { \"index\": 1, \"error\": {\n\"code\":\"...\", \"message\":\"...\" } } and document that \"summary\" provides counts\n(succeeded/failed) and that ordering is preserved by \"index\" to allow\nclient-side reconstruction.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/llm_gateway/docs/PRD.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`509-516`: **Consider adding example timeout values.**\n\nLines define timeout types but don't provide example values. While specific values may be configuration-dependent, providing recommended defaults would help implementers:\n\n- **TTFT timeout**: e.g., 30 seconds (varies by provider and model)\n- **Total generation timeout**: e.g., 5 minutes (varies by use case)\n\nAlso clarify:\n- Whether timeouts are configurable per-model or per-tenant\n- Default behavior if timeout configuration is missing\n\n---\n\n`323-341`: **Specify retry behavior for structured output validation failures.**\n\nLine 325 states \"Gateway retries with error context\" on validation failure, but retry behavior isn't specified:\n\n- **Maximum retry attempts**: e.g., 3 retries before returning validation error to consumer\n- **Backoff strategy**: Immediate retry, or exponential backoff?\n- **Error context format**: How is validation error communicated to provider in retry?\n- **Final error response**: If all retries fail, what error code and message is returned?\n\nConsider updating the sequence diagram to show the retry loop with decision points.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/245#pu