the\nsame diagnostic text and helper messages.\n\nIn\n`@dylint_lints/de07_security/de0705_no_prebuild_sql_queries/ui/bad_prebuild_queries.rs`:\n- Around line 8-11: The test case for query_user_by_name is incomplete because\nthe name parameter is unused; modify the function query_user_by_name to actually\nincorporate the name into the SQL string (e.g., via simple concatenation or\nformat! into _query) so it mirrors the unsafe pattern shown by query_user_by_id\nand will trigger DE0705; ensure you update the _query variable in\nquery_user_by_name to include the name parameter rather than leaving it as a\nconstant string.\n\nIn `@dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/Cargo.toml`:\n- Line 6: The Cargo.toml currently specifies an invalid Rust edition string\nedition = \"2024\"; update that value to a valid stable edition (for example\n\"2021\" or \"2018\") by replacing the edition = \"2024\" entry with a supported\nedition string so the crate builds with a valid Rust edition.\n\nIn `@dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/README.md`:\n- Line 3: The README uses h1 then jumps to h3 headers; update the heading\nhierarchy by changing the h3 headings \"### What it does\", \"### Example\", \"###\nConfiguration\", and \"### See Also\" to h2 (\"## ...\") so they follow the h1\ncorrectly and maintain consistent section levels throughout the file.\n\nIn `@dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/rust-toolchain`:\n- Line 1: The rust-toolchain file for de0706 is out of sync: replace the\nplain-text pin \"nightly-2025-05-01\" with the workspace-standard toolchain and\nformat used by the other dylint lints (use \"nightly-2025-09-18\" and convert the\nfile to TOML with a [toolchain] table and the same components/targets entries as\nthe other lint rust-toolchain files); ensure the file matches the other lint\ntoolchain files exactly in structure (including any components like\nrustfmt/clippy) so the lint uses the same nightly and format as the rest of the\nworkspace.\n\nIn `@dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/src/lib.rs`:\n- Around line 122-133: The pattern matching for ItemKind::ExternCrate is wrong;\nchange the match from ItemKind::ExternCrate(name, _ident) to\nItemKind::ExternCrate(extern_crate_item) and then read the crate name from the\nItemExternCrate struct (e.g., extern_crate_item.ident or the appropriate field)\nto compare against SQLX_PATTERN; keep the lint invocation using\nDE0706_NO_DIRECT_SQLX and item.span and call cx.span_lint with the same messages\nif the extracted name equals SQLX_PATTERN.\n\nIn `@dylint_lints/de11_documentation/de1107_public_apis_doc_comments/README.md`:\n- Line 3: Update the Markdown heading hierarchy by changing the H3 headings to\nH2: replace \"### What it does\", \"### Why is this bad?\", \"### Example\", \"###\nConfiguration\", and \"### See Also\" with \"## What it does\", \"## Why is this\nbad?\", \"## Example\", \"## Configuration\", and \"## See Also\" so the document uses\nH2 directly under the H1 for consistent structure.\n\nIn\n`@dylint_lints/de11_documentation/de1107_public_apis_doc_comments/rust-toolchain`:\n- Around line 1-3: Update the three inconsistent rust-toolchain files to match\nthe canonical format and version: replace bare-version files\nde0110_no_schema_for_on_gts_structs/rust-toolchain and\nde0902_no_schema_for_on_gts_structs/rust-toolchain with the [toolchain] section\nformat and set channel = \"nightly-2025-09-18\" and components =\n[\"llvm-tools-preview\", \"rustc-dev\"], and change\nde0706_no_direct_sqlx/rust-toolchain to use the same [toolchain] section and\nchannel \"nightly-2025-09-18\" (with the same components) so all lint crates are\nstandardized.\n\nIn\n`@examples/plugin-modules/tenant_resolver/tenant_resolver-gw/src/domain/service.rs`:\n- Around line 99-101: Service methods (e.g., get_root_tenant) currently use the\nundefined type SecurityCtx and import use modkit_security::SecurityCtx; change\nall service method signatures that accept &SecurityCtx (including\nget_root_tenant and the other methods around lines 108, 123, 139) to accept\n&SecurityContext instead, update the import to use\nmodkit_security::SecurityContext, and adjust any parameter names/uses\naccordingly so the service trait and implementations match callers (handlers.rs,\nlocal_client.rs, tenant_resolver_sdk/api.rs) that pass &SecurityContext.\n\nIn `@modules/system/api_gateway/src/module.rs`:\n- Around line 221-232: The call to AuthPolicyLayer::new is passing four\narguments but the signature expects only three; remove the intermediate\nscope_builder argument so the call becomes AuthPolicyLayer::new(validator,\nauthorizer, policy). Locate where auth_state.scope_builder is cloned and used in\nthe router.layer call and drop that variable from the call, keeping\nauth_state.validator, auth_state.authorizer, and the Arc route_policy (policy)\nas the three arguments.\n\nIn `@modules/system/types-registry/types-registry/src/domain/local_client.rs`:\n- Around line 30-51: The implementation implements a non-existent trait\nTypesRegistryApi and includes unused SecurityCtx parameters; update\nTypesRegistryLocalClient to implement the SDK trait TypesRegistryClient and\nremove the `_ctx: &SecurityCtx` parameter from the register, list, and get\nmethod signatures so they match the TypesRegistryClient trait (keep method names\nregister, list, get and keep existing calls to self.service.register/list/get\nbut adapt their signatures to the trait).\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0301_no_infra_in_domain/.cargo/config.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-6`: **Linker config is required per-crate for dylint builds.**\n\nAddressing the previous review concern: even though this crate is a workspace member, each dylint lint crate requires its own `.cargo/config.toml` with the `dylint-link` linker configuration. This is because cargo applies config hierarchically and the linker setting must be present in the lint crate's directory for `cargo dylint` to build it correctly.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (20)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0703_no_hardcoded_secrets/.cargo/config.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-6`: **LGTM — standard dylint linker configuration.**\n\nThe configuration correctly sets `dylint-link` as the linker for all targets, which is required for dylint lints. The comment about the Rust 1.74.0+ alternative is helpful.\n\nMinor nit: Line 1 has a leading blank line that could be removed for consistency.\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Optional: remove leading blank line</summary>\n\n```diff\n-\n [target.'cfg(all())']\n rustflags = [\"-C\", \"linker=dylint-link\"]\n # For Rust versions 1.74.0 and onward, the following alternative can be used\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/ui/bad_direct_sqlx.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-14`: **Test coverage looks good; consider adding glob import case.**\n\nThe test file correctly demonstrates bad patterns with proper comment annotations. The `use sqlx;` and `use sqlx::Error;` cases cover basic import scenarios.\n\nConsider adding a glob import test case for more comprehensive coverage:\n\n```rust\n// Should trigger DE0706 - sqlx glob\nuse sqlx::*;\n```\n\nBased on the relevant code snippet, `libs/modkit-db/src/advisory_locks.rs` uses `sqlx::query_scalar` directly. Verify that the lint has appropriate scoped exclusions for infrastructure modules that legitimately need direct sqlx access.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de04_infrastructure/de0407_no_hardcoded_connection_strings/README.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-5`: **Consider fixing heading hierarchy for markdown consistency.**\n\nThe document jumps from `#` (h1) to `###` (h3), skipping h2. This triggers MD001 (heading-increment).\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n # DE0407: No Hardcoded Connection Strings\n\n-### What it does\n+## What it does\n```\n\nApply the same change to other `###` headings (`Why is this bad?`, `Example`, `Configuration`, `See Also`).\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0705_no_prebuild_sql_queries/README.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-5`: **Consider fixing heading hierarchy for markdown consistency.**\n\nSame as DE0407 README — the document jumps from `#` (h1) to `###` (h3).\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n # DE0705: No Prebuilt SQL Queries\n\n-### What it does\n+## What it does\n```\n\nApply to all `###` headings.\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`36-77`: The project already documents safe raw SQL usage in `/docs/SECURE-ORM.md` (lines 506-533), which covers the `insecure-escape` feature flag approach. This pattern is intentionally restricted to migrations and administrative tools. Since the linting rule README focuses on discouraging raw SQL in application code, a reference to the official SECURE-ORM documentation might be helpful but is not essential. If added, it should clarify that raw SQL is limited to specific scenarios (migrations) and direct readers to SECURE-ORM.md for the complete pattern with proper feature flag guards.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/lint_utils/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`32-47`: **New path helpers look good overall, with a minor robustness concern.**\n\nThe doc comments properly explain the purpose of these exclusions. However, the shorter fallback patterns (`modkit-db/src/` and `hyperspot-server/src/`) could potentially match unintended paths if a similarly-named directory exists elsewhere in the codebase.\n\nConsider whether the shorter patterns are necessary, or if they should be more specific:\n\n```diff\n pub fn is_in_modkit_db_path(source_map: &SourceMap, span: Span) -> bool {\n-    // Try both with and without leading slash for compatibility\n-    check_span_path(source_map, span, \"/libs/modkit-db/\") \n+    check_span_path(source_map, span, \"/libs/modkit-db/\")\n         || check_span_path(source_map, span, \"libs/modkit-db/\")\n-        || check_span_path(source_map, span, \"modkit-db/src/\")\n }\n```\n\nIf the short patterns are needed for specific test scenarios, that's fine—just wanted to flag the potential for false positives.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de11_documentation/de1107_public_apis_doc_comments/ui/good_docs.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`5-21`: **Annotation placement could be more precise.**\n\nThe `test_comment_annotations_match_stderr` function checks the line immediately after the annotation comment. Currently, the doc comments sit between the annotations and the item declarations:\n\n```\n// Should not trigger DE1107 - doc comments  ← annotation (line 5)\n/// A user entity...                         ← doc comment (line 6) - this is what gets checked\npub struct User {                            ← item (line 7) - this is what the lint would report on\n```\n\nSince `good_docs.rs` produces no errors, the test passes. However, for stricter validation, consider placing annotations immediately before items:\n\n```rust\n/// A user entity representing a registered user.\n// Should not trigger DE1107 - doc comments\npub struct User {\n```\n\nThis is a minor precision improvement—the current tests work correctly.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0706_no_direct_sqlx/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`61-74`: **`use_tree_to_string` only returns the first nested path for nested imports.**\n\nFor `use sqlx::{Error, Pool};`, this function returns only `sqlx::Error` (the first nested item), not all paths. While this is sufficient for detecting sqlx usage, the diagnostic message may be incomplete for multi-item imports.\n\nConsider whether this is the intended behavior or if all nested paths should be reported.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0301_no_infra_in_domain/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`91-104`: **Nested use tree only returns first item, but prefix matching saves correctness.**\n\nThe loop returns on the first nested item found, so `use sea_orm::{entity, query}` would only return `\"sea_orm::entity\"`. However, since `check_use_in_domain` uses `contains()` matching against the prefix (e.g., `\"sea_orm\"`), infra patterns are still caught. Consider refactoring to return all paths for more accurate diagnostics if you want to report the specific problematic import.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Optional: Return all nested paths for precise diagnostics</summary>\n\n```diff\n-fn use_tree_to_string(tree: &UseTree) -> String {\n+fn use_tree_to_strings(tree: &UseTree) -> Vec<String> {\n     match &tree.kind {\n         UseTreeKind::Simple(..) | UseTreeKind::Glob => {\n-            tree.prefix.segments.iter()\n+            vec![tree.prefix.segments.iter()\n                 .map(|seg| seg.ident.name.as_str())\n                 .collect::<Vec<_>>()\n-                .join(\"::\")\n+                .join(\"::\")]\n         }\n         UseTreeKind::Nested { items, .. } => {\n             let prefix = tree.prefix.segments.iter()\n                 .map(|seg| seg.ident.name.as_str())\n                 .collect::<Vec<_>>()\n                 .join(\"::\");\n             \n+            let mut paths = Vec::new();\n             for (nested_tree, _) in items {\n-                let nested_str = use_tree_to_string(nested_tree);\n-                if !nested_str.is_empty() {\n-                    return format!(\"{}::{}\", prefix, nested_str);\n+                for nested_str in use_tree_to_strings(nested_tree) {\n+                    if !nested_str.is_empty() {\n+                        paths.push(format!(\"{}::{}\", prefix, nested_str));\n+                    }\n                 }\n             }\n-            prefix\n+            if paths.is_empty() { vec![prefix] } else { paths }\n         }\n     }\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0705_no_prebuild_sql_queries/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`126-140`: **Fragile path detection using Debug output.**\n\nUsing `format!(\"{:?}\", qpath)` and string matching on the debug representation is brittle—the Debug impl for `QPath` may change across rustc versions. Consider matching path segments directly or using clippy_utils path helpers.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ More robust path matching</summary>\n\n```diff\n fn extract_format_string<'tcx>(cx: &LateContext<'tcx>, expr: &'tcx Expr<'tcx>) -> Option<String> {\n-    // Try to extract the format string from format_args! expansion\n-    // This is complex due to macro expansion, so we do a simplified check\n     if let ExprKind::Call(func, args) = &expr.kind {\n         if let ExprKind::Path(qpath) = &func.kind {\n-            let path_str = format!(\"{:?}\", qpath);\n-            if path_str.contains(\"format_args\") {\n+            // Match path segments directly instead of debug output\n+            if let rustc_hir::QPath::Resolved(_, path) = qpath {\n+                let is_format_args = path.segments.iter()\n+                    .any(|seg| seg.ident.name.as_str().contains(\"format_args\"));\n+                if is_format_args {\n                 if let Some(first_arg) = args.first() {\n                     return extract_string_literal(cx, first_arg);\n                 }\n+                }\n             }\n         }\n     }\n     None\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0301_no_infra_in_domain/ui/mixed_imports_0301.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`6-13`: **Comment wording is slightly confusing.**\n\nThe comments on lines 6 and 12 say \"Should not trigger DE0301 - infra in domain\" but these imports are NOT infrastructure dependencies. The phrase \"infra in domain\" after the dash is misleading since it suggests they are infra imports.\n\nConsider clarifying:\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested comment clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-// Should not trigger DE0301 - infra in domain\n+// Should not trigger DE0301 - std library is allowed\n use std::collections::HashMap;\n\n // Should trigger DE0301 - infra in domain\n use sea_orm::DatabaseConnection;\n\n-// Should not trigger DE0301 - infra in domain\n+// Should not trigger DE0301 - error handling crate is allowed\n use thiserror::Error;\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/ui/good_domain_0308.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`6-10`: **Same comment wording inconsistency as in mixed_imports_0301.rs.**\n\nThe comments say \"Should not trigger DE0308 - HTTP in domain\" but these imports are NOT HTTP-related. The phrase after the dash is misleading.\n\nConsider:\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-// Should not trigger DE0308 - HTTP in domain\n+// Should not trigger DE0308 - std library allowed\n use std::sync::Arc;\n\n-// Should not trigger DE0308 - HTTP in domain\n+// Should not trigger DE0308 - error handling crate allowed\n use anyhow::Result;\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de04_infrastructure/de0409_no_secrets_in_migrations/ui/good_migration.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`4-17`: **Fix misleading comment wording.**\n\nThe comments say \"Should not trigger DE0409 - secret in migration\" which is contradictory. The phrase \"secret in migration\" implies there IS a secret, but these are safe patterns. Consider rewording to clarify these are safe patterns without secrets.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested comment clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-// Should not trigger DE0409 - secret in migration\n+// Should not trigger DE0409 - safe migration pattern (no secrets)\n fn create_table() {\n     let _sql = \"CREATE TABLE users (id SERIAL PRIMARY KEY, name VARCHAR(255))\";\n }\n\n-// Should not trigger DE0409 - secret in migration\n+// Should not trigger DE0409 - safe migration pattern (no secrets)\n fn add_column() {\n     let _sql = \"ALTER TABLE users ADD COLUMN email VARCHAR(255)\";\n }\n\n-// Should not trigger DE0409 - secret in migration\n+// Should not trigger DE0409 - safe migration pattern (no secrets)\n fn create_index() {\n     let _sql = \"CREATE INDEX idx_users_email ON users(email)\";\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de04_infrastructure/de0409_no_secrets_in_migrations/README.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-3`: **Fix heading hierarchy for markdown consistency.**\n\nPer markdownlint MD001, headings should only increment by one level at a time. After the h1 (`#`), the next heading should be h2 (`##`), not h3 (`###`).\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n # DE0409: No Secrets in Migrations\n\n-### What it does\n+## What it does\n```\n\nApply the same change to other `###` headings throughout the file (lines 7, 15, 63, 71).\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0301_no_infra_in_domain/README.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-3`: **Fix heading hierarchy for markdown consistency.**\n\nSame issue as DE0409 README - headings should increment by one level at a time. Change `###` to `##` throughout.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n # DE0301: No Infrastructure in Domain\n\n-### What it does\n+## What it does\n```\n\nApply to all `###` headings (lines 7, 15, 46, 54).\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de04_infrastructure/de0407_no_hardcoded_connection_strings/ui/good_config_usage_0407.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`3-24`: **Fix misleading comment wording.**\n\nSame issue as `good_migration.rs` - the comments say \"Should not trigger DE0407 - hardcoded connection\" which implies there IS a hardcoded connection. Consider rewording for clarity.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested comment clarification</summary>\n\n```diff\n-// Should not trigger DE0407 - hardcoded connection\n+// Should not trigger DE0407 - uses environment variables\n fn setup_database() -> Result<(), Box<dyn std::error::Error>> {\n     // Load from environment\n     let _pg_url = std::env::var(\"DATABASE_URL\")?;\n\n     // Load from environment with fallback\n     let _redis_url = std::env::var(\"REDIS_URL\").unwrap_or_else(|_| String::new());\n\n     Ok(())\n }\n\n-// Should not trigger DE0407 - hardcoded connection\n+// Should not trigger DE0407 - config key name, not a connection string\n fn get_config_key() -> &'static str {\n     // Regular strings are fine\n     \"database_url\"\n }\n\n-// Should not trigger DE0407 - hardcoded connection\n+// Should not trigger DE0407 - log message, not a connection string\n fn log_message() {\n     // Messages containing URL-like text but not connection strings\n     let _msg = \"Please set POSTGRES_URL environment variable\";\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0301_no_infra_in_domain/ui/good_domain_0301.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`7-14`: **Misleading comment text in test annotations.**\n\nThe comments say \"Should not trigger DE0301 - infra in domain\" but the phrase \"infra in domain\" reads as if infrastructure IS present. For clarity and consistency with other UI tests, consider rewording to describe what the import actually is:\n\n```diff\n-// Should not trigger DE0301 - infra in domain\n+// Should not trigger DE0301 - standard library import\n use std::sync::Arc;\n\n-// Should not trigger DE0301 - infra in domain\n+// Should not trigger DE0301 - domain-appropriate dependency\n use uuid::Uuid;\n\n-// Should not trigger DE0301 - infra in domain\n+// Should not trigger DE0301 - domain-appropriate dependency\n use anyhow::Result;\n```\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de03_domain_layer/de0308_no_http_in_domain/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`53-65`: **HTTP_PATTERNS contains redundant entries.**\n\nThe pattern `\"http::\"` will match all of `\"http::StatusCode\"`, `\"http::Method\"`, etc. The more specific entries are redundant when using `contains()` matching on line 99.\n\nConsider either:\n1. Keeping only the namespace prefixes (`\"http::\"`, `\"axum::http\"`, etc.)\n2. Or using exact prefix matching if you want to allow some `http::` items while blocking others\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Simplified patterns (if all http:: items should be blocked)</summary>\n\n```diff\n const HTTP_PATTERNS: &[&str] = &[\n     \"http::\",\n-    \"http::StatusCode\",\n-    \"http::Method\",\n-    \"http::HeaderMap\",\n-    \"http::Request\",\n-    \"http::Response\",\n     \"axum::http\",\n-    \"hyper::StatusCode\",\n-    \"hyper::Method\",\n+    \"hyper::\",\n     \"actix_web::http\",\n     \"reqwest::StatusCode\",\n ];\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>dylint_lints/de07_security/de0703_no_hardcoded_secrets/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`79-85`: **Consider avoiding repeated allocations in the SQL keyword check.**\n\nThe `format!(\" {}\", keyword)` inside the loop creates a new `String` allocation for each keyword on every call. This can be simplified to avoid allocations.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Suggested optimization</summary>\n\n```diff\n     // Skip SQL queries\n     let sql_keywords = [\"select \", \"insert \", \"update \", \"delete \", \"create \", \"alter \", \"drop \"];\n     for keyword in &sql_keywords {\n-        if lower.starts_with(keyword) || lower.contains(&format!(\" {}\", keyword)) {\n+        if lower.starts_with(keyword) || lower.contains(keyword) {\n             return false;\n         }\n     }\n```\n\nNote: Since `keyword` already has a trailing space (e.g., `\"select \"`), checking `lower.contains(keyword)` will match ` select ` patterns. If you need to match keywords not at word boundaries, this simplification still provides reasonable coverage.\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/system/api_gateway/tests/auth_disabled_root_context.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`124-159`: **Consider: Test name suggests comparison but only tests root context.**\n\nThe test `test_auth_disabled_vs_normal_scope` only tests the root context case. If the intent was to compare against a normal (non-root) scope, consider adding a second scenario or renaming to `test_auth_disabled_root_scope_properties`.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/224#pullrequestreview-3666862054","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/224","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/224#pullrequestreview-3666862054"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/224"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-15T17:49:52Z","commit_id":"072f9b8ead8d2f587f07e787b943da0164a19e24"}]},{"source_pr_number":223,"source_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223","source_state":"closed","source_author":"alizid10","target_issue_number":null,"title":"[PR #223] Implementation of temp settings module","status":"exported","mirrored_at":null,"comments_status":"none","comments_mirrored":0,"pr_head_ref":"feat/settings","pr_head_sha":"f154644beca8697855955fa21f5b6fc95b5eb0bd","pr_base_ref":"main","pr_head_owner":"alizid10","pr_from_fork":1,"source_data":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223","id":3159688092,"node_id":"PR_kwDOPaLyBs