due to timeout of 90000ms. You can increase the timeout in your CodeRabbit configuration to a maximum of 15 minutes (900000ms). (7)</summary>\n\n* GitHub Check: Test Suite (ubuntu-latest, rust=stable)\n* GitHub Check: Test Suite (macos-latest, rust=stable)\n* GitHub Check: Test Suite (windows-latest, rust=stable)\n* GitHub Check: Run Dylint Lints\n* GitHub Check: UI macro tests (trybuild)\n* GitHub Check: users_info integration (Postgres)\n* GitHub Check: Code Coverage (cargo-llvm-cov)\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔇 Additional comments (76)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>testing/docker/hyperspot.Dockerfile (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`22-22`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe addition of the proto directory copy is appropriate and correctly placed in the builder stage. Since `protobuf-compiler` is installed for prost-build (lines 5-8), the proto definitions need to be available during the build for code generation. Correctly not copied to the runtime stage since it's only needed at build time.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>guidelines/DNA (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **Fix invalid submodule pointer: commit reference does not exist in guidelines/DNA repository.**\n\nThe submodule pointer for `guidelines/DNA` is set to commit `33e143ef7659f0bd4586c965b8d3d2763eefd31f`, but this commit does not exist in the hypernetix/DNA repository. This will cause submodule checkout to fail. Update the submodule pointer to a valid commit that exists in the repository, or ensure the correct commit is available in the upstream DNA repository.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>⛔ Skipped due to learnings</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: modules/system/types-registry/openspec/AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:55.263Z\nLearning: Always consult `guidelines/DNA/` for REST API design patterns and architectural principles\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/__init__.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **LGTM!**\n\nStandard Python package marker file for the settings E2E tests module.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>Cargo.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`52-53`: **LGTM!**\n\nNew workspace members for the settings module correctly registered. The crate naming (`simple-user-settings*`) appropriately indicates the temporary nature of this module.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/README.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-70`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe README provides comprehensive documentation for the E2E tests, including clear descriptions of test files, execution instructions, and coverage summary.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit/src/contracts.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`88-88`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe simplified return type leverages the public re-export at line 5, making the trait definition cleaner while maintaining the same semantic meaning.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/gts/errors.json (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`2-6`: **Verify if 404 error is used given lazy creation behavior.**\n\nThe E2E test README indicates GET endpoint uses \"lazy creation\" (returns defaults when settings don't exist). If settings are always lazily created, this 404 error may be unused. Consider if this error code is needed or if it's reserved for future use cases.\n\n---\n\n`7-16`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe 422 Validation Error and 500 Internal Database Error definitions are appropriate with correct HTTP status codes.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/errors.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-10`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean use of the `declare_errors!` macro to generate strongly-typed errors from the JSON catalog. The documentation correctly identifies the source of truth.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/fields.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-6`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean and idiomatic approach for grouping related field name constants. The unit struct pattern works well for namespacing these identifiers.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit-auth/src/axum_ext.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`46-66`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe `AuthzContext` extractor correctly mirrors the existing `Authz` pattern. The middleware already inserts `SecurityContext` into request extensions for all authentication paths (lines 180, 217, 241, 247, 253), so this extractor will work as expected.\n\nMinor note: The existing `Authz` extractor has a `// TODO: drop this clone` comment at line 38. Consider adding the same comment at line 60 for consistency if the clone optimization is planned for both extractors.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/errors.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-34`: **LGTM!**\n\nWell-structured error type following `thiserror` conventions. Good use of `#[must_use]` on constructors and ergonomic `impl Into<String>` for the validation message. The opaque `Internal` variant is appropriate for SDK-facing errors to avoid leaking implementation details.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/Cargo.toml (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-36`: **LGTM overall!**\n\nThe Cargo.toml is well-structured and follows workspace conventions. Dependency declarations use appropriate workspace references and path-based internal crates.\n\n---\n\n`32-32`: **The `insecure-escape` feature is intentionally required and appropriately used.**\n\nThe feature enables `db.sea()` access in the `migrate()` method to run database schema migrations. Database migrations require direct admin-level access and cannot operate within tenant-scoped connections. The safety mechanisms are in place: the feature is opt-in (OFF by default) and requires `INSECURE_ESCAPE_ALLOW=1` at runtime. This usage is legitimate.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/repo.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-24`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe repository trait is well-designed:\n- Properly uses `async_trait` with `Send + Sync` bounds\n- Clear separation between full upsert and patch operations\n- `anyhow::Result` is appropriate for infrastructure-level error handling\n- Method signatures align well with the SeaORM implementation\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/models.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-22`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean, transport-agnostic data models with appropriate derives. The separation between full entity (`SimpleUserSettings`) and partial update (`SimpleUserSettingsPatch`) follows good API design patterns.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/error.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`24-34`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe error conversion mapping is sensible - `Database` errors are correctly mapped to `internal()` to avoid leaking implementation details, while `Validation` errors preserve the field context.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/api.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`27-52`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe API trait is well-designed:\n- Clear documentation with usage example for `ClientHub` integration\n- Proper `Send + Sync` bounds for async usage\n- Good distinction between full update (POST semantics) and partial update (PATCH semantics)\n- Doc comments clearly explain behavior for missing records\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/Cargo.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`12-16`: **Remove the unused `time` dependency from Cargo.toml.**\n\nThe `time` crate is declared as a dependency but is not used anywhere in the codebase. The models (`SimpleUserSettings` and `SimpleUserSettingsPatch`) contain no timestamp fields, and no imports or references to `time` exist in the source code.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/entity.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-20`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe entity definition is well-structured:\n- Composite primary key `(tenant_id, user_id)` is appropriate for per-user settings scoped by tenant.\n- The `#[secure]` attribute correctly configures tenant and resource column scoping.\n- `auto_increment = false` is correctly set for UUID primary keys.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/mapper.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-14`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean and correct implementation of the entity-to-domain model conversion. All fields are properly mapped.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/mapper_test.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-44`: **LGTM!**\n\nGood test coverage for the mapper including both the normal case and empty string edge case. The tests are well-structured and verify all field mappings.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-22`: **LGTM!**\n\nWell-organized SDK entry point with:\n- Clear crate-level documentation and usage example\n- Appropriate `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` for safety\n- Clean re-exports for ergonomic API consumption\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/sea_orm_repo.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`24-38`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe `find_by_user` method correctly uses `AccessScope::both` to scope the query by both tenant and user, ensuring proper data isolation.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service.rs (5)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-20`: **LGTM on `ServiceConfig` with sensible defaults.**\n\nThe configuration structure is clean and the default max length of 100 is reasonable for theme/language fields.\n\n---\n\n`22-30`: **Clean dependency injection pattern.**\n\nThe `Service` struct properly uses `Arc<dyn SettingsRepository>` for the repository, enabling easy testing via mock implementations and runtime flexibility.\n\n---\n\n`32-49`: **LGTM on `get_settings` with default fallback.**\n\nThe method correctly returns existing settings or constructs a default `SimpleUserSettings` using context IDs with empty strings for theme/language.\n\n---\n\n`51-62`: **LGTM on `update_settings` with validation before persistence.**\n\nGood pattern: validate both fields upfront before calling the repository, ensuring invalid data never reaches storage.\n\n---\n\n`64-78`: **LGTM on `patch_settings` with conditional validation.**\n\nCorrectly validates only the fields present in the patch, allowing partial updates without requiring both fields.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service_test.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`11-53`: **Well-structured mock repository for testing.**\n\nThe `MockRepository` cleanly implements the `SettingsRepository` trait with configurable results, enabling focused unit testing of the service logic without database dependencies.\n\n---\n\n`59-82`: **Comprehensive test coverage for service behaviors.**\n\nGood coverage of:\n- Retrieving existing settings\n- Default value fallback\n- Full update with validation\n- Partial patch updates\n- Validation error paths\n- Empty patch handling\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 108-133, 135-166, 168-201, 203-237, 239-270\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-7`: **LGTM on module organization.**\n\nClean separation of domain concerns with `error`, `fields`, `repo`, and `service` modules. Test module properly gated with `#[cfg(test)]`.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/local_client.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-19`: **Clean adapter construction with `#[must_use]`.**\n\nGood use of `#[must_use]` to prevent accidental discard of the constructed client.\n\n---\n\n`21-51`: **LGTM on `SimpleUserSettingsApi` implementation.**\n\nThe `LocalClient` correctly implements the SDK's API trait by delegating to the domain service and converting errors via `Into::into`. This provides a clean boundary between the domain layer and the public API contract.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **LGTM on infra module structure.**\n\nStandard module exposure for the infrastructure layer, making the storage subsystem accessible.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **LGTM!**\n\nStandard module re-export for exposing the REST API surface.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>apps/hyperspot-server/src/registered_modules.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`12-12`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe module registration follows the established pattern. Note that unlike `users_info` and other example modules, this is unconditionally linked. This is appropriate if the settings module should always be available in production builds.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>apps/hyperspot-server/Cargo.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`44-44`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe local dependency is correctly declared with a relative path. The placement under \"user modules\" is appropriate for this non-system module.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/AGENTS.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-17`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe OpenSpec guidance file follows the established pattern from the repository's conventions. The managed block markers ensure the content can be refreshed by the `openspec update` tooling. Based on