ellent onboarding context for contributors working on the settings module.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_get.py (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`6-39`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe test properly validates the lazy creation behavior described in the design document, checking that GET returns defaults with the expected structure when no settings exist. The authentication skip logic correctly handles the case when `auth_headers` is an empty dict from the fixture.\n\n---\n\n`42-75`: **Consider adding a note about test isolation assumptions.**\n\nThe idempotency test assumes settings aren't modified between the two GET calls. This is valid within the single test function, but consider adding a brief comment noting this assumption for clarity, especially if test parallelization is introduced later.\n\n---\n\n`78-93`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe test appropriately handles multiple valid authentication scenarios (200 for permissive configs, 401/403 for auth-required configs), making it resilient across different deployment environments.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/error.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`22-29`: **Good security practice—internal details are not leaked.**\n\nThe database error handling appropriately logs the full error for debugging while returning a generic message to clients, preventing information disclosure.\n\n---\n\n`33-38`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe `From` implementation enables ergonomic error propagation with `?` in handlers, using a sensible default instance path.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/openspec/AGENTS.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-456`: **LGTM!**\n\nThis is a comprehensive OpenSpec guidance document for AI assistants. The structure is clear with a well-organized workflow (creating, implementing, archiving changes), CLI reference, and troubleshooting tips.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-7`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean module organization with proper separation of concerns and test module gated behind `#[cfg(test)]`.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/routes.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`54-112`: **LGTM!**\n\nRoute definitions are well-structured with proper HTTP semantics:\n- GET for retrieval (read access)\n- POST for full update (write access)  \n- PATCH for partial update (write access)\n\nOpenAPI integration includes appropriate request/response schemas and error codes (400, 401, 403, 422, 500).\n\n---\n\n`114-116`: The handlers successfully extract `Extension<Arc<Service>>` despite the layer being applied after route registration. In Axum, `router.layer()` applies to all routes existing on the router at the time of the call. Since `OperationBuilder::register()` returns a router with routes already added, the Extension layer correctly propagates to all three endpoints (GET, POST, PATCH). The current ordering is correct and no changes are needed.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/migrations/initial_001.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`8-32`: **LGTM!**\n\nMigration is well-structured with:\n- Appropriate composite primary key `(TenantId, UserId)` for multi-tenant user settings\n- Idempotent `if_not_exists()` clause\n- Clean `down()` migration for rollback\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/openspec/changes/add-settings-module-rust/specs/user-settings/spec.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`23-31`: **Verify JSON field naming convention consistency.**\n\nLine 26 specifies \"camelCase JSON field naming\" for responses, but the DTO tests in `dto_test.rs` (lines 79-80) verify `snake_case` for `user_id` and `tenant_id` fields. This may be an intentional deviation or an inconsistency between spec and implementation.\n\n\nPlease confirm whether the implementation should use camelCase (as specified) or snake_case (as tested). If snake_case is intentional, update the spec accordingly.\n\n---\n\n`153-156`: **Clarify SDK serde requirement.**\n\nThe spec states SDK models \"do not include `serde` or other HTTP-specific derives\" for transport agnosticism. However, the SDK types are re-exported and used in REST DTOs which require serialization. Verify this constraint is achievable given the current design where `SimpleUserSettings` needs to be converted to/from JSON.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-23`: **Clean module organization with appropriate visibility.**\n\nGood use of `#[doc(hidden)]` to mark internal implementation modules while keeping the public API surface minimal and clear. The re-exports from the SDK provide clean ergonomics for consumers.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/dto_test.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-100`: **Good DTO test coverage.**\n\nThe tests comprehensively cover domain-to-DTO conversions, JSON serialization/deserialization, and partial patch handling with `Option` semantics. Well-structured test suite.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/handlers.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`13-38`: **Handlers correctly delegate to service with security context.**\n\nThe handlers properly:\n- Extract `AuthzContext` for tenant/user scoping\n- Delegate to service methods for business logic\n- Convert domain models to DTOs for responses\n- Propagate errors via `?` operator\n\nThis aligns with the specification's security context integration requirements.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/migrations/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-12`: **Standard SeaORM migration s