h_settings` (31-38)\n\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service.rs (3)</summary>\n\n* `get_settings` (32-49)\n* `update_settings` (51-62)\n* `patch_settings` (64-78)\n\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/local_client.rs (3)</summary>\n\n* `get_settings` (23-28)\n* `update_settings` (30-40)\n* `patch_settings` (42-51)\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/dto_test.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/file_parser/src/domain/ir.rs (1)</summary>\n\n* `language` (193-196)\n\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>libs/modkit/src/bootstrap/config/mod.rs (1)</summary>\n\n* `None` (1247-1247)\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_update.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/conftest.py (2)</summary>\n\n* `base_url` (12-14)\n* `auth_headers` (18-28)\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/error.rs (1)</summary>\n\n* `validation` (16-21)\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</details><details>\n<summary>🪛 LanguageTool</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md</summary>\n\n[style] ~30-~30: You have already used this phrasing in nearby sentences. Consider replacing it to add variety to your writing.\nContext: ...\"I want to create a spec proposal\" - \"I want to create a spec\"  Loose matching guidance...\n\n(REP_WANT_TO_VB)\n\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/design.md</summary>\n\n[style] ~118-~118: To form a complete sentence, be sure to include a subject.\nContext: ...es implementation, matches Go behavior. Can be added later if needed via event sour...\n\n(MISSING_IT_THERE)\n\n---\n\n[style] ~151-~151: To form a complete sentence, be sure to include a subject.\nContext: ... keep it simple with two string fields. Can be extended later if needed.  3. **Shou...\n\n(MISSING_IT_THERE)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n<details>\n<summary>🪛 Ruff (0.14.11)</summary>\n\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_integration.py</summary>\n\n165-165: Local variable `patched_settings` is assigned to but never used\n\nRemove assignment to unused variable `patched_settings`\n\n(F841)\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>⏰ Context from checks skipped due to timeout of 90000ms. You can increase the timeout in your CodeRabbit configuration to a maximum of 15 minutes (900000ms). (9)</summary>\n\n* GitHub Check: Run Dylint Lints\n* GitHub Check: Test Suite (windows-latest, rust=stable)\n* GitHub Check: Test Suite (ubuntu-latest, rust=stable)\n* GitHub Check: Dylint Lints Tests\n* GitHub Check: Test Suite (macos-latest, rust=stable)\n* GitHub Check: modkit-db integration (backend=pg)\n* GitHub Check: users_info integration (Postgres)\n* GitHub Check: UI macro tests (trybuild)\n* GitHub Check: Code Coverage (cargo-llvm-cov)\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔇 Additional comments (54)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-456`: **Excellent OpenSpec documentation—comprehensive and well-structured.**\n\nThis documentation provides thorough guidance for AI coding assistants working with OpenSpec. The three-stage workflow is clearly explained, CLI commands are well-documented, and the practical examples (bash scripts, markdown templates) make it immediately actionable. The troubleshooting section and common pitfalls guidance (especially around MODIFIED requirements at line 274) are particularly valuable.\n\nThe static analysis hint about repetitive phrasing on line 30 is a false positive—the repetition of \"I want to\" is intentional as these lines demonstrate example user phrases that trigger proposal creation.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/gts/errors.json (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-17`: **LGTM! Error catalog is well-structured and properly aligned.**\n\nThe JSON is valid, HTTP status codes are appropriate (404 for not found, 422 for validation, 500 for database errors), and the error codes follow a consistent versioned naming convention. The errors.json file serves as the source of truth for the error catalog, which is used by the modkit_errors_macro to generate strongly-typed Rust error code constants. All three domain error variants (NotFound, Validation, Database) correctly map to their corresponding JSON definitions via the REST error handler.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean module declaration that properly exposes the REST API surface.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/Cargo.toml (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-36`: **Well-structured crate manifest.**\n\nGood use of workspace inheritance for versioning, lints, and dependencies. The organization with SDK, external, and internal dependencies is clear and follows project conventions. Based on learnings, workspace dependencies are properly referenced using `.workspace = true`.\n\n---\n\n`32-32`: **Remove the unused `insecure-escape` feature from `modkit-db` dependency.**\n\nThe `insecure-escape` feature is enabled but never used in this module. This feature provides raw database access that bypasses all security controls and should only be enabled for administrative tools or maintenance operations when explicitly needed. Remove it unless there is a documented reason this module requires administrative database access.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>⛔ Skipped due to learnings</summary>\n\n```\nLearnt from: CR\nRepo: hypernetix/hyperspot PR: 0\nFile: AGENTS.md:0-0\nTimestamp: 2026-01-14T17:18:14.316Z\nLearning: Always open `@/docs/MODKIT_UNIFIED_SYSTEM.md` to learn about the project's `@/lib/modkit`* unified system\n```\n\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/config.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-15`: **Clean configuration implementation.**\n\nGood use of serde's `default` attribute combined with a manual `Default` implementation. This ensures consistent defaults whether the config is deserialized from YAML/ENV or constructed programmatically.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/Cargo.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-18`: **LGTM!**\n\nWell-structured SDK crate with minimal dependencies. Good practice to keep the SDK lightweight and transport-agnostic. Based on learnings, workspace dependencies are properly referenced.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/models.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-22`: **Clean, transport-agnostic data models.**\n\nWell-documented structs with appropriate derives. The `Patch` pattern with `Option<T>` fields is idiomatic for partial update operations. The `Default` derive on `SimpleUserSettingsPatch` is convenient for building partial updates incrementally.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/errors.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-34`: **Well-structured error type with proper Rust idioms.**\n\nGood use of `thiserror` for deriving `Error` and `Display`, `#[must_use]` attributes on constructors, and `impl Into<String>` for flexible input. The enum covers the essential error categories for an SDK.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/migrations/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-12`: **LGTM!**\n\nStandard SeaORM migrator setup that correctly wires the `initial_001` migration. The implementation follows conventions and is appropriately minimal.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/specs/user-settings/spec.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-172`: **Comprehensive specification covering key requirements.**\n\nThe spec thoroughly documents the REST API semantics, security context integration, data model, error handling with RFC-9457, and SDK design. Good coverage of scenarios for tenant/user isolation and edge cases.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/api.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`16-41`: **Well-designed async trait with proper security context threading.**\n\nThe trait correctly requires `SecurityContext` for all operations, follows `Send + Sync` bounds for async usage, and provides clear documentation. The `patch_settings` method appropriately uses a dedicated patch struct for partial updates.\n\n---\n\n`27-32`: **Positional arguments design acknowledged as temporary.**\n\nThe separate `theme` and `language` parameters (vs. a struct) were discussed in a previous review. Since this is a temporary implementation that will be replaced, no action needed now.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-22`: **LGTM! Clean SDK crate structure.**\n\nThe SDK crate layout follows Rust best practices with clear public API surface, helpful documentation with example usage, and `#![forbid(unsafe_code)]` for safety guarantees. The re-exports provide a clean interface for consumers.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/repo.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-24`: **LGTM! Well-designed repository trait.**\n\nThe trait provides a clean abstraction for settings persistence with appropriate async support, thread-safety bounds (`Send + Sync`), and security context propagation. The method signatures align well with the SeaORM implementation shown in the relevant snippets.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service_test.rs (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-53`: **LGTM! Clean mock implementation.**\n\nThe `MockRepository` provides appropriate test doubles for the repository trait. The implementation correctly mirrors the expected behavior for each method.\n\n---\n\n`108-166`: **Good coverage for update and validation scenarios.**\n\nTests appropriately verify successful updates and max_field_length validation. The custom `ServiceConfig` with `max_field_length: 10` effectively tests the validation boundary.\n\n---\n\n`168-270`: **Solid patch operation test coverage.**\n\nTests cover partial updates, validation on patch fields, and the empty patch edge case. The assertions correctly verify that only provided fields are updated while others remain unchanged.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/entity.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`17-20`: **LGTM! Standard SeaORM boilerplate.**\n\nEmpty `Relation` enum and default `ActiveModelBehavior` are appropriate for an entity without relations or custom behavior hooks.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/dto.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-26`: **LGTM! Well-structured response DTO.**\n\nThe `SimpleUserSettingsDto` properly handles UUID serialization with `#[schema(value_type = String)]` for OpenAPI documentation while keeping the actual type as `Uuid`. The `From` implementation provides clean conversion from the domain model.\n\n---\n\n`28-33`: **LGTM! Clear update request structure.**\n\nRequired fields enforce that full updates must provide both `theme` and `language`.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean module declaration exposing the storage subsystem.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/mapper.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-14`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean `From` implementation with direct field mapping. Consistent with the DTO conversion patterns in the codebase.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-7`: **LGTM!**\n\nWell-organized storage module structure with proper test gating.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-7`: **LGTM!**\n\nStandard REST API module structure with proper separation of concerns (dto, error, handlers, routes) and test gating.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/error.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-38`: **LGTM on the error mapping structure.**\n\nThe domain error to RFC9457 Problem mapping is well-structured with appropriate error categorization. Database errors are correctly logged before being converted to a generic internal error response, avoiding information leakage.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/specs/user-settings/spec.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-170`: **Specification structure is comprehensive.**\n\nThe spec covers all essential aspects: CRUD operations, security context integration, data model, error handling, and SDK design. The scenarios are well-defined with clear WHEN/THEN/AND patterns. The composite primary key and deny-by-default security patterns are appropriate.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_integration.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`91-92`: **Auth handling is now consistent.**\n\nThe early skip when `auth_headers` is empty addresses the previous review feedback about silent test passes.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/dto_test.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-100`: **Good DTO test coverage.**\n\nThe tests cover essential paths: domain-to-DTO conversion, patch conversion (including empty case), serialization with snake_case validation, and deserialization including partial payloads. This provides good confidence in the DTO layer.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/fields.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-6`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean approach for centralizing field name constants. This prevents magic strings and ensures consistent field references across validation, error messages, and other domain logic.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-7`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean domain module structure following DDD-light conventions. The public modules (error, fields, repo, service) are appropriately exposed, and the test module is correctly gated with `#[cfg(test)]`.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_update.py (5)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-42`: **LGTM!**\n\nWell-structured E2E test for the full update endpoint. Good coverage of the happy path with proper authentication handling and response validation.\n\n---\n\n`44-91`: **LGTM!**\n\nGood upsert verification pattern: GET → POST → GET to confirm persistence.\n\n---\n\n`94-136`: **LGTM!**\n\nCorrectly validates replacement semantics with sequential POST operations.\n\n---\n\n`139-165`: **LGTM!**\n\nGood edge case coverage for empty string values.\n\n---\n\n`168-194`: **LGTM!**\n\nGood validation coverage for field length limits.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/design.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-157`: **LGTM!**\n\nComprehensive design document that appropriately covers context, decisions, risks, and migration plan. The SDK pattern, Secure ORM, and lazy creation decisions are well-reasoned with clear alternatives considered. This aligns with the learning that design.md should be created for new architecture patterns and security complexity. Based on learnings.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/handlers.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`13-19`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean handler implementation with proper `AuthzContext` extraction for security and service delegation via `Extension`.\n\n---\n\n`31-38`: **LGTM!**\n\nConsistent pattern with `get_settings` handler.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/local_client.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-19`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean `LocalClient` struct with `#[must_use]` on the constructor. Good facade pattern over the domain service.\n\n---\n\n`21-52`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean `SimpleUserSettingsApi` implementation that delegates to the domain service with proper error conversion via `map_err(Into::into)`. The `From<DomainError> for SettingsError` trait is properly implemented.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/error.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-34`: **LGTM! Well-structured domain error handling.**\n\nThe error enum provides clear categorization (NotFound, Validation, Database), and the `From<DomainError> for SettingsError` conversion correctly maps domain errors to SDK-level errors. The `validation` helper constructor is a nice ergonomic addition.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/sea_orm_repo.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`40-74`: **LGTM! Atomic upsert correctly implemented.**\n\nThe `upsert_full` method now uses `OnConflict` to perform an atomic upsert, eliminating the previously flagged TOCTOU race condition. The pattern of inserting then fetching is appropriate here.\n\n---\n\n`24-38`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe `find_by_user` implementation correctly scopes the query by tenant and user, with clean result mapping.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/routes.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-48`: **LGTM! Clean routing setup with proper auth/license gating.**\n\nThe `Resource`, `Action`, and `License` enums are well-defined with appropriate `AsRef<str>` implementations. The trait implementations for `AuthReqResource`, `AuthReqAction`, and `LicenseFeature` enable clean integration with the `OperationBuilder`.\n\n---\n\n`49-117`: **LGTM! Routes properly configured with OpenAPI documentation.**\n\nThe three endpoints (GET, POST, PATCH) are correctly configured with:\n- Appropriate HTTP methods for the operations\n- Proper auth requirements (Read for GET, Write for POST/PATCH)\n- Complete OpenAPI error responses\n- Correct handler bindings\n\nThe `Extension(service)` layer at line 114 correctly wraps all previously registered routes, making the service available to handlers.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/module.rs (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`17-31`: **LGTM! Module name updated as requested.**\n\nThe module name is now `\"simple-user-settings\"` (line 18), addressing the previous reviewer feedback about reserving \"settings\" for a future proper implementation.\n\n---\n\n`54-78`: **LGTM! Module initialization is well-structured.**\n\nThe `init` method properly:\n- Loads configuration from context\n- Establishes secure database connection\n- Creates the repository and service with proper dependency injection\n- Registers the local client with the client hub for cross-module access\n\n---\n\n`81-100`: **LGTM! REST registration with proper service guard.**\n\nThe `register_rest` method correctly guards against the service not being initialized (line 94) before attempting to register routes.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service.rs (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-20`: **LGTM! Configuration with sensible defaults.**\n\nThe `ServiceConfig` struct with a default `max_field_length` of 100 is appropriate for theme and language fields.\n\n---\n\n`32-49`: **LGTM! Sensible default behavior for missing settings.**\n\nWhen no settings exist, returning a default `SimpleUserSettings` with empty strings avoids forcing clients to handle `Option` types. This aligns with the \"settings always exist\" semantic.\n\n---\n\n`51-78`: **LGTM! Proper validation flow for updates.**\n\nBoth `update_settings` and `patch_settings` correctly validate fields before persisting. The patch method appropriately only validates fields that are present in the request.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/lib.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-7`: **LGTM on the SDK re-exports.**\n\nThe re-export pattern from `simple_user_settings_sdk` provides a clean public API surface for consumers of this crate.\n\n---\n\n`14-23`: **LGTM on internal module visibility.**\n\nUsing `#[doc(hidden)]` for implementation-detail modules (`api`, `config`, `domain`, `errors`, `infra`) is an appropriate pattern—it keeps them accessible internally while excluding them from public documentation.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<sub>✏️ Tip: You can disable this entire section by setting `review_details` to `false` in your review settings.</sub>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3665656478","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3665656478"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-15T13:32:40Z","commit_id":"5e234d6ef6dfa938ff81aac93a8943ee6647e684"},{"id":3670091713,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7awSfB","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 6**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn\n`@modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/specs/user-settings/spec.md`:\n- Around line 23-26: The spec's \"camelCase JSON field naming\" is incorrect and\nmust be aligned with the REST DTOs which use #[serde(rename_all =\n\"snake_case\")]; update the Scenario in specs/user-settings/spec.md (the GET\n/simple-user-settings/v1/settings scenario) to state that the response uses\nsnake_case JSON field naming (or alternatively change the REST DTO attribute on\nthe UserSettings/response struct from #[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")] to\ncamelCase if you intend the API to be camelCase), and ensure the example\nresponse and any references to field names match the chosen casing.\n- Around line 108-117: The \"Scenario: Composite primary key uniqueness\" heading\nand its following list are over-indented causing markdownlint errors; open the\nspecs/user-settings/spec.md and fix the indentation by unindenting the \"####\nScenario: Composite primary key uniqueness\" heading and the subsequent list\nitems so they align with the top-level list (remove the extra leading spaces),\nensure there is a blank line before the heading, and make the bullet lines for\nWHEN/THEN/AND use consistent top-level indentation so markdownlint no longer\nflags them.\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/handlers.rs`:\n- Around line 121-128: Update the tests that use create_test_router to parse and\nassert JSON response bodies in addition to StatusCode::OK: after sending\nrequests to routes wired to get_settings, update_settings, and patch_settings,\nread the response body, deserialize to the expected struct or serde_json::Value,\nand assert that fields like \"theme\" == \"dark\" and \"language\" == \"en\" match the\nmock Service return values; ensure you update each test that uses Router from\ncreate_test_router (and the mock Service setup) to include these body assertions\nwhile keeping the existing status checks and using the same\nSecurityContext::anonymous() extension.\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/module.rs`:\n- Around line 113-118: The test `test_settings_module_clone_empty_service` is\nperforming a move instead of exercising the Clone impl; update the test to call\n`module.clone()` (e.g., let cloned = module.clone();) so it actually invokes\nSettingsModule's Clone implementation and then assert that\n`cloned.service.load().is_none()` (and optionally that\n`module.service.load().is_none()` still holds if you want to verify the original\nremains usable). Ensure you reference `SettingsModule::default`, the `Clone`\nimpl, and the `cloned` variable in the updated test.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_integration.py`:\n- Around line 127-178: The variable patched_settings assigned from\npatch_response.json() in test_settings_consistency_across_methods is unused\n(patched_settings) causing a lint error; either remove the assignment or add an\nassertion on patch_response/patched_settings to validate the PATCH result (e.g.,\nassert patch_response.status_code == 200 is already present, so also assert\npatched_settings[\"theme\"] == \"light\" and patched_settings[\"language\"] == \"fr\")\nto both satisfy Ruff and strengthen the test; update the logic in\ntest_settings_consistency_across_methods around the\npatch_response/patched_settings usage accordingly.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_update.py`:\n- Around line 168-194: The inline comment in\ntest_update_settings_validation_max_length is inconsistent with the assertion:\nupdate the comment that currently says \"Should return 400 Bad Request for\nvalidation error\" to correctly state the expected status code 422 (Unprocessable\nEntity) to match the assertion in the test; locate the\ntest_update_settings_validation_max_length function and change the comment text\nto reflect 422 (or alternatively change the assertion if the intended expected\nstatus is 400) so comment and assertion are aligned.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/tasks.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`71-73`: **Test coverage claim contradicts Codecov metrics.**\n\nThis task claims 90%+ coverage is verified and that tests were added for `routes.rs`, `handlers.rs`, `module.rs`, and `error.rs`. However, the Codecov report in the PR comments shows:\n\n- **Actual patch coverage: 65.65%** (192 lines missing)\n- `routes.rs`: **0.00%** coverage (68 lines missing)\n- `handlers.rs`: **0.00%** coverage (24 lines missing)  \n- `error.rs`: **0.00%** coverage (20 lines missing)\n- `module.rs`: **22.58%** coverage (24 lines missing)\n\nThis represents a ~24 percentage point gap between claimed and actual coverage. Since this is archived documentation, it should accurately reflect the actual implementation state for future reference.\n\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-- [x] 10.4 Verify 90%+ test coverage\n-  - Added tests for `routes.rs`, `handlers.rs`, `module.rs`, `error.rs`\n-  - All 34 unit tests passing\n+- [ ] 10.4 Verify 90%+ test coverage (current: ~65% patch coverage per Codecov)\n+  - Need tests for `routes.rs` (0%), `handlers.rs` (0%), `error.rs` (0%)\n+  - Need additional coverage for `module.rs` (22.58%), `domain/error.rs` (46.15%)\n+  - 34 existing unit tests passing but insufficient coverage on REST layer\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/migrations/initial_001.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`10-42`: **Add `created_at` / `updated_at` columns (still missing).**\n\nThe migration still omits timestamp columns required for auditability and lifecycle tracking.\n\n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Proposed fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n                     .col(ColumnDef::new(Settings::TenantId).uuid().not_null())\n                     .col(ColumnDef::new(Settings::UserId).uuid().not_null())\n                     .col(ColumnDef::new(Settings::Theme).string())\n                     .col(ColumnDef::new(Settings::Language).string())\n+                    .col(ColumnDef::new(Settings::CreatedAt).timestamp_with_time_zone().not_null())\n+                    .col(ColumnDef::new(Settings::UpdatedAt).timestamp_with_time_zone().not_null())\n                     .primary_key(\n                         Index::create()\n                             .col(Settings::TenantId)\n                             .col(Settings::UserId),\n                     )\n```\n\n```diff\n enum Settings {\n     Table,\n     TenantId,\n     UserId,\n     Theme,\n     Language,\n+    CreatedAt,\n+    UpdatedAt,\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/proposal.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`9-29`: **Doc naming is out of sync (paths + SecurityCtx).**\n\nThe proposal still uses `/settings/v1/settings`, `SecurityContext`, and `modules/settings/...` paths, which no longer match the current simple-user-settings naming.\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-  - `GET /settings/v1/settings` - Retrieve user settings\n-  - `POST /settings/v1/settings` - Full update of settings\n-  - `PATCH /settings/v1/settings` - Partial update of settings\n- Use `SecurityContext` for automatic tenant and user isolation (no explicit tenant_id/user_id in requests)\n+  - `GET /simple-user-settings/v1/settings` - Retrieve user settings\n+  - `POST /simple-user-settings/v1/settings` - Full update of settings\n+  - `PATCH /simple-user-settings/v1/settings` - Partial update of settings\n+ Use `SecurityCtx` for automatic tenant and user isolation (no explicit tenant_id/user_id in requests)\n@@\n-  - New: `modules/settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/` (SDK crate)\n-  - New: `modules/settings/simple-user-settings/` (implementation crate)\n+  - New: `modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/` (SDK crate)\n+  - New: `modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/` (implementation crate)\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/sea_orm_repo.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`76-136`: **Race condition still present in `upsert_patch`.**\n\nThis method still has a TOCTOU vulnerability as flagged in the previous review. The flow reads the existing record (lines 85-89), then builds an `ActiveModel` with merged values, and finally performs an upsert. A concurrent request could modify the record between the read and write, causing lost updates.\n\nThe `upsert_full` method was fixed with atomic `OnConflict`, but `upsert_patch` requires preserving existing values when patch fields are `None`, making the fix more complex. Consider wrapping the read-modify-write in a database transaction to ensure atomicity.\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>🔧 Suggested transactional approach</summary>\n\n```diff\n async fn upsert_patch(\n     &self,\n     ctx: &SecurityContext,\n     patch: SimpleUserSettingsPatch,\n ) -> anyhow::Result<SimpleUserSettings> {\n     let user_id = ctx.subject_id();\n     let tenant_id = ctx.tenant_id();\n     let scope = AccessScope::both(vec![tenant_id], vec![user_id]);\n+\n+    let txn = self.db.conn().begin().await?;\n\n     let existing = self\n         .db\n         .find::<SettingsEntity>(&scope)\n-        .one(self.db.conn())\n+        .one(&txn)\n         .await?;\n\n     let model = if let Some(existing) = existing {\n         // ... existing merge logic ...\n         if !columns_to_update.is_empty() {\n             SettingsEntity::insert(active_model)\n                 .on_conflict(/* ... */)\n-                .exec(self.db.conn())\n+                .exec(&txn)\n                 .await?;\n         }\n\n         self.db\n             .find::<SettingsEntity>(&scope)\n-            .one(self.db.conn())\n+            .one(&txn)\n             .await?\n             .ok_or_else(|| anyhow::anyhow!(\"Record should exist after upsert\"))?\n     } else {\n         // ... insert logic ...\n-        active_model.insert(self.db.conn()).await?\n+        active_model.insert(&txn).await?\n     };\n\n+    txn.commit().await?;\n     Ok(model.into())\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/project.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`36-50`: **Add language specifier to fenced code block.**\n\nThe directory structure code block is missing a language specifier. Use `text` or `plaintext` for non-code content to satisfy markdown linting.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n modules/<module>/\n ├─ <module>-sdk/           # Public API: trait, models, errors (NO serde)\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`125-141`: **Add language specifier to directory structure code blocks.**\n\nSeveral fenced code blocks showing directory structures are missing language specifiers. Use `text` or `plaintext` for consistency with markdown linting.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix for lines 125 and 147</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n openspec/\n ├── project.md              # Project conventions\n```\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n New request?\n ├─ Bug fix restoring spec behavior? → Fix directly\n```\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`351-360`: **Add language specifier to code block.**\n\nThis directory structure example should have a `text` language specifier.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-```\n+```text\n openspec/changes/add-2fa-notify/\n ├── proposal.md\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service_test.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`59-269`: **Comprehensive test coverage for domain service operations.**\n\nThe test suite covers get/update/patch happy paths, field validation with custom `max_field_length`, partial updates preserving existing values, and empty patch behavior. \n\nConsider adding a test case for repository error propagation (e.g., when `find_by_user` returns `Err`) to verify `DomainError::Database` handling, though this is optional for initial implementation.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📜 Review details</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commits</summary>\n\nReviewing files that changed from the base of the PR and between 5e234d6ef6dfa938ff81aac93a8943ee6647e684 and 617e89ab7df6e18df15aa9ce03c79a7872619d16.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>⛔ Files ignored due to path filters (1)</summary>\n\n* `Cargo.lock` is excluded by `!**/*.lock`\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📒 Files selected for processing (49)</summary>\n\n* `Cargo.toml`\n* `apps/hyperspot-server/Cargo.toml`\n* `apps/hyperspot-server/tests/cli_smoke_tests.rs`\n* `config/e2e-local.yaml`\n* `config/quickstart.yaml`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/AGENTS.md`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/design.md`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/proposal.md`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/specs/user-settings/spec.md`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/tasks.md`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/project.md`\n* `modul