n\"**Reasoning:** Simplifies implementation, matches Go behavior. Can be added\nlater if needed via event sourcing or audit log.\") and make the same adjustment\nat the other occurrence.\n\nIn\n`@modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/proposal.md`:\n- Around line 11-14: Update the REST endpoint paths listed in the proposal to\nmatch the implementation by replacing `/settings/v1/settings` with the canonical\npath `/simple-user-settings/v1/settings` for all three endpoints (`GET`, `POST`,\n`PATCH`) so the bullets read `GET /simple-user-settings/v1/settings`, `POST\n/simple-user-settings/v1/settings`, and `PATCH\n/simple-user-settings/v1/settings`.\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/specs/user-settings/spec.md`:\n- Around line 15-18: Update the \"Default settings for new users\" scenario to\ndefine the exact JSON response shape for first-time retrievals (explicitly state\nwhether \"theme\" and \"language\" are null, empty string, or omitted) and make the\nsame explicit representation in the PATCH-create scenario; reference the\nscenario title \"Default settings for new users\" and the fields \"theme\" and\n\"language\" so tests/clients know the canonical format and ensure the\nPATCH-create example shows the created record matching that exact JSON shape.\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/errors.rs`:\n- Around line 10-12: SettingsError::Validation currently flattens domain\nvalidation info into a single message causing loss of the field name; change the\nenum variant to include field: String (i.e., Validation { field: String,\nmessage: String }) and update the conversion from DomainError::Validation (and\nany related constructor functions) to map domain.field -> field and\ndomain.message -> message so the field-level structure is preserved end-to-end\n(update the SettingsError::from / impl From<DomainError> and any\nSettingsError::new_validation helper to accept and set the field).\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/lib.rs`:\n- Around line 5-7: The crate's public re-exports in lib.rs are missing the\nSimpleUserSettingsUpdate type so consumers can't construct the payload for\nupdate_settings; update the pub use from simple_user_settings_sdk to include\nSimpleUserSettingsUpdate alongside SettingsError, SimpleUserSettings,\nSimpleUserSettingsApi, and SimpleUserSettingsPatch so the update model is\nexposed in the public API surface.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_get.py`:\n- Around line 6-39: The docstring says the first GET should return empty strings\nbut the test (test_get_settings_returns_defaults) only asserts types; update the\ntest to match the docstring by asserting settings[\"theme\"] == \"\" and\nsettings[\"language\"] == \"\" (after the existing presence and type checks) or, if\nyou prefer to relax the behavior, change the docstring to note that only string\ntypes are guaranteed — pick one and make the assertions and docstring consistent\naround the settings variable and the \"theme\" and \"language\" keys in\ntest_get_settings_returns_defaults.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_patch.py`:\n- Around line 185-213: In test_patch_settings_creates_if_not_exists update the\nassertion for the language field to accept null or a missing key: after\nobtaining settings from patch_response.json(), use settings.get(\"language\") and\nassert it is either None or a str (i.e., allow settings.get(\"language\") is None\nor isinstance(settings.get(\"language\"), str)); reference the test function name\ntest_patch_settings_creates_if_not_exists and the variables patch_response and\nsettings to locate the change.\n- Around line 215-240: The inline comment above the final assertion in\ntest_patch_settings_validation_max_length is inconsistent (it says 400) while\nthe test asserts 422; update the comment to match the asserted status code\n(e.g., \"Should return 422 Unprocessable Entity for validation error\") so the\ncomment and the assertion on patch_response.status_code in\ntest_patch_settings_validation_max_length are consistent.\n````\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/sea_orm_repo.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`76-136`: **Race condition remains in `upsert_patch`.**\n\nThis method still has a TOCTOU vulnerability: the read at lines 85-89 and the subsequent insert/update at lines 110-117 or 126-132 are not atomic. Two concurrent patch requests could both see `existing = None` and race to insert.\n\nConsider wrapping this in a database transaction or using a similar `OnConflict` pattern for the insert branch.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>testing/docker/hyperspot.Dockerfile (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`24-26`: **Pre-existing: Comment does not match actual build mode.**\n\nThe comment says \"release mode\" but the command lacks `--release`, and line 34 copies from `target/debug/`. For E2E tests, debug mode may be intentional, but the comment should be corrected.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested comment fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-# Build the hyperspot-server binary in release mode\n+# Build the hyperspot-server binary in debug mode\n # Using --bin to build only the specific binary\n RUN cargo build --bin hyperspot-server --features users-info-example\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/error.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`28-30`: **SettingsError SDK doesn't support structured field validation info.**\n\nThe `DomainError::Validation` variant carries separate `field` and `message` fields, but `SettingsError::Validation` only accepts a single `message` parameter. This mapping necessarily concatenates them into a string because the SDK's error API doesn't expose field-level structure. If field-level validation details are important to expose to clients, the `SettingsError::Validation` variant should be extended with a `field` field to preserve this information.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_update.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`44-91`: **Consider test isolation for E2E reliability.**\n\nThis test assumes a specific starting state. In a shared E2E environment, prior test runs may leave settings in unexpected states. Consider using unique identifiers per test run (if the API supports it) or accepting that tests verify behavior rather than exact values.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/handlers.rs (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`22-36`: **Minor: Inconsistent return pattern with other handlers.**\n\n`update_settings` explicitly returns `(StatusCode::OK, Json(dto))` while `get_settings` and `patch_settings` simply return `Ok(Json(...))`. This works but is inconsistent. Consider aligning the return style.\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested alignment</summary>\n\n```diff\n pub async fn update_settings(\n     AuthzContext(ctx): AuthzContext,\n     Extension(svc): Extension<Arc<Service>>,\n     Json(req): Json<UpdateSimpleUserSettingsRequest>,\n-) -> ApiResult<impl IntoResponse> {\n+) -> ApiResult<JsonBody<SimpleUserSettingsDto>> {\n     let update = SimpleUserSettingsUpdate {\n         theme: req.theme,\n         language: req.language,\n     };\n     let settings = svc\n         .update_settings(&ctx, update)\n         .await?;\n-    let dto: SimpleUserSettingsDto = settings.into();\n-    Ok((StatusCode::OK, Json(dto)))\n+    Ok(Json(settings.into()))\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`146-168`: **Test verifies mock behavior, not handler transformation.**\n\nThe test sends `{\"theme\":\"light\",\"language\":\"es\"}` but asserts `\"dark\"/\"en\"` because the mock ignores input and always returns the same `upsert_result`. This is valid for testing the handler wiring but doesn't verify that input values are passed correctly to the service. Consider adding a test that captures the arguments passed to the mock.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`84-91`: **Confirm whether max length is byte- or character-based.**  \n`value.len()` counts bytes; if the limit is meant to be *characters*, this will reject multibyte input too early. If the requirement is character length, consider `chars().count()`.  \n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✅ Suggested adjustment (if character-based)</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        if value.len() > self.config.max_field_length {\n+        if value.chars().count() > self.config.max_field_length {\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📜 Review details</summary>\n\n**Configuration used**: defaults\n\n**Review profile**: CHILL\n\n**Plan**: Pro\n\n<details>\n<summary>📥 Commits</summary>\n\nReviewing files that changed from the base of the PR and between 61201e763921f5e1ce3837dd429946cd122f2d03 and e3a24cda8143cb53d8b74e085246f4a4da416c0c.\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>⛔ Files ignored due to path filters (1)</summary>\n\n* `Cargo.lock` is excluded by `!**/*.lock`\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>📒 Files selected for processing (58)</summary>\n\n* `Cargo.toml`\n* `apps/hyperspot-server/Cargo.toml`\n* `apps/hyperspot-server/src/registered_modules.rs`\n* `apps/hyperspot-server/tests/cli_smoke_tests.rs`\n* `config/e2e-local.yaml`\n* `config/quickstart.yaml`\n* `guidelines/DNA`\n* `libs/modkit-auth/src/axum_ext.rs`\n* `libs/modkit/src/contracts.rs`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/AGENTS.md`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md`\n* `modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-mo