rage/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-7`: **LGTM!**\n\nWell-organized storage module structure with proper separation of concerns:\n- `entity` for SeaORM models\n- `mapper` for domain ↔ entity conversions\n- `migrations` for schema management\n- `sea_orm_repo` for repository implementation\n- Test module correctly gated with `#[cfg(test)]`\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/error.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`12-37`: **LGTM!**\n\nThe error mapping logic is well-structured:\n- Correctly maps domain errors to RFC 9457 Problem responses using the error catalog\n- Database errors are properly logged before returning a sanitized response to clients\n- `From<DomainError>` implementation enables ergonomic `?` operator usage in handlers\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-456`: **Well-documented OpenSpec instructions.**\n\nComprehensive onboarding guide covering the three-stage workflow, delta formats, validation, and troubleshooting. The content correctly uses:\n- SHALL/MUST for normative requirements (as per learnings)\n- Proper `#### Scenario:` format guidance\n- Clear decision trees and examples\n\nAside from the bold-as-heading formatting issues noted above, the document is thorough and follows established OpenSpec conventions.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/fields.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-6`: **LGTM!**\n\nClean pattern for centralizing field name constants, avoiding magic strings across the codebase.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/errors.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-35`: **LGTM! Well-structured SDK error types.**\n\nThe `SettingsError` enum is well-designed with appropriate variants and idiomatic constructors. The `Validation` variant correctly preserves field-level structure (field + message), addressing the previous review feedback. The `#[must_use]` attributes on constructors are a nice touch for API ergonomics.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/Cargo.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-18`: **LGTM! Proper workspace-aligned SDK crate manifest.**\n\nThe manifest correctly uses workspace defaults (`version`, `edition`, `license`, `authors`, and `lints`) and all dependencies are properly configured with workspace inheritance. The SDK crate is registered in the workspace root members list and appropriately structured for a transport-agnostic SDK.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/specs/user-settings/spec.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`142-145`: **SDK models correctly follow transport-agnostic design principle.**\n\nThe models (`SimpleUserSettings`, `SimpleUserSettingsPatch`, `SimpleUserSettingsUpdate`) use only `Debug`, `Clone`, `PartialEq`, `Eq`, and `Default` derives—no `serde` or HTTP-specific serialization. This implementation correctly fulfills the spec requirement that SDK models be suitable for any transport.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/config.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`3-18`: **LGTM — config defaults are clear and consistent.**\n\nThe serde default and Default impl are straightforward and match expected semantics.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/error_test.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-70`: **Nice coverage for DomainError → Problem mappings.**\n\nThe tests validate status, instance, and detail content for all core variants.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/repo.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-24`: **Repository interface looks well-scoped for the use cases.**\n\nCovers read, full upsert, and patch updates cleanly.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>apps/hyperspot-server/tests/cli_smoke_tests.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`213-222`: **Good update to keep CLI smoke config aligned with new module.**\n\nAdding the module entry avoids false negatives in config validation.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>apps/hyperspot-server/Cargo.toml (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`41-45`: **LGTM — local module dependency is wired correctly.**\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service_test.rs (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`12-55`: **MockRepository implementation looks good.**\n\nThe mock correctly simulates repository behavior for all three operations. The `upsert_patch` implementation properly merges patch fields with existing state, which aligns with expected patch semantics.\n\n---\n\n`61-84`: **Test correctly validates retrieval of existing settings.**\n\nThe test properly verifies that when settings exist, the service returns them with the expected values.\n\n---\n\n`86-107`: **Addressed: assertions for user_id and tenant_id now present.**\n\nThe test now properly asserts that when no settings are found, the returned defaults use the context's subject_id and tenant_id (lines 103-104), addressing the previous review feedback.\n\n---\n\n`142-179`: **Validation test correctly verifies max length enforcement.**\n\nThe test properly configures a custom `max_field_length` of 10 and verifies that an 11-character input triggers `DomainError::Validation`.\n\n---\n\n`181-214`: **Patch semantics correctly tested.**\n\nThe test validates that patching only `theme` preserves the existing `language` value, demonstrating proper partial update behavior.\n\n---\n\n`216-283`: **Remaining patch tests are well-structured.**\n\nBoth the validation test for patch max length and the empty patch test correctly verify edge cases. The empty patch test confirms idempotent behavior when no fields are provided.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/dto_test.rs (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`7-25`: **DTO conversion test is thorough.**\n\nThe test correctly verifies that all fields are mapped from `SimpleUserSettings` to `SimpleUserSettingsDto`, including both IDs and optional fields.\n\n---\n\n`38-62`: **Patch conversion tests correctly validate optional field handling.**\n\nBoth tests properly verify the `From` implementation for converting `PatchSimpleUserSettingsRequest` to `SimpleUserSettingsPatch`, including the empty patch case.\n\n---\n\n`64-99`: **Serialization and deserialization tests provide good coverage.**\n\nThe tests verify:\n- JSON output uses snake_case field naming\n- Full deserialization of update requests\n- Partial deserialization correctly sets missing fields to `None`\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/routes.rs (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`9-47`: **Resource, Action, and License implementations are clean.**\n\nThe implementations correctly satisfy the required traits for the operation builder. Using enums for resources and actions provides type safety.\n\n---\n\n`59-60`: **Verify: Empty array passed to `require_license_features`.**\n\nAll three endpoints pass an empty array `[]` to `require_license_features::<License>([])`. This appears to define the `License` type but not actually require any specific features. Confirm this is the intended behavior and not a placeholder that should list actual feature requirements.\n\n\n\n\nAlso applies to: 77-78, 98-99\n\n---\n\n`49-117`: **Route registration follows consistent patterns.**\n\nThe three endpoints (GET, POST, PATCH) are registered with appropriate:\n- HTTP methods and paths\n- Operation metadata for OpenAPI\n- Auth and license requirements\n- Request/response schemas\n- Error response codes (400, 401, 403, 422, 500 as applicable)\n\nThe `Extension(service)` layer is correctly added after all routes are registered.\n\n---\n\n`119-170`: **Unit tests provide good coverage for trait implementations.**\n\nThe tests verify:\n- `AsRef<str>` implementations return expected strings\n- Types correctly implement `AuthReqResource`, `AuthReqAction`, and `LicenseFeature` traits\n\nThese compile-time trait assertions are a good practice for ensuring type safety.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/mapper.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-14`: **Mapper implementation is correct and minimal.**\n\nThe `From` implementation provides a clean, direct mapping from the database entity to the domain model. All fields are transferred without modification, which is appropriate for this use case.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/mod.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-7`: **Domain module structure is well-organized.**\n\nThe module correctly exposes the public API (`error`, `fields`, `repo`, `service`) while keeping tests internal via `#[cfg(test)]`. This follows standard Rust conventions for organizing domain logic.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/specs/user-settings/spec.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-167`: **Well-structured specification with comprehensive coverage.**\n\nThe specification clearly defines the module's purpose, REST endpoints, security requirements, and data model. The explicit JSON shape for default settings (lines 17-23) and the cross-reference in the PATCH-create scenario (line 92) provide clear guidance for implementers and test authors.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/models.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-31`: **Clean, transport-agnostic model design.**\n\nThe models correctly implement the spec's requirements: `SimpleUserSettingsUpdate` enforces full replacement with required fields, while `SimpleUserSettingsPatch` supports partial updates with optional fields. The absence of serde derives keeps these models transport-agnostic as specified.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/api.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`12-40`: **Well-designed API trait with clear semantics.**\n\nThe trait properly distinguishes between full replacement (`update_settings` with `SimpleUserSettingsUpdate`) and partial updates (`patch_settings` with `SimpleUserSettingsPatch`). The `SecurityContext` requirement on all methods ensures proper authorization as specified.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/handlers.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`93-191`: **Tests verify handler wiring correctly.**\n\nThe tests appropriately validate that handlers return JSON responses with expected status codes and that the mock service is correctly integrated. Note that input transformation (e.g., verifying the submitted `theme`/`language` values flow through) is appropriately tested at the domain/service layer rather than here.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/module.rs (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`17-23`: **LGTM - Module declaration looks correct.**\n\nThe module name has been updated to `\"simple-user-settings\"` as requested. The use of `ArcSwapOption<Service>` is appropriate for thread-safe lazy initialization of the service.\n\n---\n\n`33-39`: **LGTM - Clone implementation is correct.**\n\nThe Clone impl correctly clones the `Arc<Service>` reference (not the Service itself), maintaining shared state semantics as intended for module instances.\n\n---\n\n`41-52`: **LGTM - Migration implementation is correct.**\n\nThe migration logic properly uses the secure database connection and propagates errors appropriately.\n\n---\n\n`54-79`: **LGTM - Module initialization is well-structured.**\n\nThe initialization flow correctly wires the configuration, repository, service, and local client. The service is properly registered in the client hub for dependency injection.\n\n---\n\n`81-101`: **LGTM - REST registration handles uninitialized state correctly.**\n\nGood defensive check ensuring the service is initialized before registering routes. The error message is clear and actionable.\n\n---\n\n`103-120`: **LGTM - Tests cover basic module behavior.**\n\nThe tests properly verify default construction and clone behavior. The clone test now correctly exercises the `Clone` implementation.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/dto.rs (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`6-15`: **LGTM - DTO structure is correct.**\n\nThe DTO properly derives serialization traits and ToSchema. The `#[schema(value_type = String)]` annotations correctly document the Uuid fields as strings in the OpenAPI schema while preserving type safety in Rust.\n\n---\n\n`17-26`: **LGTM - Conversion from domain model to DTO is correct.**\n\nDirect 1:1 field mapping is appropriate here.\n\n---\n\n`44-51`: **LGTM - Patch conversion is correct.**\n\nThe `From` impl correctly maps the optional patch fields to the domain model.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service.rs (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`12-22`: **LGTM - ServiceConfig with sensible defaults.**\n\nThe default `max_field_length` of 100 is reasonable for theme/language settings and aligns with the module-level configuration.\n\n---\n\n`24-32`: **LGTM - Service struct with proper dependency injection.**\n\nThe use of `Arc<dyn SettingsRepository>` enables proper repository sharing and testability through mocking.\n\n---\n\n`34-51`: **LGTM - Lazy default settings pattern is appropriate.**\n\nReturning an in-memory default when no settings exist avoids unnecessary database writes while still providing a consistent API response. Users only get persisted records when they explicitly update their settings.\n\n---\n\n`53-66`: **LGTM - Full update with validation is correct.**\n\nValidation is properly performed before persistence. The `upsert_full` approach ensures both fields are always set during a full update operation.\n\n---\n\n`68-82`: **LGTM - Patch with conditional validation is well-implemented.**\n\nThe method correctly validates only the fields that are present in the patch request, preserving proper PATCH semantics.\n\n---\n\n`84-92`: No action needed. Theme identifiers and BCP 47 language codes are ASCII-only by specification (RFC 5646 restricts language tags to US-ASCII letters, digits, and hyphens). The `value.len()` validation is correct and appropriate for these fields.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_update.py (5)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`6-41`: **Solid happy-path coverage for full update.**  \nAssertions validate response structure and updated fields.\n\n---\n\n`44-92`: **Good upsert-on-first-call test flow.**  \nThe GET→POST→GET sequence is clear and validates persistence.\n\n---\n\n`94-136`: **Replacement behavior verified cleanly.**  \nSecond POST assertions cover the expected overwrite semantics.\n\n---\n\n`139-165`: **Empty-string acceptance test is clear.**  \nCovers a useful edge case without extra noise.\n\n---\n\n`197-221`: **Missing-field validation test reads well.**  \nAccepting either 400/422 keeps it tolerant across validators.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_integration.py (3)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`6-81`: **Comprehensive workflow coverage.**  \nCovers GET/POST/PATCH lifecycle and validates state at each step.\n\n---\n\n`84-125`: **Idempotency test is clear and consistent.**  \nTop-level auth skip keeps the behavior deterministic.\n\n---\n\n`127-181`: **Good cross-method consistency assertions.**  \nPOST vs PATCH convergence is validated end-to-end.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<sub>✏️ Tip: You can disable this entire section by setting `review_details` to `false` in your review settings.</sub>\n\n</details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3671549756","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3671549756"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-16T15:51:42Z","commit_id":"738ad1cfd2eee942b46586157312771596154df5"},{"id":3677979934,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7bOYUe","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 4**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn\n`@modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/design.md`:\n- Around line 66-72: Clarify the \"empty defaults\" behavior in the design:\nexplicitly state whether GET should return JSON fields as empty strings \"\" or\nnull, and update the Rust DTO guidance to use concrete String fields (e.g.,\nnon-Option types) serialized as \"\" when unset (rather than Option<String> ->\nnull) so it matches E2E tests; mention that POST/PATCH will create/overwrite the\npersistent record and that the GET response semantics apply only when no record\nexists.\n\nIn\n`@modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/specs/user-settings/spec.md`:\n- Around line 14-17: Update the \"Default settings for new users\" scenario to\nexplicitly define the JSON representation for theme and language (e.g., whether\nthey are null, empty string \"\", or omitted) for both the first-time GET response\nand the PATCH-create response; ensure the same explicit representation is\napplied to all other occurrences (including the referenced block at 82-86) and\nupdate any example responses and acceptance criteria in the spec so clients and\nimplementations use the exact keys \"theme\" and \"language\" with the chosen JSON\nvalues consistently.\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/project.md`:\n- Line 60: The project.md entry incorrectly states JSON naming is `camelCase`;\nupdate that line to state `snake_case` to match the DTOs annotated with\n`#[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]` in the three DTOs (see the DTOs in\nsimple-user-settings/src/api/rest/dto.rs) and the spec in spec.md that requires\nsnake_case; ensure the wording explicitly says \"snake_case via\n`#[serde(rename_all = \\\"snake_case\\\")]` and that the response uses snake_case\nJSON field naming to keep documentation consistent with implementation and\nspecification.\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_get.py`:\n- Around line 37-42: The test assumes theme/language are empty strings but the\nAPI returns JSON null (mapped to Python None); update\ntesting/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_get.py to assert that\nsettings[\"theme\"] and settings[\"language\"] may be None (e.g., replace\nisinstance(..., str) with a check that allows None or str and change equality\nchecks to expect None when unset), so assertions reference the settings dict\nkeys \"theme\" and \"language\" and accept None as the default unset value.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (5)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`43-47`: **Use proper headings instead of bold text.**\n\nMarkdownlint flags bold text used as headings at lines 43, 293, 297, and 301. Switch to heading syntax for consistency and lint compliance.\n\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested edit</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Workflow**\n+#### Workflow\n```\n\nSimilarly at lines 293, 297, 301:\n```diff\n-**\"Change must have at least one delta\"**\n+#### \"Change must have at least one delta\"\n\n-**\"Requirement must have at least one scenario\"**\n+#### \"Requirement must have at least one scenario\"\n\n-**Silent scenario parsing failures**\n+#### Silent scenario parsing failures\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/tasks.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`71-74`: **Coverage claim still slightly overstated.**\n\nThe task claims \"90%+ test coverage\" and is marked complete, but Codecov shows 83.82% patch coverage. While significant improvement from earlier reviews, the claim should be updated to reflect actual metrics or the coverage gap addressed.\n\nFiles with notable missing coverage per Codecov:\n- `routes.rs`: 44.00% (56 lines missing)\n- `module.rs`: 53.65% (19 lines missing)\n- `sdk/errors.rs`: 0.00% (12 lines missing)\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested update</summary>\n\n```diff\n-- [x] 10.4 Verify 90%+ test coverage\n-  - Added tests for `routes.rs`, `handlers.rs`, `module.rs`, `error.rs`\n-  - All 34 unit tests passing\n+- [x] 10.4 Test coverage verification\n+  - Current patch coverage: ~84% per Codecov\n+  - Added tests for `routes.rs`, `handlers.rs`, `module.rs`, `error.rs`\n+  - All 34 unit tests passing\n+  - Note: Some infrastructure code (routes, module setup) has lower coverage\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/migrations/initial_001.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`15-44`: **Add required `created_at` / `updated_at` columns if spec mandates them.**\n\nThe migration still creates only `theme` and `language`. If the spec requires timestamps for auditing/automatic management, they should be included here to avoid schema mismatch.  \n \n\n<details>\n<summary>🧩 Example adjustment (align types/defaults per backend)</summary>\n\n```diff\n CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS settings (\n     tenant_id UUID NOT NULL,\n     user_id UUID NOT NULL,\n     theme VARCHAR(255),\n     language VARCHAR(255),\n+    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),\n+    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL DEFAULT NOW(),\n     PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, user_id)\n );\n```\n\n```diff\n CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS settings (\n     tenant_id VARCHAR(36) NOT NULL,\n     user_id VARCHAR(36) NOT NULL,\n     theme VARCHAR(255),\n     language VARCHAR(255),\n+    created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n+    updated_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL DEFAULT CURRENT_TIMESTAMP ON UPDATE CURRENT_TIMESTAMP,\n     PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, user_id)\n );\n```\n\n```diff\n CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS settings (\n     tenant_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n     user_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n     theme TEXT,\n     language TEXT,\n+    created_at TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),\n+    updated_at TEXT NOT NULL DEFAULT (CURRENT_TIMESTAMP),\n     PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, user_id)\n );\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/error.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`8-10`: **Trace ID extraction uses span ID instead of actual trace ID.**\n\nThe current implementation extracts `tracing::Span::current().id()` (a span identifier) but names it `trace_id`. Per W3C Trace Context, trace ID (128-bit) and span ID (64-bit) are distinct. This was flagged in a previous review but appears unaddressed.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/design.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`118-118`: **Minor: Incomplete sentence.**\n\nThe sentence fragment \"Can be added later if needed via event sourcing or audit log\" should have a subject for grammatical completeness. Consider: \"This can be added later if needed via event sourcing or an audit log.\"\n\nAlso applies to line 151: \"Can be extended later if needed.\"\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (4)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/__init__.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **Consider updating the docstring and directory name for clarity.**\n\nThe docstring references \"settings module\" but the actual module is named `simple-user-settings`. Per the PR comments, the module was renamed from \"settings\" to \"simple-user-settings\" to avoid future naming conflicts. Consider updating:\n- The docstring to `\"\"\"E2E tests for simple-user-settings module.\"\"\"`\n- Optionally renaming the directory from `settings` to `simple_user_settings` for consistency\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_update.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`210-247`: **Narrow the exception handling to avoid silent failures.**\n\nCatching `Exception` and swallowing it can hide schema/auth issues and make failures hard to diagnose. Consider narrowing the exception set and surfacing a warning while keeping the fallback.  \n \n\n<details>\n<summary>💡 Suggested adjustment</summary>\n\n```diff\n+import warnings\n ...\n-        except Exception:\n-            pass\n+        except (httpx.HTTPError, ValueError) as exc:\n+            warnings.warn(\n+                f\"OpenAPI schema unavailable; using fallback max length: {exc}\"\n+            )\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/config.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`3-18`: **Consider centralizing the default constant to avoid drift.**\n\nThere’s another hard-coded `100` default in `modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service.rs`. A shared const would prevent divergence.  \n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Example refactor</summary>\n\n```diff\n+pub const DEFAULT_MAX_FIELD_LENGTH: usize = 100;\n ...\n fn default_max_field_length() -> usize {\n-    100\n+    DEFAULT_MAX_FIELD_LENGTH\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_patch.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`210-212`: **Consider being explicit about expected `language` value.**\n\nThe assertion `settings.get(\"language\") is None or isinstance(settings.get(\"language\"), str)` is permissive. Per the service implementation (lines 44-49 in `service.rs`), when PATCH creates settings with only `theme`, the `language` field should be `None`. A stricter assertion would better document the expected behavior:\n\n```python\nassert settings[\"language\"] is None  # Not provided in PATCH, so should be None\n```\n\nThis was discussed in a prior review. If the permissive check is intentional (e.g., to accommodate future default values), consider adding a comment explaining why.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3677979934","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3677979934"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-19T12:55:51Z","commit_id":"69daedd42a585309f3b1e9ab4f752b703577f4b6"},{"id":3678053160,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7bOqMo","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 6**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn\n`@modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/specs/user-settings/spec.md`:\n- Around line 125-128: Update the \"Scenario: Validation error\" in the spec (the\nscenario header and its \"THEN\" clause) to use HTTP 422 Unprocessable Entity\ninstead of HTTP 400 Bad Request so it matches the error catalog; ensure the\nsentence \"the system returns HTTP 400 Bad Request with Problem Details\" is\nchanged to \"the system returns HTTP 422 Unprocessable Entity with Problem\nDetails\" and keep the field-level validation info line unchanged.\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/specs/user-settings/spec.md`:\n- Around line 113-125: Update the Data Model to specify validation rules for the\ntheme and language fields: declare `theme` as String optional with a max length\n(e.g., 50) and allowed enum values such as \"light\", \"dark\", \"auto\", and declare\n`language` as String optional with a max length (e.g., 10) and a format\nconstraint (ISO 639-1 with optional region, e.g., \"en\" or \"en-US\"); then add\nvalidation scenarios for Theme validation and String length validation that\ndescribe HTTP 400 responses and error messages identifying the invalid\nfield/value (referencing the `theme` and `language` fields and the \"Composite\nprimary key uniqueness\" scenario context).\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/lib.rs`:\n- Around line 3-6: The crate-level docs list of public API is missing the\nexported model SimpleUserSettingsUpdate; update the documentation comment in\nlib.rs to include `SimpleUserSettingsUpdate` alongside\n`SimpleUserSettingsClient`, `SimpleUserSettings`, `SimpleUserSettingsPatch`, and\n`SettingsError` so the SDK docs reflect all public exports.\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/Cargo.toml`:\n- Line 26: The Cargo.toml currently hard-codes sea-orm with the sqlx-sqlite\nfeature which overrides modkit-db's configurable backend; remove the direct\nsea-orm and sea-orm-migration entries (or make them optional) and instead mirror\nmodkit-db's feature-driven pattern: add optional dependencies for\nsea-orm/sea-orm-migration with default-features = false and create cargo\nfeatures (e.g., pg, mysql, sqlite) that enable the appropriate sqlx backend\nfeature on sea-orm and/or simply rely on modkit-db transitively; update the\nfeatures section to expose the same database selection flags and ensure any code\nreferences to sea-orm types remain behind these features or use modkit-db\nabstractions (reference symbols: the sea-orm dependency line, sea-orm-migration\nline, and modkit-db feature names).\n\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/domain/service.rs`:\n- Around line 84-91: The validation in validate_field currently uses value.len()\n(byte length) which incorrectly counts bytes for non-ASCII text; change the\ncheck to count user-visible characters by using value.chars().count() compared\nagainst self.config.max_field_length (or document that max_field_length is bytes\nif that is intended), and update the error message/logic in validate_field and\nany related tests to reflect character-based limits (references: validate_field,\nself.config.max_field_length, DomainError::validation).\n\nIn `@testing/e2e/modules/settings/README.md`:\n- Around line 7-24: Update the endpoint paths listed in the README so they match\nthe actual test endpoints: replace occurrences of /settings/v1/settings with\n/simple-user-settings/v1/settings for the entries describing\ntest_settings_get.py, test_settings_update.py, test_settings_patch.py and\ntest_settings_integration.py; ensure each bullet now references\n/simple-user-settings/v1/settings exactly as used in the tests.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/migrations/initial_001.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`16-44`: **Add required `created_at` / `updated_at` columns (per spec).**  \nIf the spec still mandates timestamps, they’re missing in all backend SQL strings. Please add the columns consistently (and defaults if required by the spec).  \n \n<details>\n<summary>🐛 Example fix (align types/defaults with your spec)</summary>\n\n```diff\n CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS settings (\n     tenant_id UUID NOT NULL,\n     user_id UUID NOT NULL,\n     theme VARCHAR(255),\n     language VARCHAR(255),\n+    created_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n+    updated_at TIMESTAMPTZ NOT NULL,\n     PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, user_id)\n );\n```\n\n```diff\n CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS settings (\n     tenant_id VARCHAR(36) NOT NULL,\n     user_id VARCHAR(36) NOT NULL,\n     theme VARCHAR(255),\n     language VARCHAR(255),\n+    created_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n+    updated_at TIMESTAMP NOT NULL,\n     PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, user_id)\n );\n```\n\n```diff\n CREATE TABLE IF NOT EXISTS settings (\n     tenant_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n     user_id TEXT NOT NULL,\n     theme TEXT,\n     language TEXT,\n+    created_at TEXT NOT NULL,\n+    updated_at TEXT NOT NULL,\n     PRIMARY KEY (tenant_id, user_id)\n );\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/specs/user-settings/spec.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`35-38`: **Clarify \"empty default values\" to match canonical JSON shape.**\n\nLine 37 uses the ambiguous phrase \"empty default values\" but should reference the specific canonical JSON shape defined in the \"Default settings for new users\" scenario (lines 18-23), where both `theme` and `language` fields are present with explicit `null` values.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n #### Scenario: First-time user retrieval\n - **WHEN** a new user (no existing settings record) sends `GET /simple-user-settings/v1/settings`\n-- **THEN** the system returns HTTP 200 with empty default values\n+- **THEN** the system returns HTTP 200 with the canonical JSON shape (both fields present with `null` values, as defined in \"Default settings for new users\")\n - **AND** no database insert occurs\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`43-47`: **Replace bold pseudo-headings with real headings (markdownlint MD036).**  \nThis matches the prior review feedback for the same lines.  \n \n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested edit</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Workflow**\n+#### Workflow\n...\n-**\"Change must have at least one delta\"**\n+#### \"Change must have at least one delta\"\n...\n-**\"Requirement must have at least one scenario\"**\n+#### \"Requirement must have at least one scenario\"\n...\n-**Silent scenario parsing failures**\n+#### Silent scenario parsing failures\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 293-302\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/design.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`125-126`: **Make the reasoning/decision sentences complete.**  \nSame issue as previously flagged; these are sentence fragments.  \n \n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested edit</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Reasoning:** Simplifies implementation, matches Go behavior. Can be added later if needed via event sourcing or audit log.\n+**Reasoning:** This simplifies implementation and matches Go behavior. These settings can be added later if needed via event sourcing or an audit log.\n...\n-   - **Decision:** No, keep it simple with two string fields. Can be extended later if needed.\n+   - **Decision:** No—keep it simple with two string fields. This can be extended later if needed.\n```\n</details>\n\n\nAlso applies to: 158-159\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_patch.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`210-212`: **Assert the expected default for `language` explicitly.**  \nLine 210–212 allows any string, which can hide incorrect defaults. If the contract is “language defaults to None when omitted,” assert that directly.  \n\n\n<details>\n<summary>✅ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        assert settings.get(\"language\") is None or isinstance(settings.get(\"language\"), str)\n+        assert settings[\"language\"] is None\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/infra/storage/sea_orm_repo.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`78-114`: **Prevent lost updates on concurrent PATCH requests.**  \n`upsert_patch` reads existing values, merges, then writes. Two concurrent PATCHes can overwrite each other’s fields (lost update). Consider wrapping read+merge+write in a DB transaction with row locking, or using a single DB-side update that coalesces `NULL` to existing values so the merge is atomic.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (5)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/dto_test.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`27-36`: **Test name is misleading and test logic is trivial.**\n\nThis test is named `test_update_request_to_dto` but doesn't test any conversion—it only verifies that struct fields match the values used during construction. This is tautological and doesn't exercise meaningful behavior.\n\nConsider either:\n1. Renaming to `test_update_request_construction` if the intent is just to verify the struct can be created.\n2. Actually testing a conversion (e.g., `From<UpdateSimpleUserSettingsRequest> for SimpleUserSettingsUpdate` if such a conversion exists).\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/specs/user-settings/spec.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`149-152`: **Clarify \"HTTP-specific derives\" or rephrase transport independence requirement.**\n\nLine 151 states SDK models \"do not include `serde` or other HTTP-specific derives.\" However, `serde` is not HTTP-specific—it's a general serialization framework used for JSON, CBOR, MessagePack, and many other formats.\n\nConsider either:\n1. Removing `serde` from the restriction if only avoiding HTTP-specific traits (like `axum` derives), OR\n2. Rephrasing to clarify that SDK models avoid *any* serialization dependencies to remain transport-agnostic\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>📝 Example rephasing</summary>\n\n```diff\n #### Scenario: SDK models have no transport dependencies\n - **WHEN** SDK models are defined\n-- **THEN** they do not include `serde` or other HTTP-specific derives\n+- **THEN** they do not include serialization frameworks (serde) or transport-specific derives (HTTP, gRPC)\n - **AND** they are plain Rust structs suitable for any transport (gRPC, local, HTTP)\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/proposal.md (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-30`: **Confirm OpenSpec archive workflow completed.**  \nSince this is now archived, please ensure any capability/spec updates were reflected in `specs/` and `openspec validate --strict` was run after archiving. Based on learnings, please verify.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_update.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`210-227`: **Narrow exception handling and reuse the helper for max length.**  \nLine 218–227 currently swallows all exceptions and duplicates logic. Consider using `_get_settings_max_field_length` and catching only expected `httpx`/parsing errors to avoid masking real failures.  \n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed refactor</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        max_field_length = 10000\n-        try:\n-            response = await client.get(\n-                f\"{base_url}/openapi.json\",\n-                headers=auth_headers,\n-            )\n-            response.raise_for_status()\n-            max_field_length = _extract_settings_theme_max_length(response.json()) or max_field_length\n-        except Exception:\n-            pass\n+        max_field_length = 10000\n+        try:\n+            max_field_length = await _get_settings_max_field_length(client, base_url)\n+        except (httpx.HTTPError, ValueError):\n+            # Fallback to a large sentinel if OpenAPI is unavailable\n+            pass\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_patch.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`223-226`: **Avoid hard-coded max length in validation test.**  \nLine 223–226 uses a fixed length (200), which becomes brittle if limits change. Consider deriving the limit from OpenAPI (as in update tests) or a shared helper.  \n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Example approach</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        patch_data = {\n-            \"language\": \"x\" * 200  # Way too long\n-        }\n+        max_field_length = 10000\n+        try:\n+            response = await client.get(f\"{base_url}/openapi.json\", headers=auth_headers)\n+            response.raise_for_status()\n+            max_field_length = (\n+                _extract_settings_theme_max_length(response.json()) or max_field_length\n+            )\n+        except (httpx.HTTPError, ValueError):\n+            pass\n+        patch_data = {\n+            \"language\": \"x\" * (max_field_length + 1)\n+        }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3678053160","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3678053160"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-19T13:15:27Z","commit_id":"0eb00141e0dbb376fab73e812618ecde0b320974"},{"id":3678553268,"node_id":"PRR_kwDOPaLyBs7bQkS0","user":{"login":"coderabbitai[bot]","id":136622811,"node_id":"BOT_kgDOCCSy2w","avatar_url":"https://avatars.githubusercontent.com/in/347564?v=4","gravatar_id":"","url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D","html_url":"https://github.com/apps/coderabbitai","followers_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/followers","following_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/following{/other_user}","gists_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/gists{/gist_id}","starred_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/starred{/owner}{/repo}","subscriptions_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/subscriptions","organizations_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/orgs","repos_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/repos","events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/events{/privacy}","received_events_url":"https://api.github.com/users/coderabbitai%5Bbot%5D/received_events","type":"Bot","user_view_type":"public","site_admin":false},"body":"**Actionable comments posted: 1**\n\n<details>\n<summary>🤖 Fix all issues with AI agents</summary>\n\n```\nIn `@modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/specs/user-settings/spec.md`:\n- Around line 130-133: The spec currently requires HTTP 400 for validation\nerrors while the error catalog/handlers return 422; pick one status (recommend\n400) and update all references accordingly: change the scenario in\nuser-settings/specs/user-settings/spec.md to the chosen status if needed, update\nthe validation error entry in the error catalog (the validation error\nconstant/entry used by your error registry), and update the handler/mapping that\nproduces responses (e.g., ValidationErrorHandler or mapValidationErrorToResponse\n/ handleValidationError) so it emits the same HTTP status and Problem Details\nshape; ensure tests and any examples are updated to the selected status so the\nspec, error catalog, and handlers are consistent.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Duplicate comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/AGENTS.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`43-47`: **Use proper headings instead of bold text.**\n\nMarkdownlint flags bold text used as headings at line 43 (`**Workflow**`). Switch to heading syntax for consistency.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Workflow**\n+#### Workflow\n```\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`291-303`: **Use proper headings in Troubleshooting section.**\n\nThe \"Common Errors\" subsection uses bold text instead of headings for error descriptions at lines 293, 297, and 301.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n ### Common Errors\n\n-**\"Change must have at least one delta\"**\n+#### \"Change must have at least one delta\"\n - Check `changes/[name]/specs/` exists with .md files\n - Verify files have operation prefixes (## ADDED Requirements)\n\n-**\"Requirement must have at least one scenario\"**\n+#### \"Requirement must have at least one scenario\"\n - Check scenarios use `#### Scenario:` format (4 hashtags)\n - Don't use bullet points or bold for scenario headers\n\n-**Silent scenario parsing failures**\n+#### Silent scenario parsing failures\n - Exact format required: `#### Scenario: Name`\n - Debug with: `openspec show [change] --json --deltas-only`\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/openspec/changes/archive/2026-01-15-add-settings-module-rust/design.md (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`125-125`: **Minor: Add subject to sentence fragment.**\n\nThe sentence \"Can be added later if needed via event sourcing or audit log.\" lacks a subject.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-**Reasoning:** Simplifies implementation, matches Go behavior. Can be added later if needed via event sourcing or audit log.\n+**Reasoning:** This simplifies implementation and matches Go behavior. History tracking can be added later if needed via event sourcing or an audit log.\n```\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`158-158`: **Minor: Add subject to sentence fragment.**\n\nSame issue: \"Can be extended later if needed.\" lacks a subject.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-   - **Decision:** No, keep it simple with two string fields. Can be extended later if needed.\n+   - **Decision:** No—keep it simple with two string fields. This can be extended later if needed.\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings-sdk/src/lib.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`3-6`: **Doc comment missing `SimpleUserSettingsUpdate`.**\n\nThe crate documentation lists model types but omits `SimpleUserSettingsUpdate`, which is exported at line 22.\n\n<details>\n<summary>✏️ Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n //! This crate provides the public API for the settings module:\n //! - `SimpleUserSettingsClient` trait for inter-module communication\n-//! - Model types (`SimpleUserSettings`, `SimpleUserSettingsPatch`)\n+//! - Model types (`SimpleUserSettings`, `SimpleUserSettingsPatch`, `SimpleUserSettingsUpdate`)\n //! - Error type (`SettingsError`)\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/error.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`8-10`: **The `trace_id` extraction still returns span ID, not trace ID.**\n\nPer W3C Trace Context, `trace_id` (128-bit) and `span_id` (64-bit) are distinct. The current code extracts `Span::current().id()` which is the span ID, not the trace ID. For proper distributed tracing correlation, extract the actual trace ID using OpenTelemetry:\n\n```rust\nuse tracing_opentelemetry::OpenTelemetrySpanExt;\n\nlet cx = tracing::Span::current().context();\nlet trace_id = cx.span()\n    .span_context()\n    .trace_id()\n    .to_string();\n```\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<details>\n<summary>🧹 Nitpick comments (6)</summary><blockquote>\n\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/config.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`17-18`: **Extract the default into a constant to avoid a magic number.**\n\nThis makes the default reusable for docs/tests and keeps intent explicit.\n\n\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Proposed refactor</summary>\n\n```diff\n+const DEFAULT_MAX_FIELD_LENGTH: usize = 100;\n+\n fn default_max_field_length() -> usize {\n-    100\n+    DEFAULT_MAX_FIELD_LENGTH\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/__init__.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`1-1`: **Consider renaming the E2E package to match `simple-user-settings`.**\n\nThis package and docstring still say “settings,” but the module is now `simple-user-settings`. To avoid confusion and future name collisions if a real “settings” module appears, consider renaming the folder/package and updating the docstring accordingly.\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_update.py (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`218-227`: **Consider narrowing the exception catch for better diagnostics.**\n\nThe broad `except Exception: pass` silently swallows all errors when fetching OpenAPI spec. While the 10000 fallback is a safe default, narrowing the exception types would help identify unexpected failures during test runs:\n\n<details>\n<summary>♻️ Optional improvement</summary>\n\n```diff\n         try:\n             response = await client.get(\n                 f\"{base_url}/openapi.json\",\n                 headers=auth_headers,\n             )\n             response.raise_for_status()\n             max_field_length = _extract_settings_theme_max_length(response.json()) or max_field_length\n-        except Exception:\n-            pass\n+        except (httpx.HTTPError, KeyError, TypeError):\n+            pass  # Fall back to 10000 if OpenAPI unavailable or schema differs\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>testing/e2e/modules/settings/test_settings_integration.py (2)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`20-21`: **Auth check logic is inconsistent with other tests.**\n\nThe condition `if get1_response.status_code in (401, 403) and not auth_headers` will only skip if *both* conditions are true. If `auth_headers` is provided but the token is invalid/expired (resulting in 401/403), the test will continue and fail at line 23. Other tests like `test_settings_idempotency` skip unconditionally on 401/403.\n\nConsider aligning with the pattern used in `test_settings_idempotency`:\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        if get1_response.status_code in (401, 403) and not auth_headers:\n+        if get1_response.status_code in (401, 403):\n             pytest.skip(\"Endpoint requires authentication\")\n```\n</details>\n\n---\n\n`147-148`: **Same inconsistent auth check pattern.**\n\nSame issue as in `test_settings_full_workflow` - the `and not auth_headers` condition makes the skip unreliable when auth headers are provided but invalid.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Suggested fix</summary>\n\n```diff\n-        if post_response.status_code in (401, 403) and not auth_headers:\n+        if post_response.status_code in (401, 403):\n             pytest.skip(\"Endpoint requires authentication\")\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n<details>\n<summary>modules/simple-user-settings/simple-user-settings/src/api/rest/dto.rs (1)</summary><blockquote>\n\n`35-51`: **Consider removing redundant `#[serde(default)]` on `Option` fields.**\n\nThe `#[serde(default)]` attribute is redundant for `Option<T>` fields since they already deserialize to `None` when absent. This is a minor nit and the current code works correctly.\n\n<details>\n<summary>Optional simplification</summary>\n\n```diff\n #[derive(Debug, Deserialize, ToSchema)]\n #[serde(rename_all = \"snake_case\")]\n pub struct PatchSimpleUserSettingsRequest {\n-    #[serde(default)]\n     pub theme: Option<String>,\n-    #[serde(default)]\n     pub language: Option<String>,\n }\n```\n</details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n</blockquote></details>\n\n<!-- This is an auto-generated comment by CodeRabbit for review status -->","state":"COMMENTED","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3678553268","pull_request_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223","author_association":"CONTRIBUTOR","_links":{"html":{"href":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/223#pullrequestreview-3678553268"},"pull_request":{"href":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/223"}},"submitted_at":"2026-01-19T15:13:41Z","commit_id":"f154644beca8697855955fa21f5b6fc95b5eb0bd"}]},{"source_pr_number":222,"source_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/222","source_state":"closed","source_author":"striped-zebra-dev","target_issue_number":null,"title":"[PR #222] Deprecate old SecurityCtx","status":"exported","mirrored_at":null,"comments_status":"none","comments_mirrored":0,"pr_head_ref":"revamp_security_context","pr_head_sha":"6a82ed87367fe303daf211ed7ae46f8dc301427e","pr_base_ref":"main","pr_head_owner":"striped-zebra-dev","pr_from_fork":1,"source_data":{"url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pulls/222","id":3159649159,"node_id":"PR_kwDOPaLyBs68VGuH","html_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/222","diff_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/222.diff","patch_url":"https://github.com/cyberfabric/cyberware-rust/pull/222.patch","issue_url":"https://api.github.com/repos/cyberfabric/cyberware-